#PROFILE UEFI
#TARGET X86_64
#RUNTIME NONE
#CALLCONV UEFI

' RFC-0044 Phase 3: the real fixture and QEMU proof combining Phase 1's real multi-handle BlockIo
' enumeration, Phase 2's real single-disk GPT ESP+ArcFS partition layout, RFC-0043 Phase V's real
' ArcFS.FormatVolume/ArcFS.ActivateSystemVolume boot-policy pattern, and render-and-halt.abas's own
' real GOP render + pixel-readback self-verification (RFC-0006/WP-030) -- all in ONE boot, on ONE
' real GPT-partitioned disk, not the two-separate-disk topology every prior ArcFS-on-real-hardware
' proof in this project used.
'
' A real, honest finding from Phase 3 development (not silently patched over, per RFC-0044 Section
' 7.2's own mandate): a single GPT-partitioned disk under this QEMU/OVMF combination yields FOUR
' real BlockIo handles, not two -- the whole disk itself, one additional handle this fixture does
' not explain, then the ESP, then the ArcFS partition, in that real discovery order. RFC-0044
' Section 6's own literal "SelectDiscovered(1)" (a hardcoded index) does NOT correspond to the
' ArcFS partition in this real topology. This fixture instead identifies the ArcFS partition by its
' own known, real geometry (SectorCount = 16384, build-arcology-gpt-image.py's ARCFS_SECTORS) --
' the SAME robust-to-discovery-order technique Phase 1's own multi-blockio-discovery.abas fixture
' already proved, reused here rather than trusting a specific index.
'
' Frozen copies of stdlib/uefi_block_device_policy.abas, stdlib/block_device_policy.abas, and
' stdlib/arcfs_policy.abas are inlined below, matching this project's established no-include-system
' fixture convention (same recipe RFC-0043 Phase V's aps-arcfs-sysvol-writer.abas/
' aps-arcfs-sysvol-reader.abas already use).

' UEFI-backed BlockDevice Provider (RFC-0038 Section 6.1/6.2), backed by a REAL discovered
' EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL device -- the hardware-side follow-on to RFC-0038 Section 17.5's stop
' condition, closed at the compiler-binding level in
' .agents/reports/aps-blockio-discovery-binding.md, and proven against a real attached QEMU disk
' in .agents/reports/aps-blockio-disk-provider.md.


' --- Documented deviations, stated up front, same spirit as block_device_policy.abas's own
' header note. ---
'
' 1. Same "concrete functions instead of an abstract BlockDevice interface value" deviation
'    RAMDisk.* already documents -- this backend has no runtime-polymorphic dispatch at all.
'
' 2. A SECOND, NEW deviation this provider needed that RAMDisk.* did not: the discovered
'    UEFI.BlockIoProtocol value itself cannot be persisted across calls as a still-typed value the
'    way mediaId/blockSize/sectorCount can. What CAN be persisted -- and what genuinely works,
'    confirmed directly against real generated x86-64 before relying on it -- is the raw protocol
'    pointer as a plain U64, RELOADED and RE-DECLARED with an explicit `AS UEFI.BlockIoProtocol`
'    type annotation in each function that needs to call a real protocol method. This works
'    because this compiler's field-chain resolution (src/compiler/fission.cpp) keys off a
'    variable's DECLARED type (tracked per-function in a name->type map populated by every typed
'    LET, not only function parameters), not off whether the variable literally is a parameter --
'    an is_method call on ANY local variable carrying an explicit UEFI.* type annotation resolves
'    through the same generic CallExternal path a genuine parameter would. Confirmed in generated
'    x86-64: `call [r11+0x18]`, ReadBlocks's own registered offset, appears identically whether
'    blockIo arrives as a parameter or is reloaded from memory and re-annotated. (An earlier,
'    narrower reading of this constraint -- assuming only literal parameters work -- is corrected
'    here; not a defect in the earlier binding work, just an incomplete first read of the
'    resolution code before this provider needed the fuller picture.)
'
' 3. `UefiBlockDevice.Discover` requires the discovered media's own BlockSize to be exactly 512.
'    UEFI.BLOCKIO.MediaFlags only exposes the packed RemovableMedia/MediaPresent/LogicalPartition/
'    ReadOnly word (uefi-bindings.md's own documented scope reduction: no single-byte
'    displacement-addressed encoder primitive exists, so a whole packed word is read instead of
'    one bit) -- this provider extracts the bits it needs from that word via ordinary SHR/AND.
'
' 4. UEFI.BLOCKIO.DISCOVER (used by UefiBlockDevice.Discover above) is a single LocateProtocol
'    call (matching UEFI.GOP.DISCOVER's own documented single-handle scope): it returns whichever
'    EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL handle firmware hands back first, not necessarily any particular disk a
'    caller has in mind. RFC-0044 closes this for callers that need a SPECIFIC device among
'    several: UefiBlockDevice.DiscoverAll/SelectDiscovered below enumerate every real, distinct
'    EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL handle (via the real EFI_BOOT_SERVICES.LocateHandle/HandleProtocol
'    bindings, not LocateProtocol's single-handle contract) and let a caller select one by index.
'    Selection is deliberately INDEX-based, not partition-GUID-aware (RFC-0044's own Non-Goals) --
'    a caller wanting "the ESP" or "the second GPT partition" specifically must still correlate
'    that itself (e.g. by probing each discovered index's own geometry/content), matching this
'    backend's consistent "no heap, no dynamic dispatch, callers do their own correlation" shape.

' RFC-0044 Section 4.3: real multi-handle enumeration state, a SEPARATE fixed scratch region from
' the single-device state above -- deliberately independent so DiscoverAll/SelectDiscovered can
' coexist with plain UefiBlockDevice.Discover callers without state collision. Layout: discovered
' count (U32, +0), then up to UefiBlockDeviceMultiMaxHandles() entries of 32 bytes each starting
' at +8 (blockIo pointer U64 +0, mediaId U32 +8, sectorCount U64 +16, writable flag U32 +24 --
' the SAME per-device field shape UefiBlockDevice.Discover's own single-device state already
' uses), followed by scratch space for the GUID buffer (+512) and the raw EFI_HANDLE array
' LocateHandle itself writes into (+528) -- all safely within one 4096-byte page.
FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiStateAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(37879808))   ' 0x2440000, clear of the 0x2400000 single-device page
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN UefiBlockDeviceMultiStateAddress()
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiEntryAddress(index AS U64) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiStateAddress(), 8 + index * 32)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiScratchGuidAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiStateAddress(), 512)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiScratchHandlesAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiStateAddress(), 528)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiScratchInterfaceAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiStateAddress(), 768)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceMultiMaxHandles() AS U64
    RETURN 8
END FUNCTION

' Real multi-handle EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL enumeration (RFC-0044 Section 4.3), closing RFC-0038
' Section 17.5's own "does not attempt multi-handle enumeration" scope reduction at the stdlib
' level -- via EFI_BOOT_SERVICES.LocateHandle(ByProtocol, EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL_GUID) followed by
' HandleProtocol on each returned real EFI_HANDLE, not LocateProtocol's single-handle contract.
' Returns the real count of genuinely distinct, present, 512-byte-sector BlockIo devices found (0
' on failure or none matching) -- each one's own real MediaId/SectorCount/writable flag is
' recorded via the SAME per-device fields UefiBlockDevice.Discover already records for the
' single-handle case. A device that HandleProtocol fails to resolve, or whose media is not
' present, or whose BlockSize is not exactly 512, is silently skipped (not recorded, not counted)
' -- matching UefiBlockDevice.Discover's own fail-closed-per-device precedent (scope reduction #3
' above) rather than surfacing a partial/degraded entry.
FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.DiscoverAll(systemTable AS UEFI.SystemTable) AS U64
    LET guidBuf AS MMIOPTR = UefiBlockDeviceMultiScratchGuidAddress()
    MEMORY.Write64(guidBuf, 1284199179690597153)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(guidBuf, 8), 4283602510276475278)

    LET maxHandles AS U64 = UefiBlockDeviceMultiMaxHandles()
    LET bufferSize AS U64 = maxHandles * 8
    LET bufferSizeAddr AS PTR = ADDRESS.Local(bufferSize)
    LET handleBuffer AS MMIOPTR = UefiBlockDeviceMultiScratchHandlesAddress()

    LET status AS U64 = systemTable.BootServices.LocateHandle(2, guidBuf, 0, bufferSizeAddr, handleBuffer)
    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceMultiCountAddress(), 0)
    IF status <> 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET foundCount AS U64 = bufferSize \ 8
    IF foundCount > maxHandles THEN
        foundCount = maxHandles
    END IF

    LET recorded AS U64 = 0
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < foundCount
        LET handleEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(handleBuffer, i * 8)
        LET handle AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(handleEntry)
        LET ifaceOutAddr AS MMIOPTR = UefiBlockDeviceMultiScratchInterfaceAddress()
        LET hpStatus AS U64 = systemTable.BootServices.HandleProtocol(handle, guidBuf, ifaceOutAddr)
        IF hpStatus = 0 THEN
            LET blockIo AS UEFI.BlockIoProtocol = MEMORY.Read64(ifaceOutAddr)
            LET mediaFlags AS U32 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.MediaFlags(blockIo)
            LET mediaPresent AS U32 = (mediaFlags \ 256) AND 255
            IF mediaPresent <> 0 THEN
                LET blockSize AS U32 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.BlockSize(blockIo)
                IF blockSize = 512 THEN
                    LET readOnly AS U32 = (mediaFlags \ 16777216) AND 255
                    LET lastBlock AS U64 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.LastBlock(blockIo)
                    LET mediaId AS U32 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.MediaId(blockIo)
                    LET entryAddr AS MMIOPTR = UefiBlockDeviceMultiEntryAddress(recorded)
                    MEMORY.Write64(entryAddr, blockIo)
                    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 8), mediaId)
                    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 16), lastBlock + 1)
                    IF readOnly = 0 THEN
                        MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 24), 1)
                    ELSE
                        MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 24), 0)
                    END IF
                    recorded = recorded + 1
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceMultiCountAddress(), recorded)
    RETURN recorded
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.DiscoveredCount() AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read32(UefiBlockDeviceMultiCountAddress())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.DiscoveredMediaId(index AS U64) AS U32
    RETURN MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiEntryAddress(index), 8))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.DiscoveredSectorCount(index AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiEntryAddress(index), 16))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.DiscoveredIsWritable(index AS U64) AS BOOL
    RETURN MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceMultiEntryAddress(index), 24))
END FUNCTION

' Selects one of the devices DiscoverAll() found as THE active device for every existing
' UefiBlockDevice.* call (ReadSectors/WriteSectors/SectorCount/IsWritable/etc.) -- copies that
' entry's own recorded fields into the SAME single-device state UefiBlockDevice.Discover already
' populates, so no existing function needed any change at all to support routing to a
' specifically-selected device among several. Returns FALSE (no state change) for an out-of-range
' index, matching this file's consistent fail-closed contract.
FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.SelectDiscovered(index AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET count AS U64 = UefiBlockDevice.DiscoveredCount()
    IF index >= count THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET entryAddr AS MMIOPTR = UefiBlockDeviceMultiEntryAddress(index)
    LET blockIo AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(entryAddr)
    LET mediaId AS U32 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 8))
    LET sectorCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 16))
    LET writable AS U32 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(entryAddr, 24))

    MEMORY.Write64(UefiBlockDeviceStatePointerAddress(), blockIo)
    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateMediaIdAddress(), mediaId)
    MEMORY.Write64(UefiBlockDeviceStateSectorCountAddress(), sectorCount)
    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateWritableAddress(), writable)
    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateDiscoveredAddress(), 1)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceStateAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(37748736))   ' 0x2400000, this provider's own scratch state
END FUNCTION

' State layout, all within one page: blockIo pointer (U64, +0), mediaId (U32, +8), sectorCount
' (U64, +16), writable flag (U32, +24), discovered flag (U32, +28).
FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceStatePointerAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN UefiBlockDeviceStateAddress()
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceStateMediaIdAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceStateAddress(), 8)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceStateSectorCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceStateAddress(), 16)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceStateWritableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceStateAddress(), 24)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDeviceStateDiscoveredAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(UefiBlockDeviceStateAddress(), 28)
END FUNCTION

' Discovers a real EFI_BLOCK_IO_PROTOCOL device and records its identity/geometry into fixed
' scratch state for every later UefiBlockDevice.* call to use. Requires the discovered media to be
' present and to use exactly 512-byte sectors (scope reduction #3 above) -- returns FALSE rather
' than a partially-usable provider otherwise, matching RFC-0038 Requirement 6.1's own fail-closed
' spirit for RAMDisk.
FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.Discover(systemTable AS UEFI.SystemTable) AS BOOL
    LET blockIo AS UEFI.BlockIoProtocol = UEFI.BLOCKIO.Discover(systemTable)
    IF blockIo = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET mediaFlags AS U32 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.MediaFlags(blockIo)
    LET mediaPresent AS U32 = (mediaFlags \ 256) AND 255
    LET readOnly AS U32 = (mediaFlags \ 16777216) AND 255
    IF mediaPresent = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET blockSize AS U32 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.BlockSize(blockIo)
    IF blockSize <> 512 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET lastBlock AS U64 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.LastBlock(blockIo)
    LET mediaId AS U32 = UEFI.BLOCKIO.MediaId(blockIo)

    MEMORY.Write64(UefiBlockDeviceStatePointerAddress(), blockIo)
    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateMediaIdAddress(), mediaId)
    MEMORY.Write64(UefiBlockDeviceStateSectorCountAddress(), lastBlock + 1)
    IF readOnly = 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateWritableAddress(), 1)
    ELSE
        MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateWritableAddress(), 0)
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write32(UefiBlockDeviceStateDiscoveredAddress(), 1)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.IsDiscovered() AS BOOL
    RETURN MEMORY.Read32(UefiBlockDeviceStateDiscoveredAddress())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.SectorSize() AS U32
    RETURN 512
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.SectorCount() AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(UefiBlockDeviceStateSectorCountAddress())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.IsWritable() AS BOOL
    RETURN MEMORY.Read32(UefiBlockDeviceStateWritableAddress())
END FUNCTION

' Requirement 6.1: MUST reject (return FALSE, MUST NOT partially transfer) a request whose
' startSector + count exceeds SectorCount() -- checked BEFORE the real ReadBlocks call, matching
' RAMDisk.ReadSectors's own bounds-check-first shape.
FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.ReadSectors(startSector AS U64, count AS U32, destination AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET total AS U64 = UefiBlockDevice.SectorCount()
    LET countAsU64 AS U64 = count
    IF startSector + countAsU64 > total THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET blockIo AS UEFI.BlockIoProtocol = MEMORY.Read64(UefiBlockDeviceStatePointerAddress())
    LET mediaId AS U32 = MEMORY.Read32(UefiBlockDeviceStateMediaIdAddress())
    LET bufferSize AS U64 = countAsU64 * 512
    LET status AS U64 = blockIo.ReadBlocks(mediaId, startSector, bufferSize, destination)
    RETURN status = 0
END FUNCTION

' Requirement 6.1: WriteSectors on a device where IsWritable() is FALSE MUST return FALSE without
' touching the medium -- checked before ever calling the real WriteBlocks method, matching
' RAMDisk.WriteSectors's own contract, now genuinely reachable (a real EFI_BLOCK_IO_MEDIA can
' legitimately be read-only, unlike RAMDisk's always-writable fixed region).
FUNCTION UefiBlockDevice.WriteSectors(startSector AS U64, count AS U32, source AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    IF UefiBlockDevice.IsWritable() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET total AS U64 = UefiBlockDevice.SectorCount()
    LET countAsU64 AS U64 = count
    IF startSector + countAsU64 > total THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET blockIo AS UEFI.BlockIoProtocol = MEMORY.Read64(UefiBlockDeviceStatePointerAddress())
    LET mediaId AS U32 = MEMORY.Read32(UefiBlockDeviceStateMediaIdAddress())
    LET bufferSize AS U64 = countAsU64 * 512
    LET status AS U64 = blockIo.WriteBlocks(mediaId, startSector, bufferSize, source)
    RETURN status = 0
END FUNCTION

' APS RAM Disk Block Device Provider (RFC-0038: APS Block Storage and Filesystem Provider
' Substrate, Requirements 6.1/6.2). Backs the Filesystem Provider layer (fat32_policy.abas) with
' no real storage hardware driver needed -- the RAM disk itself is just a byte range in memory.


' --- A documented deviation, stated up front, in the same spirit as loop_policy.abas's own
' header note. ---
'
' RFC-0038 Section 6.1 specifies `RAMDisk.Create(sizeBytes AS U64) AS BlockDevice` -- a value of
' an abstract `BlockDevice` interface type, so calling code can hold a RAM disk, a future AHCI
' device, etc. behind one shape. This implementation exposes `RAMDisk.*` as concrete, directly-
' named functions instead (`RAMDisk.SectorSize()`, `RAMDisk.ReadSectors(...)`, ...), and
' `RAMDisk.Create` returns `BOOL`, not a `BlockDevice` value. Reason: this compiler's freestanding
' x86-64 backend has no runtime-polymorphic dispatch mechanism at all -- confirmed the same way
' RFC-0037's `Loop.RegisterHook` deviation was confirmed (AmirInstruction::Kind::AddressOf is
' unhandled by generate_x86_64_function; CallValue can only call a compile-time-known function
' name). There is only one RAM disk in this milestone -- the same "one instance, fixed scratch
' state" pattern every prior RFC in this chain has used (one timer, one loop, one exception
' table) -- so the concrete-function shape loses nothing real yet; it becomes a genuine gap only
' once a second, different BlockDevice implementation (a real AHCI/NVMe/virtio-blk driver, all
' explicitly Non-Goals/Future Extensions of this RFC) needs to be swapped in behind the same
' calling code, at which point this needs the same treatment RFC-0027's freestanding callables
' would need for Loop.RegisterHook.
'
' A second deviation, specific to RAMDisk.Create: RFC-0038's own text says the backing memory
' should be "AllocatePages-sourced." This implementation's RAMDisk.Create does not allocate
' memory at all -- it records the size of a *fixed, pre-populated* memory region (see
' RAMDiskBaseAddress below) whose bytes are already correct by the time this program starts,
' because the QEMU/OVMF test harness places them there before boot (scripts/run/
' run-uefi-hello-with-preload.sh, a `-device loader,file=...,addr=...` preload -- see that
' script's own header comment and .agents/reports/aps-block-storage.md for why). This sidesteps
' RFC-0038 Stop Condition #17.5's anticipated UEFI-Block-IO-binding gap entirely rather than
' hitting it: no UEFI protocol call populates this RAM disk in this implementation, at any point.
' A future increment that actually copies bytes from a live UEFI Block IO device into freshly
' AllocatePages'd memory would change this function's *body*, not its contract --
' RAMDisk.SectorSize/SectorCount/ReadSectors/WriteSectors/IsWritable are unaffected either way.

FUNCTION RAMDiskBaseAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(67108864))   ' 0x4000000, 64 MiB -- see header note
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RAMDiskStateAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(33751040))   ' 0x2030000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RAMDisk.Create(sizeBytes AS U64) AS BOOL
    MEMORY.Write64(RAMDiskStateAddress(), sizeBytes \ 512)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RAMDisk.SectorSize() AS U32
    RETURN 512
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RAMDisk.SectorCount() AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(RAMDiskStateAddress())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RAMDisk.IsWritable() AS BOOL
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Requirement 6.1: MUST reject (return FALSE, MUST NOT partially transfer) a request whose
' startSector + count exceeds SectorCount().
FUNCTION RAMDisk.ReadSectors(startSector AS U64, count AS U32, destination AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET total AS U64 = RAMDisk.SectorCount()
    LET countAsU64 AS U64 = count
    IF startSector + countAsU64 > total THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET byteOffset AS U64 = startSector * 512
    LET byteCount AS U64 = count * 512
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < byteCount
        LET value AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(RAMDiskBaseAddress(), byteOffset + i))
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(destination, i), value)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Requirement 6.1: WriteSectors on a device where IsWritable() is FALSE MUST return FALSE without
' touching the medium -- not reachable for the RAM disk itself (always writable), but implemented
' symmetrically with ReadSectors for contract completeness; unexercised by this RFC's own QEMU
' proof, which only reads a pre-populated, read-only-in-spirit test image.
FUNCTION RAMDisk.WriteSectors(startSector AS U64, count AS U32, source AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET total AS U64 = RAMDisk.SectorCount()
    LET countAsU64 AS U64 = count
    IF startSector + countAsU64 > total THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET byteOffset AS U64 = startSector * 512
    LET byteCount AS U64 = count * 512
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < byteCount
        LET value AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(source, i))
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(RAMDiskBaseAddress(), byteOffset + i), value)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ArcologyFS (ArcFS) Phase A: in-memory semantic model (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase A --
' "Implement: OIDs; directory namespace; files/streams; handle semantics; transactions;
' rename/delete behavior; capability checks; no persistent disk yet. Purpose: validate the object
' and API contract before disk-format complexity."). No on-disk format, no copy-on-write metadata,
' no checkpoints -- this is the object/namespace/handle CONTRACT proven against real memory and a
' real CPU before any of RFC-0039's later, much larger disk-format phases exist.


' --- Documented Phase A scope reductions, stated up front. ---
'
' 1. 64-bit Object IDs, not RFC-0039's 128-bit. A monotonic counter big enough that overflow is
'    not a practical concern (RFC-0039 Section 19 already treats even its own 128-bit space's
'    overflow as a non-concern at any sane rate) -- widening later, if ever needed, changes this
'    file's OID storage width, not the OID/namespace/handle *contract* Phase A exists to prove.
' 2. Fixed-capacity in-memory tables (64 objects, 128 namespace entries, 8 open handles), not a
'    growable structure or an on-disk B+tree -- the same "one instance / small fixed table"
'    pattern every prior RFC in this chain has used (RFC-0036's pending table, RFC-0037's hook
'    table, RFC-0038's mount table), extended here to a whole object graph. RFC-0039's own Phase A
'    description explicitly wants the *contract* proven before disk-format complexity; growable
'    storage is exactly that complexity, deferred to Phase C (the transactional writer).
' 3. Fixed 4096-byte file capacity (one data-pool page per file, 1:1 with its object-table row),
'    not extents or sparse files (RFC-0039 Sections 18-19) -- again, Phase A's job is the handle/
'    Read/Write *contract*, not real storage layout.
' 4. Paths are raw byte buffers with an explicit length, not STRING. Not a leftover workaround --
'    freestanding STRING equality is now fixed (see .agents/reports/
'    freestanding-string-equality.md) -- but STRING has no substring/indexing/length operations at
'    all on this backend (LEN/MID are rejected outright, cleanly, as undeclared functions, not
'    silently wrong), and colon-delimited path parsing fundamentally needs those. Byte buffers with
'    MEMORY.Read8 loops are the same, already-proven pattern RFC-0038 established for exactly this
'    reason.
' 5. No transactions. RFC-0039 Phase A's own list includes "transactions," but every mutation here
'    (CreateFile, Rename, Remove) is already a single, indivisible operation on fixed in-memory
'    tables -- there is no multi-step staging area to make atomic yet (Phase A explicitly has "no
'    persistent disk yet," which in this implementation's judgment means no copy-on-write staging
'    either). A no-op BeginTransaction/CommitTransaction pair was considered and rejected as
'    actively misleading -- it would imply composability across multiple calls that does not
'    exist. Left for Phase C, where a real staging concept has something to stage.
' 6. No capability/authority checks (RFC-0039 Section 26). RFC-0017 (Substrate Resource Model),
'    which this would integrate with, has no concrete freestanding callable surface anywhere in
'    this repository yet (confirmed by search during RFC-0038's own implementation) -- building
'    one specifically for this file would be separate, real scope creep (RFC-0017's own
'    implementation), the same judgment RFC-0038 already made about the same gap.
'
' Every object has a stable OID independent of its name (RFC-0039 Section 5.1's central design
' principle) -- Rename below updates only the namespace row referencing an object, never the
' object's own table row, so a handle opened before a rename/move keeps working, and the OID
' returned by ArcFS.Resolve after a move is provably identical to the one returned by CreateFile.

' --- Fixed scratch addresses (policy-owned; no AllocatePages needed -- these are ordinary
' identity-mapped low memory under the same CR3 cutover every freestanding fixture in this project
' already establishes, exactly like RFC-0036/0037/0038's own fixed-address tables). Deliberately
' clear of every address this project has already claimed (0x2000000..0x2035000, 0x4000000). ---

FUNCTION ArcFSNextOidAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(33816576))   ' 0x2040000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 10: 128-bit OID identity and allocation. The low-half counter above
' (ArcFSNextOidAddress) is unchanged; this is its high-half companion, plus a scratch slot
' recording the high half that went with the OID an ArcFSAllocateOid() call just returned (since
' that function's own return type stays a single U64 -- see ArcFSAllocateOid's own header note for
' why a companion getter, not a wider return type).
FUNCTION ArcFSNextOidHighAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(33988608))   ' 0x2093000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHighAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(33992704))   ' 0x2094000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7.1: the Allocation Bitmap's in-memory mirror -- one byte per logical block,
' indexed directly by sector number, same "one instance, fixed scratch state" pattern every table
' in this file already uses. Loaded fresh at mount time (ArcFSLoadBitmap), mutated in memory by
' ArcFSAllocateDataExtent/ArcFS.ReclaimGeneration, and durably written by ArcFS.PrepareCommit --
' exactly the same in-memory/on-disk split the Object and Namespace Trees already established.
' RFC-0043 Phase U: relocated to the dedicated post-Phase-Q scratch region (0x10000000+, see
' ArcFSObjectTableAddress's own header note) -- ArcFSMaxTrackedSectors() raised from 508 to 200152
' means this mirror's own real need (200152 bytes) no longer fits the single-page gap it used to
' occupy near the original policy-owned cluster.
FUNCTION ArcFSAllocationBitmapAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(287309824))   ' 0x11200000, 1 MiB block (needs 200152 bytes)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSBitmapGet(sector AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAllocationBitmapAddress(), sector))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSBitmapSet(sector AS U64, allocated AS U64) AS U64
    LET v AS U8 = allocated
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAllocationBitmapAddress(), sector), v)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 9: fixed scratch addresses for Object Tree node construction. A full 4096-byte
' node buffer (nodes are 8x the 512-byte sector scratch every other record uses); a sorted working
' copy of the active object rows being bulk-built into a tree this commit (up to ArcFSMaxObjects()
' entries, matching the in-memory table's own ceiling); and two alternating (key, nodeSector) pair
' arrays used to build each tree level from the level below it, bottom-up.
FUNCTION ArcFSNodeScratchAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34996224))   ' 0x2158000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11.2: relocated to the dedicated post-Phase-Q scratch region (0x10000000+, see
' ArcFSObjectTableAddress's own header note for the full region layout) -- ArcFSMaxObjects() raised
' from 64 to 2048 means this array's own real need (2048*40=80KB) no longer fits the single-page
' gap it used to share with ArcFSNodeScratchAddress.
FUNCTION ArcFSTreeSortedAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(271581184))   ' 0x10300000, 1 MiB block (needs 2048*40=80KB)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSTreeLevelAAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(272629760))   ' 0x10400000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSTreeLevelBAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(273678336))   ' 0x10500000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

' A mutable output counter shared across ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto's own recursive calls,
' the same "companion address" idiom ArcFSAllocateOid/ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh already
' established, needed here because this backend has no multi-value return.
FUNCTION ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35012608))   ' 0x215C000
END FUNCTION

' Namespace Tree's own gather/build/collect scratch -- CANNOT reuse the Object Tree's own
' ArcFSTreeSortedAddress/ArcFSTreeLevelAAddress/etc. even though the two trees never build or walk
' concurrently within one call (ArcFS.PrepareCommit finishes the Object Tree completely before
' touching the namespace one), because a namespace leaf entry is 56 bytes (vs. 40 for an object
' entry) and this format allows up to ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() (128) of them -- 128*56 = 7168 bytes,
' which does not fit the single-4096-byte-page gap the Object Tree's own sorted-array address
' leaves before ArcFSTreeLevelAAddress starts. Fresh, correctly-sized addresses instead of trying
' to cram into that gap (verified via the real decimal literals below, not the comments elsewhere
' in this cluster, several of which were found to be stale during this phase -- see this phase's
' own report). Node staging itself (ArcFSNodeScratchAddress) IS safely shared, since only one
' node is ever staged at a time regardless of which tree is being built or walked.
' RFC-0042 Section 11.2: relocated to the dedicated post-Phase-Q scratch region -- ArcFSMaxNamespace
' Rows() raised from 128 to 4096 means this array's own real need (4096*56=224KB) no longer fits the
' 2-page gap it used to occupy.
FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(274726912))   ' 0x10600000, 1 MiB block (needs 4096*56=224KB)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeLevelAAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(275775488))   ' 0x10700000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeLevelBAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(276824064))   ' 0x10800000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35082240))   ' 0x2175000
END FUNCTION

' Attribute Tree's own gather/build/collect scratch (RFC-0040 Section 12) -- same "cannot safely
' reuse another tree's own scratch region" reasoning as the Namespace Tree's own addresses above,
' since an attribute leaf entry (48 bytes: oidLow+oidHigh+attributeId+type+valueLow+valueHigh) is
' yet another distinct size from either the Object Tree's (40) or the Namespace Tree's (56).
' RFC-0040 Section 12.1's multi-attribute model widened this from 40*64=2560 bytes (one page) to
' 48*128=6144 bytes (two pages) -- moved forward from the original single-page gap before
' ArcFSDataPoolAddress (0x2200000, comfortably far ahead) rather than trying to cram into it.
' RFC-0042 Section 11.2: relocated to the dedicated post-Phase-Q scratch region -- ArcFSMaxAttributes()
' raised from 128 to 4096 means this array's own real need (4096*48=192KB) no longer fits the
' 2-page gap it used to occupy.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(277872640))   ' 0x10900000, 1 MiB block (needs 4096*48=192KB)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeLevelAAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(278921216))   ' 0x10A00000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeLevelBAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(279969792))   ' 0x10B00000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35143680))   ' 0x2184000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11.2: this is the FIRST structure (in file order) of a dedicated, generously
' spaced scratch region this phase introduces at 0x10000000+, well clear of both the original
' policy-owned cluster (0x2000000-0x2400000ish, still home to every structure this phase does NOT
' widen) and RAMDiskBaseAddress (0x4000000) -- every structure below whose real size scales with
' ArcFSMaxObjects()/ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()/ArcFSMaxAttributes()/ArcFSMaxFileChunks() lives here now,
' each in its own dedicated 1 MiB block (except the Data Pool, which needs its own much larger
' block) rather than threaded into the old cluster's now-inadequate gaps. Confirmed to fit real,
' empirically-verified backing RAM -- see .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-phase-q.md and
' .agents/reports/aps-qemu-ram-ceiling.md.
FUNCTION ArcFSObjectTableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(268435456))   ' 0x10000000, 1 MiB block (needs 2048*56=112KB)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(269484032))   ' 0x10100000, 1 MiB block (needs 4096*80=320KB)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(270532608))   ' 0x10200000, 1 MiB block (needs 4096*64=256KB)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 11: widened for Phase K -- each object's own data-pool slot holds
' ArcFSMaxFileChunks() chunks, not one. RFC-0042 Section 11: the Data Pool's real size is the
' PRODUCT ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() * ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() -- with this phase's
' own 2048/8/4096 values, 2048*8*4096 = 64 MiB -- given its own dedicated 128 MiB block (real
' headroom for future growth without yet another relocation), the single largest structure this
' file defines by a wide margin. See ArcFSMaxFileChunks()'s own header note for why this phase did
' NOT raise object count and file-chunk count by the same multiplier (their product, not their sum,
' drives this address's own real footprint).
FUNCTION ArcFSDataPoolAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(301989888))   ' 0x12000000, 128 MiB block (needs 2048*8*4096=64MB)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 11.3 (sparse files): one byte per (object row, chunk index), tracking which
' chunks have genuinely been written this session -- an untouched chunk is a hole: no real extent
' is ever allocated for it, and it reads as zero.
FUNCTION ArcFSChunkTouchedAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(286261248))   ' 0x11100000, 1 MiB block (needs 2048*8=16KB)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: gather/build scratch for the Extent Tree -- a real B+tree replacing the
' fixed 512-byte 4-slot extent LIST as the on-disk shape for chunk extents (see this file's own
' report, .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-extent-tree-reflink.md, for the full design). Same "cannot
' safely reuse another tree's own scratch region" reasoning the Namespace/Attribute Trees already
' documented -- an Extent Tree leaf entry (32 bytes: dataSlot+chunkIndex+extentSector+reserved) is
' yet another distinct width. Sized for up to ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() entries --
' every touched chunk across every currently active data-pool slot.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(281018368))   ' 0x10C00000, 1 MiB block (needs 2048*8*32=512KB)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeLevelAAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(282066944))   ' 0x10D00000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeLevelBAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(283115520))   ' 0x10E00000, 1 MiB block
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(36847616))   ' 0x2324000
END FUNCTION

' A small temp list of the UNIQUE data-pool slots currently in active use (up to ArcFSMaxObjects()
' U64 values) -- the dedup step that makes on-disk reflink sharing real: two object rows
' still sharing an undiverged data-pool slot contribute the SAME dataSlot value once, not twice, so
' ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries below writes each shared chunk's real extent exactly once.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeUniqueSlotsAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(284164096))   ' 0x10F00000, 1 MiB block (needs 2048*8=16KB)
END FUNCTION

' Mount-time-only, reset at the start of every ArcFSLoadObjects() call: direct-indexed by
' COMMITTED dataSlot value (0..ArcFSMaxObjects()-1, the same bound ArcFSChunkTouchedGet already
' indexes by), holding the row index that has claimed the real LIVE slot for that committed
' identity this mount, or ArcFSMaxObjects() (the standard "not found" sentinel this file already
' uses for row/slot lookups) if no row has claimed it yet. This is what makes reflink sharing
' survive a remount: the FIRST row loaded for a given committedDataSlot becomes that live slot's
' real owner (and actually reads its chunks off disk); every LATER row sharing the same
' committedDataSlot points at the SAME owner instead of getting its own private copy.
' RFC-0042 Section 11.2: relocated to the dedicated post-Phase-Q scratch region -- real need grew
' from 512 to 2048*8=16384 bytes (ArcFSMaxObjects() raised 32x), no longer fitting its old gap.
FUNCTION ArcFSMountSlotOwnerAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(285212672))   ' 0x11000000, 1 MiB block (needs 2048*8=16KB)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11: raised to 64 handles (see ArcFSMaxHandles()'s own header note); real need
' grew from 128 to 64*16=1024 bytes, still comfortably inside this address's own existing gap
' before ArcFSNextOidHighAddress (0x2093000) -- confirmed directly (12288 bytes of headroom), so
' this stays in the original policy-owned cluster rather than moving.
FUNCTION ArcFSHandleTableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34144256))   ' 0x2090000
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' Phase D: mutation completeness (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase D -- "create/write/resize;
' rename/move; delete; sparse files; reflinks; typed attributes").
' ==========================================================================================
'
' Rename/move/delete already exist (Phase A's ArcFS.Rename/ArcFS.Remove) and already survive a
' commit/remount cycle unchanged -- they mutate the same in-memory tables Phase C's commit
' protocol already durably writes, so Phase D adds no new API for them; it adds a fixture that
' actually PROVES the round trip, closing a real gap (Phase C's own proof fixture never renamed or
' deleted anything against the real on-disk format).
'
' What IS new: ArcFS.Resize (grow/shrink within the existing fixed file capacity) and ArcFS.Reflink
' (RFC-0039 Section 22), both of which need a real answer to "what happens when two OIDs' data
' currently lives in the same physical page and one of them writes." Phase A/B/C's own "dataSlot =
' object-table row index" convention has no room for two rows to point at the same slot, so Phase D
' decouples data-pool slot allocation from object-row allocation entirely: a slot allocator
' (ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress, a bump counter like Phase C's own sector allocator) plus a per-slot
' reference-count table (ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress) let Reflink make a second row point at an
' existing slot without copying anything, and let ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot -- the copy-on-write gate
' every mutating data-pool access now passes through -- give a writer its own private copy the
' MOMENT it actually needs one, never before.
'
' Also new: a minimal, documented, IN-MEMORY-ONLY typed-attribute slot per object
' (ArcFSAttributeTableAddress) -- see that section's own header for why persistence is deferred.
'
' Explicitly OUT of Phase D's scope, stated up front like every prior phase's own header note:
'
' 1. Sparse files (RFC-0039 Section 19). Every FILE object still gets exactly one mandatory fixed
'    4096-byte extent (Phase A's own scope reduction #3) -- there is no "hole" concept possible
'    without a real multi-extent-per-file model, which is a bigger undertaking than this phase's
'    budget. Deferred to whichever future phase replaces the fixed-single-extent design.
' 2. On-disk reflink sharing. ArcFS.CommitImage() still writes each active object's CURRENT bytes
'    to its OWN independent on-disk extent (see ArcFS.PrepareCommit's own updated comment below) --
'    two objects that are still sharing a data-pool slot in memory at commit time simply get two
'    identical extents on disk, not one shared one. The in-memory copy-on-write SEMANTIC (Section
'    22's "a reflink MUST NOT cause later writes to one file to modify the visible data of the
'    other") is fully proven; the on-disk SPACE SAVING a real reflink implies is not -- that needs
'    a reference-counted allocation tree, itself already named as Phase E's job in Phase C's own
'    report.
' 3. Attribute persistence. ArcFS.SetAttribute's value does not survive ArcFS.MountImage() -- see
'    that function's own header for the full reasoning (a format change here would have required
'    touching Phase B's and Phase C's already-proven, committed checkpoint format).
'
' --- Fixed scratch addresses for the above (policy-owned, same discipline as every earlier
' section): a data-pool slot allocator, decoupled from object-row allocation, its per-slot
' reference-count table, and a parallel Attribute Table (one row per object-table row index,
' matching that table's own indexing). ---

FUNCTION ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34107392))   ' 0x2087000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11.2: real need grew from 512 to 2048*8=16384 bytes (ArcFSMaxObjects() raised
' 32x) -- still fits comfortably in the gap this address already had before the next still-present
' address (ArcFSHandleTableAddress, 0x2090000, unmoved -- its own trivial growth to 64*16=1024
' bytes fits its own existing gap too), so this stays in the original policy-owned cluster rather
' than moving to the new region. Confirmed directly, not assumed: 0x2088000 to 0x2090000 is 32768
' bytes of headroom, comfortably more than the 16384 now needed.
FUNCTION ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34111488))   ' 0x2088000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11: raised from 64 to 2048 (32x), the largest single ceiling this backend has
' ever actually needed to hold real backing RAM for -- every prior increment's "small enough to
' exercise under QEMU" scope reduction was a genuine choice; this one is close to the real limit of
' what a flat, fixed-address, non-paged live table can responsibly hold at once, given the Data
' Pool's own size is the PRODUCT of this constant and ArcFSMaxFileChunks() (see that function's own
' header note for the full reasoning, and .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-phase-q.md for the complete
' capacity-target analysis, including why the RFC's own original 4096-object target was revised
' down after tracing the Data Pool's real footprint through concretely).
FUNCTION ArcFSMaxObjects() AS U64
    RETURN 2048
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 12.1: the Attribute Table's own row capacity, independent of ArcFSMaxObjects()
' now that a single object can carry more than one attribute (one row per (oid, attributeId) pair,
' not one row per object-table index). RFC-0042 Section 11: raised from 128 to 4096 (32x, matching
' ArcFSMaxObjects()'s own multiplier) -- this table's own footprint (4096*64=256KB) is trivial
' regardless, so it tracks the same scale-up the object/namespace tables get rather than lagging
' behind them.
FUNCTION ArcFSMaxAttributes() AS U64
    RETURN 4096
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11: raised from 128 to 4096 (32x, matching ArcFSMaxObjects()) -- 320KB table,
' trivial footprint.
FUNCTION ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() AS U64
    RETURN 4096
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 11: raised from 8 to 64 (8x) -- open-handle count was never going to be the
' actual bottleneck for "production scale," this just keeps it from being an artificially tighter
' ceiling than everything else now is.
FUNCTION ArcFSMaxHandles() AS U64
    RETURN 64
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSMaxNameLength() AS U64
    RETURN 32
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() AS U64
    RETURN 4096
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 11: how many fixed 4096-byte chunks one file may have. A real extent could span
' an arbitrary number of contiguous sectors in one entry; this implementation instead uses one
' fixed-size chunk per extent-list slot, trading some space efficiency for a much simpler design
' (no partial-extent length tracking, no extent merging) -- RFC-0039 Section 16.3's own
' "implementation-defined" allowance for extent representation covers this.
'
' RFC-0042 Section 11: raised from 4 to 8 (2x, 32 KiB max file size, up from 16 KiB) -- deliberately
' the SMALLEST multiplier of any capacity this phase touches, not an oversight. The Data Pool's own
' real size is ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() * ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() -- a PRODUCT of
' two constants this phase raises, not a sum -- and the RFC's own original target table (4096
' objects AND 256 chunks/1MB files simultaneously) was traced through concretely during this
' phase's own scoping and found to require a 4 GiB Data Pool, wildly outside any real RAM budget
' even after this same increment gave the QEMU test harness a much more generous 512 MiB (see
' .agents/reports/aps-qemu-ram-ceiling.md and .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-phase-q.md). Revised down
' honestly rather than shipped as originally drafted: object/namespace/attribute capacity (Table
' rows, cheap, additive) grew 32x; file size (Data Pool, expensive, multiplicative with object
' count) grew only 2x, keeping the Data Pool at a real, backed 2048*8*4096 = 64 MiB.
FUNCTION ArcFSMaxFileChunks() AS U64
    RETURN 8
END FUNCTION

' The real per-slot stride into the data pool now that a slot holds up to ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
' chunks, not one -- every data-pool address computation in this file goes through this, replacing
' the old `dataSlot * ArcFSFileCapacityBytes()` stride that Phase K's own extent-list functions
' already widened; HandleWrite/HandleRead/Resize/EnsurePrivateSlot below are brought in line with
' it as part of this same phase.
FUNCTION ArcFSSlotSizeBytes() AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
END FUNCTION

' Indexed by DATA-POOL SLOT, not object row -- the same key the data pool itself and the slot
' refcount table already use, deliberately, since ArcFS.Reflink makes two DIFFERENT rows share
' one dataSlot. Indexing by row instead would give a reflinked clone its own disconnected (and
' initially stale) touched state instead of correctly seeing the source's real one until the
' shared slot actually diverges -- caught while designing this, not left as a live bug.
FUNCTION ArcFSChunkTouchedGet(dataSlot AS U64, chunkIndex AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSChunkTouchedAddress(), dataSlot * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() + chunkIndex))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(dataSlot AS U64, chunkIndex AS U64, touched AS U64) AS U64
    LET v AS U8 = touched
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSChunkTouchedAddress(), dataSlot * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() + chunkIndex), v)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7.1 / RFC-0043 Phase U: the Allocation Bitmap's own fixed capacity --
' byte-per-block (0=free, 1=allocated), one bit of information packed into a whole byte for
' simplicity, matching this project's own repeated "verify correctness before density" preference.
' Originally 508 (one self-checksummed 512-byte sector: 508 payload bytes + a 4-byte CRC-32C
' checksum) -- Phase Q's own coupling finding (`.agents/reports/aps-arcfs-phase-q.md`) traced
' through concretely that this was the REAL binding ceiling on how much of the new 2048-object live
' capacity could ever be committed and remounted, not the object/namespace row counts themselves.
' 200152, not a rounder number: chosen as an EXACT multiple of 508 (200152 = 394 * 508) so the
' on-disk span (ArcFSBitmapSectorCount() below) never needs partial-last-sector padding logic --
' 200152 logical blocks is ~97.7 MB, comfortably past RFC-0042 Phase Q's own 64 MB Data Pool (the
' real ceiling on file CONTENT already established there) with real headroom left for tree/
' checkpoint/namespace metadata overhead on top. See RFC-0043 Section 7 for why this is not
' pre-committed to a much larger number: the goal is "past the point where this is the binding
' constraint," concretely traced, not "as large as possible."
FUNCTION ArcFSMaxTrackedSectors() AS U64
    RETURN 200152
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0043 Phase U: how many consecutive physical sectors the Allocation Bitmap's own on-disk image
' now spans -- each sector independently self-checksummed (508 payload bytes + a 4-byte CRC-32C
' checksum, the EXACT same per-sector shape the old single-sector bitmap always used), rather than
' one checksum covering the whole multi-sector span. Exact division (200152 \ 508 = 394, no
' remainder) by construction -- see ArcFSMaxTrackedSectors()'s own header note for why that
' constant was deliberately chosen as an exact multiple.
FUNCTION ArcFSBitmapSectorCount() AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSMaxTrackedSectors() \ 508
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 9: the Object Tree's own node occupancy threshold, deliberately small. Nodes
' are still the full RFC-0040 Section 9.1 mandated size (4096 bytes = 8 sectors, one physical node
' per data-extent-sized allocation) with real room for roughly 100 leaf entries -- this constant
' is not the node's physical capacity, it is how many entries this implementation chooses to pack
' into one before starting a new node, chosen small (4) specifically so a handful of real objects
' -- well under ArcFSMaxObjects()'s own 64-row ceiling -- provably produces a real multi-node,
' multi-level tree under QEMU, the same "small number you can actually exercise" testing
' discipline this project has used since Phase A's own small fixed tables. RFC-0039 Section 16's
' own "precise node split heuristics are implementation-defined" already permits this.
FUNCTION ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity() AS U64
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSObjectTreeNodeSizeBytes() AS U64
    RETURN 4096
END FUNCTION

' Namespace Tree's own leaf occupancy -- a DIFFERENT named constant from
' ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity() even though both happen to equal 4 right now, since a namespace
' leaf entry (56 bytes: parentOid+name+nameLength+childOid) is a genuinely different size from an
' object leaf entry (40 bytes) and the two could reasonably diverge in the future. Internal-node
' fanout, by contrast, IS shared with the Object Tree unchanged (see ArcFSBuildNamespaceTree's own
' header note) -- internal-node layout is generic across any tree in this format, so there is only
' one internal-node capacity in this file, not one per tree.
FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeNodeCapacity() AS U64
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

' Attribute Tree's own leaf occupancy (RFC-0040 Section 12), a distinct named constant for the
' same reason ArcFSNamespaceTreeNodeCapacity is -- a different entry width from either other tree,
' even though the value happens to match. Internal-node fanout is shared, generic infrastructure
' (ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode, ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()) exactly like the Namespace Tree's own.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeNodeCapacity() AS U64
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

' Extent Tree's own leaf occupancy (RFC-0040 Section 11.1) -- a distinct named constant for the
' same reason the Namespace/Attribute Trees' own are, since an Extent Tree leaf entry (32 bytes:
' dataSlot+chunkIndex+extentSector+reserved) is yet another distinct width. Internal-node fanout is
' shared, generic infrastructure (ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode, ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()) exactly
' like every other tree's own.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeNodeCapacity() AS U64
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

' --- Object Tree (RFC-0039 Section 16.1, scaled to a fixed in-memory table). Row layout, 56
' bytes: [oidLow U64][type U64: 0=free/unused, 1=FILE, 2=DIRECTORY][size U64][dataSlot U64]
' [oidHigh U64][reserved0 U64][reserved1 U64]. RFC-0040 Section 10: the row's own PREVIOUS final
' field, then-unused "reserved" padding, now durably carries the object's real OID high half -- no
' row-size change was needed for that, since the field 128-bit identity needed already existed.
' RFC-0042 Section 10.1: widened again, for real THIS time -- two fresh U64 fields reserved for a
' future per-object field (a generation stamp is one named candidate, RFC-0042 Section 4 Non-Goal
' 4), cleared at ArcFS.Initialize() time and left genuinely unpopulated by every row-constructing
' function (ArcFSCreateObject, ArcFS.Reflink, ArcFSPopulateObjectRow) -- a real reservation, not
' disguised scope creep (RFC-0042 Section 10.4). The ON-DISK Object Tree leaf entry stays exactly
' 40 bytes, UNCHANGED -- these two new fields live only in the LIVE row; there is nothing new to
' persist yet, so nothing about the on-disk B+tree format needs to grow to match. ---

FUNCTION ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row AS U64) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectTableAddress(), row * 56)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 10.2: "a 128-bit monotonic counter using the representation in 10.1" (two U64
' halves, compared/incremented pairwise -- this backend has no native 128-bit integer type,
' confirmed during Phase A). Still returns only the low half via its own U64 return type, exactly
' like every caller already expects (this function's callers, and every function downstream of
' them, are Phase A's original object/namespace/handle contract, entirely unchanged this
' increment -- see this file's own RFC-0040-Phase-H-follow-up header note for the full scope
' boundary). ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh() reads the high half that belongs to the OID JUST
' returned, for the one caller that needs it (ArcFSCreateObject, to durably store it).
FUNCTION ArcFSAllocateOid() AS U64
    LET oidLow AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNextOidAddress())
    LET oidHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNextOidHighAddress())
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHighAddress(), oidHigh)

    LET newLow AS U64 = oidLow + 1
    LET newHigh AS U64 = oidHigh
    ' Carry into the high half when the low half genuinely wraps around (newLow = 0 after
    ' incrementing the maximum U64 value) -- the same "increment the low half with carry into the
    ' high half" RFC-0040 Section 10.1 specifies. A wrap ALSO skips newLow past 0: this
    ' implementation has used a bare 0 as the "no object"/failure sentinel everywhere since Phase
    ' A (every ArcFS.Create*/ArcFSCreateObject caller already treats a 0 return as failure), so a
    ' low-half wrap handing out a real object identity of exactly 0 would be indistinguishable
    ' from "allocation failed" to every existing caller -- a real correctness gap, not a
    ' theoretical one, found while designing this phase's own carry-boundary test.
    IF newLow = 0 THEN
        newHigh = oidHigh + 1
        newLow = 1
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextOidAddress(), newLow)
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextOidHighAddress(), newHigh)
    RETURN oidLow
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh() AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHighAddress())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxObjects()
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
        LET storedType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
        IF storedType <> 0 THEN
            LET storedOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            IF storedOid = oid THEN
                found = row
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindFreeObjectRow() AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxObjects()
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
        LET storedType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
        IF storedType = 0 THEN
            IF found = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
                found = row
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

' Allocates a fresh, exclusive data-pool slot (bump allocator, 0..ArcFSMaxObjects()-1, decoupled
' from object-row allocation as of Phase D -- see that section's own header for why Reflink needs
' this decoupling). Returns ArcFSMaxObjects() (an invalid slot index) if the data pool is full.
' Marks the returned slot's reference count as 1 (a fresh slot always starts exclusively owned).
FUNCTION ArcFSAllocateDataSlot() AS U64
    LET nextSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress())
    IF nextSlot >= ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN ArcFSMaxObjects()
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress(), nextSlot + 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), nextSlot * 8), 1)
    RETURN nextSlot
END FUNCTION

' Internal: allocates a fresh OID, claims a free object-table row, and gives it its OWN exclusive
' data-pool slot. Returns the new OID, or 0 if either the object table or the data pool is full.
FUNCTION ArcFSCreateObject(entryType AS U64) AS U64
    LET freeRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindFreeObjectRow()
    IF freeRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateDataSlot()
    IF dataSlot = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET oid AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateOid()
    LET oidHigh AS U64 = ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh()
    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(freeRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(base, oid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8), entryType)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24), dataSlot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 32), oidHigh)
    RETURN oid
END FUNCTION

' --- Namespace Tree (RFC-0039 Section 16.2, scaled to a fixed in-memory table). Row layout, 80
' bytes: [parentOID U64][name 32 bytes, zero-padded][nameLength U64][childOID U64][active U64]
' [reserved0 U64][reserved1 U64]. Renaming/moving an object mutates only this table -- the Object
' Tree row for that OID, and therefore its OID, is never touched (RFC-0039 Section 5.1).
' RFC-0042 Section 10.2: widened from 64 to 80 bytes, the same "two reserved U64 fields, genuinely
' unpopulated, ON-DISK leaf entry unchanged" treatment as the Object row above -- see that row's
' own header note for the full reasoning. ---

FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row AS U64) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSNamespaceTableAddress(), row * 80)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSNameMatches(rowBase AS MMIOPTR, storedLength AS U64, candidate AS MMIOPTR, candidateLength AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF storedLength <> candidateLength THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < storedLength
        LET a AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(rowBase, 8 + i))
        LET b AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(candidate, i))
        IF a <> b THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindNamespaceRow(parentOID AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 56))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET storedParent AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            IF storedParent = parentOID THEN
                LET storedLength AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40))
                IF ArcFSNameMatches(base, storedLength, nameBuffer, nameLength) = 1 THEN
                    found = row
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindNamespaceRowByChild(oid AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 56))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET child AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 48))
            IF child = oid THEN
                found = row
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindFreeNamespaceRow() AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 56))
        IF active = 0 THEN
            IF found = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
                found = row
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSWriteName(base AS MMIOPTR, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS U64
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < ArcFSMaxNameLength()
        LET v AS U8 = 0
        IF i < nameLength THEN
            v = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuffer, i))
        END IF
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8 + i), v)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: object creation (RFC-0039 Section 42's Namespace.Create*, made concrete). ---

FUNCTION ArcFSCreateEntry(parentOID AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64, entryType AS U64) AS U64
    IF nameLength = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF nameLength > ArcFSMaxNameLength() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET parentRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(parentOID)
    IF parentRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET parentType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(parentRow), 8))
    IF parentType <> 2 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET existingRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindNamespaceRow(parentOID, nameBuffer, nameLength)
    IF existingRow <> ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET freeNsRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindFreeNamespaceRow()
    IF freeNsRow = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET newOid AS U64 = ArcFSCreateObject(entryType)
    IF newOid = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET nsBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(freeNsRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(nsBase, parentOID)
    ArcFSWriteName(nsBase, nameBuffer, nameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 40), nameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 48), newOid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 56), 1)

    RETURN newOid
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.CreateFile(parentOID AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSCreateEntry(parentOID, nameBuffer, nameLength, 1)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.CreateDirectory(parentOID AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSCreateEntry(parentOID, nameBuffer, nameLength, 2)
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: single-component lookup and colon-delimited path resolution (RFC-0039 Section
' 42's Namespace.Resolve, made concrete). Paths are root-relative and begin with ':'
' (RFC-0039 Section 9.1), e.g. ":home:documents:notes.txt"; ":" alone is the root. ---

FUNCTION ArcFS.Lookup(parentOID AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindNamespaceRow(parentOID, nameBuffer, nameLength)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row), 48))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.Resolve(pathBuffer AS MMIOPTR, pathLength AS U64) AS U64
    IF pathLength = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET firstByte AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(pathBuffer)
    IF firstByte <> 58 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF pathLength = 1 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    LET current AS U64 = 1
    LET cursor AS U64 = 1
    WHILE cursor < pathLength
        LET componentStart AS U64 = cursor
        LET scanning AS U64 = 1
        WHILE scanning = 1
            IF cursor >= pathLength THEN
                scanning = 0
            ELSE
                LET b AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(pathBuffer, cursor))
                IF b = 58 THEN
                    scanning = 0
                ELSE
                    cursor = cursor + 1
                END IF
            END IF
        WEND
        LET componentLength AS U64 = cursor - componentStart
        IF componentLength = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        LET componentPtr AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(pathBuffer, componentStart)
        LET nextOid AS U64 = ArcFS.Lookup(current, componentPtr, componentLength)
        IF nextOid = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        current = nextOid
        cursor = cursor + 1
    WEND
    RETURN current
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: rename/move (OID-preserving) and delete (RFC-0039 Requirement 6.2's own
' equivalents, Section 55, Section 23). ---

FUNCTION ArcFS.Rename(oid AS U64, newParentOID AS U64, newNameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, newNameLength AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF newNameLength = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF newNameLength > ArcFSMaxNameLength() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET oldRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindNamespaceRowByChild(oid)
    IF oldRow = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET newParentRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(newParentOID)
    IF newParentRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET newParentType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(newParentRow), 8))
    IF newParentType <> 2 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET collisionRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindNamespaceRow(newParentOID, newNameBuffer, newNameLength)
    IF collisionRow <> ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        IF collisionRow <> oldRow THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF

    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(oldRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(base, newParentOID)
    ArcFSWriteName(base, newNameBuffer, newNameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40), newNameLength)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Removes the namespace relationship only -- the object row (and its OID) is not reclaimed, per
' RFC-0039 Section 23's own deferred-reclamation policy ("physical extents become reclaimable only
' when no committed reachable state requires them"; Phase A has no reclamation pass yet).
FUNCTION ArcFS.Remove(parentOID AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindNamespaceRow(parentOID, nameBuffer, nameLength)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row), 56), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: handles (RFC-0039 Section 47 -- a runtime handle is distinct from the persistent
' OID it resolves to; closing a handle does not delete the object, and a rename/move does not
' invalidate an already-open handle, since the handle only ever stores the OID, never a path). ---

FUNCTION ArcFSHandleRowAddress(index AS U64) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHandleTableAddress(), index * 16)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.OpenHandle(oid AS U64) AS U64
    LET objRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid)
    IF objRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(objRow), 8))
    IF objType <> 1 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET index AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxHandles()
    WHILE index < ArcFSMaxHandles()
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHandleRowAddress(index), 8))
        IF active = 0 THEN
            IF found = ArcFSMaxHandles() THEN
                found = index
            END IF
        END IF
        index = index + 1
    WEND
    IF found = ArcFSMaxHandles() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET row AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSHandleRowAddress(found)
    MEMORY.Write64(row, oid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 8), 1)
    RETURN found + 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.CloseHandle(handle AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF handle = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET index AS U64 = handle - 1
    IF index >= ArcFSMaxHandles() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHandleRowAddress(index), 8), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHandleOid(handle AS U64) AS U64
    IF handle = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET index AS U64 = handle - 1
    IF index >= ArcFSMaxHandles() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHandleRowAddress(index), 8))
    IF active = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSHandleRowAddress(index))
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: byte streams (RFC-0039 Section 42's ByteStream, made concrete). Read/Write
' operate on the whole fixed-capacity file at once (no persistent position across calls) -- Phase
' A's own scope reduction #3 above; RFC-0039's own Seek/partial-write semantics are Phase C+ work
' once real extents exist to seek within. ---

' Phase D: the copy-on-write gate every MUTATING data-pool access (HandleWrite, Resize) must pass
' through before touching bytes. If the row's current data slot is exclusively its own (refcount
' <= 1), returns that slot unchanged -- the overwhelmingly common case, and byte-for-byte the same
' behavior Phase A/B/C's own HandleWrite always had, since a slot's refcount only ever exceeds 1
' after a Reflink (RFC-0039 Section 22's own requirement: "a reflink MUST NOT cause later writes to
' one file to modify the visible data of the other"). Otherwise, allocates a fresh exclusive slot,
' copies the shared bytes into it, and only THEN repoints the row -- so the write that follows never
' touches bytes another OID can still see. Returns ArcFSMaxObjects() (an invalid slot index) if no
' fresh slot is available, in which case the caller MUST refuse the mutation rather than risk
' writing into a still-shared page.
FUNCTION ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot(row AS U64) AS U64
    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24))
    LET rc AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), dataSlot * 8))
    IF rc <= 1 THEN
        RETURN dataSlot
    END IF

    LET newSlot AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateDataSlot()
    IF newSlot = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN ArcFSMaxObjects()
    END IF

    LET slotSize AS U64 = ArcFSSlotSizeBytes()
    LET srcBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * slotSize)
    LET dstBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), newSlot * slotSize)
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < slotSize
        LET v AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(srcBase, i))
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dstBase, i), v)
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    ' RFC-0040 Section 11: the touched-chunk bitmap is per-slot state exactly like the bytes
    ' themselves -- a clone that copies data but not touched-ness would silently turn every real
    ' chunk back into a hole (or vice versa: a stale bit left over from the new slot's PREVIOUS
    ' occupant, since data-pool slots are reused, would wrongly mark an untouched chunk touched).
    LET c AS U64 = 0
    WHILE c < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
        ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(newSlot, c, ArcFSChunkTouchedGet(dataSlot, c))
        c = c + 1
    WEND

    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), dataSlot * 8), rc - 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24), newSlot)
    RETURN newSlot
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 11.4, closing this phase's own last major deferred item: real offset-based
' partial writes, matching pwrite(2)'s real contract -- a write no longer unconditionally replaces
' the whole file from byte 0 (Phase A/B's own original scope reduction, closed by this increment).
' `size` becomes max(oldSize, offset+written): a write never SHRINKS the file on its own anymore,
' only ArcFS.Resize does that deliberately. This retroactively fixes the exact gotcha Phase K's own
' fixture had to work around with low-level data-pool verification (see
' .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-phase-k.md) -- a short write through a reflinked clone no longer hides
' the rest of the file behind a shrunk `size`; it patches its own range and leaves the rest
' genuinely visible, the correct, expected behavior for a real filesystem write. A gap between the
' OLD size and a write that starts PAST it is zero-filled in memory for THIS session's own read
' correctness (mirroring ArcFS.Resize's own growth path exactly) but not marked touched, so it
' becomes a real sparse hole at the next commit, not a zero-filled extent -- same reasoning, same
' construction, just triggered by a write's own offset instead of an explicit Resize call.
FUNCTION ArcFS.HandleWrite(handle AS U64, buffer AS MMIOPTR, byteCount AS U64, offset AS U64) AS U64
    LET oid AS U64 = ArcFSHandleOid(handle)
    IF oid = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot(row)
    IF dataSlot = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET slotSize AS U64 = ArcFSSlotSizeBytes()
    IF offset >= slotSize THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET toWrite AS U64 = byteCount
    IF offset + toWrite > slotSize THEN
        toWrite = slotSize - offset
    END IF
    IF toWrite = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
    LET oldSize AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16))
    LET dataBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * slotSize)

    IF offset > oldSize THEN
        LET g AS U64 = oldSize
        WHILE g < offset
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dataBase, g), 0)
            g = g + 1
        WEND
    END IF

    LET dest AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(dataBase, offset)
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < toWrite
        LET v AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(buffer, i))
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, i), v)
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    ' RFC-0040 Section 11: mark every chunk this write actually reached as touched, so it gets a
    ' real on-disk extent at commit -- a chunk this write never reached stays untouched (a hole).
    LET firstChunk AS U64 = offset \ ArcFSFileCapacityBytes()
    LET lastChunk AS U64 = (offset + toWrite - 1) \ ArcFSFileCapacityBytes()
    LET c AS U64 = firstChunk
    WHILE c <= lastChunk
        ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(dataSlot, c, 1)
        c = c + 1
    WEND

    LET newSize AS U64 = oldSize
    IF offset + toWrite > newSize THEN
        newSize = offset + toWrite
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), newSize)
    RETURN toWrite
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 11.4: real offset-based partial reads, matching pread(2)'s real contract --
' reading at or past EOF returns 0 (not an error); a range that starts within the file but would
' extend past it is clamped to what the file actually contains.
FUNCTION ArcFS.HandleRead(handle AS U64, buffer AS MMIOPTR, maxBytes AS U64, offset AS U64) AS U64
    LET oid AS U64 = ArcFSHandleOid(handle)
    IF oid = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET size AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row), 16))
    IF offset >= size THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row), 24))
    LET toRead AS U64 = maxBytes
    IF offset + toRead > size THEN
        toRead = size - offset
    END IF
    LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * ArcFSSlotSizeBytes() + offset)
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < toRead
        LET v AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, i))
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(buffer, i), v)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN toRead
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: resize (RFC-0039 Section 18's "MUST support... partial final blocks," scaled to
' Phase A's own fixed-file-capacity model -- Phase D scope reduction, stated in this section's own
' header below: growth is bounded by ArcFSSlotSizeBytes() (RFC-0040 Section 11's real per-slot
' cap, ArcFSMaxFileChunks() chunks of ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() each), not a real unbounded extent
' allocator). Growing zero-fills the newly exposed range in memory (matching Section 19's "a
' logical range with no physical extent SHALL read as zero bytes" for the bytes that were never
' written) but never marks it touched, so it becomes a real sparse hole -- not a real zero-filled
' extent -- the next time this file's chunks are committed; shrinking simply lowers the visible
' size, leaving the now-unreachable bytes (and their touched-ness) in place until something
' overwrites them. Goes through the same copy-on-write gate HandleWrite does, since growing a
' shared file would otherwise zero-fill bytes another OID can still see. ---

FUNCTION ArcFS.Resize(handle AS U64, newSize AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET oid AS U64 = ArcFSHandleOid(handle)
    IF oid = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF newSize > ArcFSSlotSizeBytes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot(row)
    IF dataSlot = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
    LET oldSize AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16))
    IF newSize > oldSize THEN
        ' Deliberately does NOT mark the newly exposed chunks touched: this range is exposed by
        ' growth alone, never written through HandleWrite, so it stays a real sparse hole at
        ' commit (RFC-0040 Section 11.3 / RFC-0039 Section 19's "a logical range with no physical
        ' extent SHALL read as zero bytes") rather than a real zero-filled extent -- correct AND
        ' more space-efficient than allocating one. The in-memory zero-fill below still matters:
        ' it's what makes THIS session's reads correct before any commit/remount round-trip ever
        ' happens.
        LET dest AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * ArcFSSlotSizeBytes())
        LET i AS U64 = oldSize
        WHILE i < newSize
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, i), 0)
            i = i + 1
        WEND
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), newSize)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: reflinks (RFC-0039 Section 22). The new object gets its OWN OID and namespace
' entry but SHARES the source's data-pool slot -- a real shared-until-diverged relationship, not an
' eager copy: nothing is copied at Reflink time. ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot (used by HandleWrite/Resize
' above) is what makes the sharing safe -- the FIRST write through either OID after a reflink
' copies the shared bytes to a fresh, exclusive slot before touching anything, so "a reflink MUST
' NOT cause later writes to one file to modify the visible data of the other" (Section 22) holds by
' construction, not by convention. Only FILE objects are reflinkable (matching Section 22's own
' "efficient file duplication" framing; RFC-0039 does not describe directory reflinking). ---

FUNCTION ArcFS.Reflink(sourceOid AS U64, destParentOid AS U64, destNameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, destNameLength AS U64) AS U64
    IF destNameLength = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF destNameLength > ArcFSMaxNameLength() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET sourceRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(sourceOid)
    IF sourceRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET sourceType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(sourceRow), 8))
    IF sourceType <> 1 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET destParentRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(destParentOid)
    IF destParentRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET destParentType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(destParentRow), 8))
    IF destParentType <> 2 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET existingRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindNamespaceRow(destParentOid, destNameBuffer, destNameLength)
    IF existingRow <> ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET freeNsRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindFreeNamespaceRow()
    IF freeNsRow = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET freeObjRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindFreeObjectRow()
    IF freeObjRow = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET sourceSize AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(sourceRow), 16))
    LET sourceDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(sourceRow), 24))

    LET newOid AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateOid()
    LET newOidHigh AS U64 = ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh()
    LET newBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(freeObjRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(newBase, newOid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(newBase, 8), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(newBase, 16), sourceSize)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(newBase, 24), sourceDataSlot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(newBase, 32), newOidHigh)

    LET rcAddr AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), sourceDataSlot * 8)
    LET rc AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(rcAddr)
    MEMORY.Write64(rcAddr, rc + 1)

    LET nsBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(freeNsRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(nsBase, destParentOid)
    ArcFSWriteName(nsBase, destNameBuffer, destNameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 40), destNameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 48), newOid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 56), 1)

    RETURN newOid
END FUNCTION

' --- Public API: typed attributes (RFC-0039 Section 24 / RFC-0040 Section 12) -- ONE typed
' attribute slot per object (RFC-0040 Section 12.1's own literal "(OID, attribute namespace,
' attribute name/ID)" multi-attribute-per-object model is NOT implemented; see this section's own
' scope-reduction note below), now DURABLE -- closing RFC-0039 Phase D's own explicitly documented
' gap ("a value set here does NOT survive ArcFS.MountImage()"), RFC-0040 Section 12.3's own literal
' requirement. Row layout, 24 bytes: [type U64][valueLow U64][valueHigh U64]. `type` doubles as the
' presence flag (0 = absent, matching every other "0 is impossible/empty" convention in this file);
' `valueHigh` is unused (stays 0) for every type except UUID/OID, the one RFC-0039 Section 24 base
' type that genuinely needs 128 bits -- the same high/low pair convention this file's own OIDs
' already use. Of Section 24's base value types (UTF-8 string, signed/unsigned integer, boolean,
' timestamp, binary blob, UUID/OID, typed locator), only the integer-representable subset is
' reachable at all on this backend (STRING has no substring/length operations here, the same
' limitation Phase A's own scope reduction #4 already gave) -- unsigned integer, signed integer
' (stored as raw bits; this backend has no native signed integer type, so "signed" is purely the
' CALLER's own interpretation of the same bit pattern, the same convention this whole codebase
' already uses wherever a signed concept is needed but the language has none), boolean, timestamp,
' and UUID/OID. ---

FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeUnsigned() AS U64
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeSigned() AS U64
    RETURN 2
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeBoolean() AS U64
    RETURN 3
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeTimestamp() AS U64
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeUuidOid() AS U64
    RETURN 5
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 12: variable-length attribute values, added without widening the 64-byte
' on-disk Attribute Tree entry -- see ArcFSSetVariableAttribute's own header note for how.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeString() AS U64
    RETURN 6
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAttrTypeBlob() AS U64
    RETURN 7
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 12.1: row layout is (OID, attributeId) keyed, not object-row-index keyed --
' [0 oidLow][8 oidHigh][16 attributeId][24 active][32 type][40 valueLow][48 valueHigh]
' [56 reserved0], 64 bytes. `attributeId` is a caller-defined integer, not the RFC's literal
' "namespace + name/ID" string pair -- this backend's own established "use integer IDs instead of
' strings" pattern, the same reason every other keyed table in this file -- FAT32 raw buffers,
' OIDs, chunk indices -- already uses integers, not names (freestanding STRING gained real LEN/MID
' since this row layout was designed, but they operate on in-memory buffers, not on-disk keys, so
' this reasoning is unchanged -- see .agents/reports/freestanding-len-mid.md). `oid` throughout
' this section stays a single U64 (the low half only), matching this file's own established,
' already-accepted low-64-bits-only lookup limitation (see RFC-0040 Section 10's own header note)
' -- real oidHigh IS still stored per row (round-tripped faithfully to/from disk) even though
' lookup never compares it. RFC-0042 Section 10.3: widened from 56 to 64 bytes, one reserved U64
' field, the same "genuinely unpopulated, ON-DISK leaf entry unchanged" treatment as the Object and
' Namespace rows.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row AS U64) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeTableAddress(), row * 64)
END FUNCTION

' Linear scan for the row currently holding (oid, attributeId), or ArcFSMaxAttributes() (the
' standard "not found" sentinel this file already uses for row/slot lookups) if none does.
FUNCTION ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxAttributes()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET rowOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            LET rowAttrId AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16))
            IF rowOid = oid THEN
                IF rowAttrId = attributeId THEN
                    RETURN row
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN ArcFSMaxAttributes()
END FUNCTION

' Linear scan for the first inactive row, or ArcFSMaxAttributes() if the table is full.
FUNCTION ArcFSFindFreeAttributeRow() AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxAttributes()
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row), 24))
        IF active = 0 THEN
            RETURN row
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    RETURN ArcFSMaxAttributes()
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.SetAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, attrType AS U64, valueLow AS U64, valueHigh AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF attrType = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF attrType > ArcFSAttrTypeUuidOid() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid) = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        row = ArcFSFindFreeAttributeRow()
        IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF
    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row)
    MEMORY.Write64(base, oid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), attributeId)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 32), attrType)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40), valueLow)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 48), valueHigh)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.HasAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64) AS BOOL
    RETURN ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId) <> ArcFSMaxAttributes()
END FUNCTION

' Returns 0 if this (oid, attributeId) has no attribute set (or the row is absent), otherwise one
' of the ArcFSAttrType* constants above -- callers that must distinguish a genuinely-absent
' attribute from a real value should check this (or ArcFS.HasAttribute) first.
FUNCTION ArcFS.GetAttributeType(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row), 32))
END FUNCTION

' Returns the stored value's low half, or 0 if this (oid, attributeId) has no attribute set --
' callers that must distinguish a genuinely-zero value from "absent" should check
' ArcFS.HasAttribute first. This is the whole value for every type except UUID/OID.
FUNCTION ArcFS.GetAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row), 40))
END FUNCTION

' Returns the stored value's high half -- only meaningful for a UUID/OID-typed attribute (every
' other type leaves this 0). 0 if this (oid, attributeId) has no attribute set.
FUNCTION ArcFS.GetAttributeHigh(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row), 48))
END FUNCTION

' New with RFC-0040 Section 12.1: meaningful now that an individual (oid, attributeId) can be
' cleared without clobbering an object's OTHER attributes (the old one-slot-per-object model made
' this equivalent to just never calling ArcFS.SetAttribute again -- there was nothing else to
' preserve). Clears only the active flag; the row is free for ArcFSFindFreeAttributeRow to reuse.
FUNCTION ArcFS.RemoveAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row), 24), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0042 Section 12: variable-length (UTF-8 string / binary blob) attribute values. The
' on-disk Attribute Tree entry (48 bytes: oidLow/oidHigh/attributeId/type/valueLow/valueHigh)
' stays completely UNCHANGED -- for a String or Blob typed entry, `valueLow`/`valueHigh` simply
' change MEANING (a data-pool slot index and a real byte length, instead of a plain 64-bit
' value/high-half), reusing the SAME Data Pool + Extent Tree machinery a FILE's own content
' already uses, rather than widening the row or inventing a second allocator. No on-disk format
' change at all -- exactly the kind of silent breaking growth RFC-0042 exists to prevent.
' Capped at ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() (one chunk, 4096 bytes): plenty for a real UTF-8 string or
' small binary blob, and keeps the touched-chunk bookkeeping to exactly one chunk per value
' rather than needing the multi-chunk logic an ordinary file uses. See ArcFSGatherUniqueDataSlots
' and ArcFSPopulateAttributeRow (above) for the commit-time and mount-time halves of this.
' ==========================================================================================

' Shared implementation for ArcFS.SetStringAttribute/ArcFS.SetBlobAttribute -- identical mechanism
' for both; only the stored type code differs (purely the READER's own interpretation of the
' bytes). Zero-pads the whole chunk before copying in the real bytes, matching this file's own
' established "clear before populate" convention (e.g. ArcFS.Initialize's own table clears).
FUNCTION ArcFSSetVariableAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, attrType AS U64, bufferPtr AS MMIOPTR, byteLength AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF byteLength > ArcFSFileCapacityBytes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSFindObjectRow(oid) = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        row = ArcFSFindFreeAttributeRow()
        IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateDataSlot()
    IF dataSlot = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET slotBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * ArcFSSlotSizeBytes())
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < ArcFSFileCapacityBytes()
        LET v AS U8 = 0
        IF i < byteLength THEN
            v = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(bufferPtr, i))
        END IF
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(slotBase, i), v)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(dataSlot, 0, 1)

    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row)
    MEMORY.Write64(base, oid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), attributeId)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 32), attrType)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40), dataSlot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 48), byteLength)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.SetStringAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, bufferPtr AS MMIOPTR, byteLength AS U64) AS BOOL
    RETURN ArcFSSetVariableAttribute(oid, attributeId, ArcFSAttrTypeString(), bufferPtr, byteLength)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.SetBlobAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, bufferPtr AS MMIOPTR, byteLength AS U64) AS BOOL
    RETURN ArcFSSetVariableAttribute(oid, attributeId, ArcFSAttrTypeBlob(), bufferPtr, byteLength)
END FUNCTION

' Shared implementation for ArcFS.GetStringAttribute/ArcFS.GetBlobAttribute -- retrieval doesn't
' care about the byte interpretation, just copies real bytes back. Returns the actual byte length
' copied, or 0 if the attribute is absent OR destCapacity is insufficient -- fails closed rather
' than silently truncating, the same discipline every other bounds check in this file already
' uses.
FUNCTION ArcFSGetVariableAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, destPtr AS MMIOPTR, destCapacity AS U64) AS U64
    LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindAttributeRow(oid, attributeId)
    IF row = ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row)
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40))
    LET byteLength AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 48))
    IF byteLength > destCapacity THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET slotBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * ArcFSSlotSizeBytes())
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < byteLength
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(destPtr, i), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(slotBase, i)))
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN byteLength
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetStringAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, destPtr AS MMIOPTR, destCapacity AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSGetVariableAttribute(oid, attributeId, destPtr, destCapacity)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetBlobAttribute(oid AS U64, attributeId AS U64, destPtr AS MMIOPTR, destCapacity AS U64) AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSGetVariableAttribute(oid, attributeId, destPtr, destCapacity)
END FUNCTION

' --- Bootstrap: resets every table and creates the root directory (OID 1, which -- unlike every
' other object -- has no namespace entry of its own, matching real filesystems where root has no
' name; RFC-0039 Section 9.1's "the colon is the namespace component separator," and ":" alone
' already denotes root directly in ArcFS.Resolve above without ever looking it up by name). ---

FUNCTION ArcFS.Initialize() AS BOOL
    ' RFC-0042 Section 11: Object Table clear widened from 64*56=3584 to 2048*56=114688 bytes, and
    ' Namespace Table from 128*80=10240 to 4096*80=327680 bytes -- both tables' own row COUNTS grew
    ' 32x (ArcFSMaxObjects()/ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()), on top of Phase P's own per-row widening.
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < 114688
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectTableAddress(), i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    i = 0
    WHILE i < 327680
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSNamespaceTableAddress(), i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    i = 0
    WHILE i < 1024
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHandleTableAddress(), i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    ' Phase D additions: clear the data-slot refcount table (RFC-0042 Section 11: widened from 512
    ' to 2048*8=16384 bytes, ArcFSMaxObjects() raised 32x) and the Attribute Table (RFC-0040 Section
    ' 12.1's own row layout, widened again by RFC-0042 Section 11 from 128 rows to 4096 rows -- on
    ' top of Phase P's own per-row widening to 64 bytes/row -- for a real need of 4096*64=262144
    ' bytes), and reset the data-slot bump allocator.
    i = 0
    WHILE i < 16384
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    i = 0
    WHILE i < 262144
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeTableAddress(), i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextOidAddress(), 2)
    ' RFC-0040 Section 10: root's real 128-bit identity is (high=0, low=1); the next OID this
    ' volume allocates is (high=0, low=2), matching the low-half-only counter reset above.
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextOidHighAddress(), 0)

    LET rootBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(0)
    MEMORY.Write64(rootBase, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(rootBase, 8), 2)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(rootBase, 16), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(rootBase, 24), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(rootBase, 32), 0)

    ' Root claims data-pool slot 0 directly (matching its hardcoded row 0 above, not through
    ' ArcFSAllocateDataSlot) -- mark that slot exclusively owned and start the bump allocator at 1
    ' so the next real allocation (Phase D's ArcFSAllocateDataSlot, or a data reload during a
    ' future mount) never collides with it.
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), 0), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress(), 1)

    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0043 Phase S: real, selectable persistent block-device backing. Every read/write in this
' file has, until now, gone directly to `RAMDisk.*` -- a fixed range of plain memory the QEMU/OVMF
' test harness pre-populates before boot (see RAMDisk.Create's own header note in
' block_device_policy.abas). That means every "commit + remount" proof in this file's entire
' history has been same-process: nothing has ever left the process that wrote it. This section
' gives ArcFS the exact same real-block-device dispatch `fat32_policy.abas`'s own
' `FAT32.SelectBlockDevice`/`FAT32BlockReadSectors`/`FAT32BlockSectorCount` already established and
' already proved against real hardware (RFC-0038 Section 17.5, `.agents/reports/
' aps-blockio-fat32-uefi.md`) -- the identical shape, not a new design. Defaults to provider 0
' (RAMDisk) so every existing frozen fixture in RFC-0039/0040/0042's own history needs zero
' changes: fresh scratch memory reads as zero at boot in this project's own established QEMU/OVMF
' environment, the same fact every other never-explicitly-initialized selector in this file
' already relies on.
' ==========================================================================================

FUNCTION ArcFSBlockDeviceSelectorAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35209216))   ' 0x2194000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.BlockDeviceProviderRamDisk() AS U32
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.BlockDeviceProviderUefi() AS U32
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Selects which real BlockDevice provider every ArcFS storage call below goes through. MUST be
' called before ArcFS.FormatVolume/MountImage if UefiBlockDevice is wanted -- both read/write
' through the dispatch below from their own first sector access, the same "select before use"
' contract FAT32.SelectBlockDevice already established. Rejects any value other than the two named
' providers (fail-closed).
FUNCTION ArcFS.SelectBlockDevice(provider AS U32) AS BOOL
    IF provider <> 0 THEN
        IF provider <> 1 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write32(ArcFSBlockDeviceSelectorAddress(), provider)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.ActiveBlockDevice() AS U32
    RETURN MEMORY.Read32(ArcFSBlockDeviceSelectorAddress())
END FUNCTION

' UefiBlockDevice.ReadSectors/SectorCount fail closed by construction even if a caller selects
' provider 1 without ever calling UefiBlockDevice.Discover first (SectorCount() reads as 0 from
' never-touched scratch state, so every non-empty request gets rejected by that function's own
' bounds check) -- the identical property FAT32BlockReadSectors/FAT32BlockSectorCount already rely
' on, confirmed there rather than re-derived here.
FUNCTION ArcFSBlockReadSectors(startSector AS U64, count AS U32, destination AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    IF ArcFS.ActiveBlockDevice() = 1 THEN
        RETURN UefiBlockDevice.ReadSectors(startSector, count, destination)
    END IF
    RETURN RAMDisk.ReadSectors(startSector, count, destination)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(startSector AS U64, count AS U32, source AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    IF ArcFS.ActiveBlockDevice() = 1 THEN
        RETURN UefiBlockDevice.WriteSectors(startSector, count, source)
    END IF
    RETURN RAMDisk.WriteSectors(startSector, count, source)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSBlockSectorCount() AS U64
    IF ArcFS.ActiveBlockDevice() = 1 THEN
        RETURN UefiBlockDevice.SectorCount()
    END IF
    RETURN RAMDisk.SectorCount()
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' Phase B: read-only ArcFS image (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase B -- "superblock parser;
' checkpoint parser; object/namespace trees; extent reads; checksums; image inspection").
' ==========================================================================================
'
' ArcFS.MountImage() reads a real on-disk (well, on-BlockDevice) image through RFC-0038's
' RAMDisk.*/UefiBlockDevice.*/BlockDevice contract (RFC-0043 Phase S's own dispatch above selects
' which) -- RFC-0039 Section 80 lists a BlockStorage interface as an
' outside dependency this RFC needs; RFC-0038 is that dependency, used here for the first time --
' validates its superblock, checkpoint, and every object/namespace record's checksum, and
' POPULATES Phase A's existing in-memory tables from it. Every Phase A function
' (Resolve/Lookup/OpenHandle/HandleRead/HandleWrite/Rename/Remove) then works completely
' unchanged against the loaded data -- this is deliberate, not a shortcut: it is the concrete
' proof that Phase A's object/namespace/handle contract (RFC-0039's own stated Phase A purpose)
' is genuinely disk-format-agnostic, not something that happened to work only in memory.
'
' This is Phase B's OWN minimal, documented subset of RFC-0039's real on-disk format (Sections
' 11-17) -- see arcology-os/scripts/build/build-arcfs-test-image.py's own header comment for the
' full list of simplifications (one superblock, not a ring; one checkpoint, not generation
' history; flat fixed-size-record arrays, not real B+trees; a simple additive checksum, not a
' cryptographic one; one contiguous 8-sector extent per file, matching Phase A's own fixed-file-
' capacity scope reduction). Read-only: there is no writer here, matching Phase A's own decision
' to defer transactions to Phase C, where a real staging concept will exist to make transactional.

FUNCTION ArcFSSectorScratchAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34099200))   ' 0x2085000
END FUNCTION

' Mount-time parsed state. All U64 fields:
'   [0] checkpointSector    -- the ACTIVE checkpoint sector (mirrors the superblock's own pointer)
'   [8] objectRoot
'   [16] objectCount
'   [24] namespaceRoot
'   [32] namespaceCount
'   [40] dataRegionStart
'   [48] maxOidSeen
'   [56] generation         -- Phase C addition: the active checkpoint's generation number
'   [64] highWaterMark      -- Phase C addition, RFC-0040 Section 7 REDEFINITION: the exact
'                              next-never-used sector, loaded verbatim from the checkpoint's own
'                              durable field (RFC-0040 Section 7's real Allocation Bitmap replaced
'                              Phase C's conservative bump-cursor ESTIMATE -- see
'                              ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord's own header note for why an estimate
'                              stopped being safe once extents could be reused/scattered). Both
'                              metadata placement (still contiguous-append, unchanged) and
'                              ArcFSAllocateDataExtent's own growth fallback share this one
'                              boundary, so the two can never collide.
'   [72] pendingCheckpointSector -- Phase C addition: set by ArcFS.PrepareCommit, consumed (and
'                              cleared) by ArcFS.PublishCommit; 0 means "no prepared commit"
'   [80] previousCheckpointSector -- Phase H addition (RFC-0040 Section 8.3): the active
'                              checkpoint's own PreviousGeneration field, i.e. the checkpoint
'                              sector committed immediately before this one; 0 means "no
'                              previous" (this is generation 1). Recorded for forensic history
'                              walking; unconsumed by any Phase H function.
'   [88] maxOidHigh           -- RFC-0040 Section 10 addition: the high half of the highest OID
'                              this generation's own object records actually contain, tracked
'                              alongside [48]'s existing low half via a real 128-bit comparison
'                              (high first, low on a tie). Used only to correctly resume
'                              allocation past this generation's true high-water mark.
'   [96] allocationBitmapSector -- RFC-0040 Section 7.1 addition: the active checkpoint's own
'                              Allocation Bitmap sector, loaded into the in-memory mirror by
'                              ArcFSLoadBitmap (a separate step from ArcFSReadCheckpoint, matching
'                              every other tree's own load-after-parse-checkpoint pattern).
'   [104] attributeTreeRoot   -- RFC-0040 Section 12.1 addition: the active checkpoint's own
'                              Attribute Tree root node sector; 0 means "no attributes at all
'                              this generation" (the common case -- most objects have none), the
'                              same "0 is impossible, so 0 is the empty-tree sentinel" convention
'                              the Namespace Tree already established.
'   [112] attributeTreeCount  -- RFC-0040 Section 12.1 addition: how many attribute entries the
'                              Attribute Tree holds this generation.
'   [120] extentTreeRoot      -- RFC-0040 Section 11.1 addition: the active checkpoint's own
'                              Extent Tree root node sector; 0 means "no FILE currently has any
'                              real, touched chunk" (the legitimate empty-tree case), the same
'                              sentinel convention every other tree already established.
'   [128] extentTreeCount     -- RFC-0040 Section 11.1 addition: how many (dataSlot, chunkIndex)
'                              leaf entries the Extent Tree holds this generation.
FUNCTION ArcFSMountStateAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34103296))   ' 0x2086000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 15.1: Format Major Version 2 (FMV2). "ARCFSB02" -- FMV1's own "ARCFSB01"
' magic (RFC-0039 Phases B-G) is deliberately a DIFFERENT byte string, not a version field this
' function tolerates -- an FMV1-formatted volume MUST NOT be interpreted by this FMV2 reader, and
' vice versa (RFC-0039 Section 62.3, already normative). FMV1's own fixtures/reports remain valid
' as history (RFC-0040 Section 15.3); this reference implementation itself now targets FMV2 only.
FUNCTION ArcFSMagicMatches(base AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 0)) <> 65 THEN RETURN 0   ' 'A'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 1)) <> 82 THEN RETURN 0   ' 'R'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 2)) <> 67 THEN RETURN 0   ' 'C'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 3)) <> 70 THEN RETURN 0   ' 'F'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 4)) <> 83 THEN RETURN 0   ' 'S'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 5)) <> 66 THEN RETURN 0   ' 'B'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 6)) <> 48 THEN RETURN 0   ' '0'
    IF MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 7)) <> 50 THEN RETURN 0   ' '2' -- FMV2, was '1' (FMV1)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 8.4: CRC-32C (Castagnoli), replacing FMV1's additive sum-of-bytes placeholder
' (RFC-0039 Section 20.2's "SHOULD use a modern high-performance checksum with strong accidental-
' corruption detection"). Bit-at-a-time over the reflected polynomial 0x82F63B78 -- every record
' this format defines is a handful of bytes, so a lookup table buys nothing real here; this is the
' textbook reflected-CRC construction (init all-ones, XOR-in each byte, shift right 8 times per
' byte conditionally XORing the reflected polynomial, final XOR all-ones), the same algorithm
' iSCSI/SCTP/ext4 use under the CRC-32C name. Matches build-arcfs-test-image.py's own Python
' reimplementation of the identical algorithm on the image-building side -- see that script's own
' header comment.
FUNCTION ArcFSCrc32C(base AS MMIOPTR, count AS U64) AS U64
    LET crc AS U64 = 4294967295   ' 0xFFFFFFFF
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET b AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, i))
        LET bAsU64 AS U64 = b
        crc = crc XOR bAsU64
        LET bit AS U64 = 0
        WHILE bit < 8
            LET lsb AS U64 = crc AND 1
            crc = crc SHR 1
            IF lsb <> 0 THEN
                crc = crc XOR 2197175160   ' 0x82F63B78, reflected CRC-32C polynomial
            END IF
            bit = bit + 1
        WEND
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN (crc XOR 4294967295) AND 4294967295
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 7: the superblock's own feature-flags field (offset 48, inside the checksummed
' region) split into three 16-bit sub-fields -- compat (bits 0-15, an older reader MAY safely
' ignore), incompat (bits 16-31, an older reader MUST refuse to mount for), ro-compat (bits 32-47,
' an older reader MAY mount read-only but MUST NOT mount read-write) -- plus 16 bits of genuine
' forward-compatible headroom (48-63, MUST be written as 0, MUST be ignored, never rejected, by a
' reader that finds them nonzero). The field has held a literal 0 and the comment "none defined
' yet" since Phase H; RFC-0042 Phase N built this mechanism without yet giving it a real consumer
' (no actual feature bit was defined by RFC-0042 itself -- Section 7.3's own "never live inside
' this RFC's own new fields," since Section 8's checkpoint self-description is unconditional
' baseline behavior, not an optional feature gated by a bit). RFC-0043 Phase U (Section 7.3) is the
' first REAL consumer: ArcFSFeatureMultiSectorBitmap() below.
FUNCTION ArcFSFeatureMultiSectorBitmap() AS U64
    RETURN 65536   ' bit 16, the lowest incompat bit -- the first one this format has ever defined
END FUNCTION

' An older reader (any build before RFC-0043 Phase U) does not know the Allocation Bitmap's on-disk
' image now spans ArcFSBitmapSectorCount() sectors, not one -- reading only the first sector and
' treating byte 508 as a whole-bitmap checksum would misread real allocation state. Recognizing
' this bit and refusing to mount without it protects exactly that direction; the reverse (this
' code reading an OLD, pre-Phase-U single-sector volume) is not attempted -- see RFC-0043's own
' Non-Goals, matching RFC-0042 Section 4 Non-Goal 3's identical "no live migration of an old shape"
' position for FMV1.
' A real bit-field-scoping bug caught here before this was ever exercised with a genuinely
' nonzero recognized mask (Phase N's own `0` value could never have surfaced it): `ArcFSCheck
' FeatureFlags` compares against `incompatBits`, the ALREADY-EXTRACTED 16-bit sub-field
' ((rawFlags \ 65536) MOD 65536) -- NOT against the raw 64-bit flags value ArcFSFeatureMultiSector
' Bitmap() represents. Dividing back out of raw-flags space into sub-field space here is what
' makes the comparison land on the same bit position `ArcFSCheckFeatureFlags` itself already
' extracts, confirmed directly by running a real mount immediately after a real format before
' trusting this.
FUNCTION ArcFSRecognizedIncompatMask() AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSFeatureMultiSectorBitmap() \ 65536
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSRecognizedRoCompatMask() AS U64
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' A mount-time-only companion address (the same "0 is impossible for a real checksum-verified
' scratch overwrite, so pair it with its own address instead of widening a return type" idiom
' ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh already established): the feature-flags field belonging to whichever
' ring slot ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint's own scan most recently selected as best.
FUNCTION ArcFSLastSelectedFeatureFlagsAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35147776))   ' 0x2185000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 7.2: the mount-time contract. Returns 0 (refuse to mount) if ANY unrecognized
' incompat bit is set; 2 (mount read-only) if no unrecognized incompat bit is set but an
' unrecognized ro-compat bit is; 1 (mount normally) otherwise -- including when every bit is 0,
' every volume this build has ever formatted, unchanged. `bits - (bits AND mask)` computes exactly
' the bits set in `bits` but not in `mask` without needing a bitwise NOT this backend has no
' operator for (verified: only AND/OR/XOR exist as binary bitwise operators here) -- safe because
' `bits AND mask` is always a bitwise subset of `bits`, so ordinary subtraction never borrows.
' `flags` is a reserved word in this dialect (confirmed directly, the same gotcha this project has
' already hit for `next`) -- the parameter is named `rawFlags` instead.
FUNCTION ArcFSCheckFeatureFlags(rawFlags AS U64) AS U64
    LET incompatBits AS U64 = (rawFlags \ 65536) MOD 65536
    LET roCompatBits AS U64 = (rawFlags \ 4294967296) MOD 65536
    LET unrecognizedIncompat AS U64 = incompatBits - (incompatBits AND ArcFSRecognizedIncompatMask())
    IF unrecognizedIncompat <> 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET unrecognizedRoCompat AS U64 = roCompatBits - (roCompatBits AND ArcFSRecognizedRoCompatMask())
    IF unrecognizedRoCompat <> 0 THEN
        RETURN 2
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0042 Section 8.2: disambiguates a checkpoint record written before this RFC (104-byte
' payload, checksum at the fixed legacy offset 104) from one written by this build or later (a
' self-describing recordLength field AT offset 104, checksum at offset recordLength) purely by
' which interpretation's own checksum actually validates -- not a version flag. Moving recordLength
' to offset 0 (ahead of `generation`) was considered and rejected: it would make every already-
' existing FMV2 checkpoint unreadable outright, exactly the breaking, untested growth RFC-0042
' exists to stop making (see that RFC's own Section 8.1). Appending it instead, disambiguated by
' checksum validity, costs nothing for an already-existing record and needs this one transitional
' trick exactly once -- every future field addition after this one is unambiguously self-described
' by `recordLength` alone. Returns the confirmed real payload length (104 for a legacy record, the
' record's own self-described length otherwise), or 0 (an impossible real length, the same
' "0 is impossible, so 0 is the sentinel" convention this file already uses for tree roots) if
' NEITHER interpretation's checksum validates.
FUNCTION ArcFSVerifyCheckpointChecksum(scratch AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    LET legacyStored AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 104))
    LET legacyComputed AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 104)
    IF legacyStored = legacyComputed THEN
        RETURN 104
    END IF

    LET candidateLength AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 104))
    ' A self-describing record must be at least 112 bytes (the 104 legacy bytes plus the
    ' recordLength field itself) and must leave room for its own 4-byte checksum inside one
    ' 512-byte sector (candidateLength + 4 <= 512).
    IF candidateLength < 112 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF candidateLength > 508 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET selfStored AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, candidateLength))
    LET selfComputed AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, candidateLength)
    IF selfStored <> selfComputed THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN candidateLength
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 8.1/8.2: the superblock ring. Sectors 0-3 (fixed, predetermined -- "at least 4
' redundant superblock copies") each hold a byte-identical copy of the current superblock. Reading
' back the ring validates every copy independently (magic, format major, checksum, logical block
' size) and, among the copies that pass, selects the one whose OWN referenced checkpoint has the
' highest generation number -- so a ring left partially updated by a crash (some copies still
' pointing at the old checkpoint, others already pointing at the new one) still resolves to
' "whichever generation is more recent among what is actually readable," never a torn/ambiguous
' state, and never silently prefers a stale copy just because it happens to be ring slot 0.
' Fails closed (returns 0, matching every prior phase's activation-failure contract) only if EVERY
' ring copy is unusable or every copy's own referenced checkpoint fails its own validation.
' RFC-0040 Section 13.1: this ring slot's own candidate generation, or 0 if it doesn't validate at
' all (unreadable, bad magic, bad superblock checksum, wrong geometry/format major, or its own
' checkpoint fails ITS checksum) -- the building block for ArcFSRingIsConsistent below.
FUNCTION ArcFSRingSlotGeneration(ringSlot AS U64) AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(ringSlot, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSMagicMatches(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 64))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 64)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET logicalBlockSize AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32))
    IF logicalBlockSize <> 512 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET formatMajor AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8))
    IF formatMajor <> 2 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET candidateCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 56))
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(candidateCheckpointSector, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSVerifyCheckpointChecksum(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(scratch)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 13.1/14: real ring-WIDE consistency, distinct from mere ACTIVATION (which only
' ever needed the highest-generation valid copy -- RFC-0040 Section 8.2's own "self-healing to
' match on the next successful publish" already tolerates a stale copy silently). A copy that
' validates but disagrees with the winning generation is a genuine, real anomaly an online scan of
' the ACTIVE generation's own tree cannot explain (it says nothing about WHY a ring copy went
' stale) -- exactly the "needs a deeper, offline look" signal ArcFS.GetHealthState's own
' NeedsOfflineCheck state exists for. Returns 0 (inconsistent) if any VALIDATING slot's own
' generation differs from the best one found; an unreadable/invalid slot does not by itself count
' (that is Section 8.2's own already-tolerated, self-healing case, not a new anomaly).
FUNCTION ArcFSRingIsConsistent() AS BOOL
    LET g0 AS U64 = ArcFSRingSlotGeneration(0)
    LET g1 AS U64 = ArcFSRingSlotGeneration(1)
    LET g2 AS U64 = ArcFSRingSlotGeneration(2)
    LET g3 AS U64 = ArcFSRingSlotGeneration(3)
    LET best AS U64 = g0
    IF g1 > best THEN best = g1
    IF g2 > best THEN best = g2
    IF g3 > best THEN best = g3
    IF g0 <> 0 THEN
        IF g0 <> best THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF g1 <> 0 THEN
        IF g1 <> best THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF g2 <> 0 THEN
        IF g2 <> best THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF g3 <> 0 THEN
        IF g3 <> best THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 13/14: the ring-selection scan itself, factored out of ArcFSReadSuperblockRing
' as a SIDE-EFFECT-FREE query -- touches no live state, safe to call whether or not anything is
' currently mounted. Returns the highest-generation checkpoint sector any of the 4 ring copies
' validates to, or 0 if none do. ArcFS.ScrubVolume (RFC-0040 Section 13.1/14, widened this phase
' from its own documented "ring slot 0 only" scope reduction to real ring-wide selection) and
' ArcFS.GetHealthState both use this instead of duplicating the scan.
FUNCTION ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    LET bestGeneration AS U64 = 0
    LET bestCheckpointSector AS U64 = 0
    LET foundAny AS U64 = 0
    LET ringSlot AS U64 = 0
    WHILE ringSlot < 4
        IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(ringSlot, 1, scratch) <> 0 THEN
            IF ArcFSMagicMatches(scratch) <> 0 THEN
                LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 64))
                LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 64)
                IF storedChecksum = computedChecksum THEN
                    LET logicalBlockSize AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32))
                    LET formatMajor AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8))
                    IF logicalBlockSize = 512 THEN
                        IF formatMajor = 2 THEN
                            LET candidateCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 56))
                            ' RFC-0042 Section 7: this ring slot's own feature-flags field,
                            ' extracted here (still inside the already-validated 64-byte
                            ' checksummed superblock region) since `scratch` is about to be
                            ' re-read as the candidate checkpoint below.
                            LET candidateFlags AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 48))
                            ' Re-read scratch as the candidate checkpoint -- safe: this ring
                            ' slot's own superblock fields have already been extracted above into
                            ' locals, not left depending on scratch's contents any further.
                            IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(candidateCheckpointSector, 1, scratch) <> 0 THEN
                                IF ArcFSVerifyCheckpointChecksum(scratch) <> 0 THEN
                                    LET candidateGeneration AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(scratch)
                                    IF foundAny = 0 THEN
                                        bestGeneration = candidateGeneration
                                        bestCheckpointSector = candidateCheckpointSector
                                        foundAny = 1
                                        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSLastSelectedFeatureFlagsAddress(), candidateFlags)
                                    ELSE
                                        IF candidateGeneration > bestGeneration THEN
                                            bestGeneration = candidateGeneration
                                            bestCheckpointSector = candidateCheckpointSector
                                            MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSLastSelectedFeatureFlagsAddress(), candidateFlags)
                                        END IF
                                    END IF
                                END IF
                            END IF
                        END IF
                    END IF
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        ringSlot = ringSlot + 1
    WEND
    IF foundAny = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN bestCheckpointSector
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSReadSuperblockRing() AS BOOL
    LET bestCheckpointSector AS U64 = ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint()
    IF bestCheckpointSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), bestCheckpointSector)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSReadCheckpoint() AS BOOL
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET checkpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(state)
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(checkpointSector, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSVerifyCheckpointChecksum(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET generation AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(scratch)
    LET objectRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8))
    LET objectCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 16))
    LET namespaceRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 24))
    LET namespaceCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32))
    LET dataRegionStart AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 40))
    LET previousCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 48))
    LET allocationBitmapSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 56))
    LET highWaterMark AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 64))
    ' RFC-0040 Section 12.1: the Attribute Tree's own root sector + entry count, gained this
    ' phase -- see ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord's own header note for the full field-growth history.
    LET attributeTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 72))
    LET attributeTreeCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 80))
    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: the Extent Tree's own root sector + entry count, gained this phase.
    LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 88))
    LET extentTreeCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 96))
    IF objectCount > ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF namespaceCount > ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    ' RFC-0040 Section 12.1's multi-attribute model raised the real Attribute Table capacity to
    ' ArcFSMaxAttributes() (128), independent of ArcFSMaxObjects() (64) -- this sanity bound stayed
    ' on the OLD constant, a real latent bug: any legitimate checkpoint claiming between 65 and 128
    ' active attributes would have been wrongly refused as invalid. Never exercised by any fixture
    ' (none ever populated more than a handful of attributes at once), found while touching this
    ' exact function for RFC-0042.
    IF attributeTreeCount > ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    ' RFC-0042 Section 11: this bound was a bare literal 256 (ArcFSMaxObjects()*ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
    ' at the time it was written, 64*4, never re-derived from the named constants) -- caught and
    ' fixed while raising those same constants this phase, before it could become the identical
    ' class of stale-bound bug the attributeTreeCount check above already was.
    IF extentTreeCount > ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 8), objectRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 16), objectCount)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 24), namespaceRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 32), namespaceCount)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 40), dataRegionStart)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 56), generation)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 104), attributeTreeRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 112), attributeTreeCount)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 120), extentTreeRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 128), extentTreeCount)
    ' RFC-0040 Section 7: the EXACT high-water mark this checkpoint recorded, not a re-derived
    ' estimate (see ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord's own header note for why real extent reuse retired
    ' the old dataRegionStart+objectCount*8 conservative-overestimate formula).
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64), highWaterMark)
    ' RFC-0040 Section 8.3: generation history. Recorded for forensic inspection (walking
    ' PreviousGeneration backward through committed history without needing a snapshot taken in
    ' advance); nothing in Phase H's own scope consumes it yet, the same "prove the mechanism, name
    ' what doesn't consume it yet" pattern RFC-0039 Phase E's IsSectorReachable/
    ' ReclaimableSectorCount already established for this project.
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 80), previousCheckpointSector)
    ' RFC-0040 Section 7.1: this generation's own Allocation Bitmap sector, loaded into the
    ' in-memory mirror by ArcFSLoadBitmap (called separately by ArcFS.MountImage, since loading it
    ' needs a durably-checksum-verified read of its own, matching every other tree's own load
    ' step).
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 96), allocationBitmapSector)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Loads one object record (index into the on-disk array, 0-based) into Phase A's in-memory
' Object Table row of the SAME index. RFC-0040 Section 9: populates one flat in-memory Object
' Table row from an ALREADY-COLLECTED tree entry (not a fresh disk read -- the tree walk that
' produced it already checksum-verified every node it visited).
' RFC-0040 Section 11.1/11.5: `committedDataSlot` is the dataSlot value this object's Extent Tree
' entries were actually keyed by AT COMMIT TIME (possibly SHARED with another OID, if this was an
' undiverged reflink). Sharing now SURVIVES a remount for real: the FIRST row this mount to
' reference a given `committedDataSlot` (via ArcFSMountSlotOwnerAddress, reset once per
' ArcFSLoadObjects call, direct-indexed by committedDataSlot) becomes that live slot's real owner
' -- gets a fresh refcount of 1 at its OWN index and actually reads the chunks off disk into it --
' and every LATER row sharing the same committedDataSlot points its own `dataSlot` field at that
' SAME owner index instead of getting a private copy, incrementing the owner's refcount instead of
' loading anything a second time. This is safe by construction, not a new invariant:
' ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress/ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot/ArcFSAllocateDataSlot already only ever assumed a
' row's OWN `dataSlot` field is authoritative for where its bytes live (never that dataSlot equals
' row index) -- restoring real cross-OID sharing here needed no changes to any of them.
' ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress's own resume point (ArcFS.MountImage, `loadedObjectCount`) stays
' correct unchanged too: every live dataSlot assigned here, owner or sharer, is always SOME row's
' own index, strictly less than `loadedObjectCount`. Non-FILE objects (directories) are unaffected
' -- their own dataSlot is never reflinked, so they always own their own index, exactly as before.
FUNCTION ArcFSPopulateObjectRow(index AS U64, oid AS U64, objType AS U64, size AS U64, committedDataSlot AS U64, oidHigh AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET row AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(index)
    MEMORY.Write64(row, oid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 8), objType)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 16), size)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 32), oidHigh)

    LET maxOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 48))
    LET maxOidHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 88))
    LET oidIsGreater AS BOOL = 0
    IF oidHigh > maxOidHigh THEN
        oidIsGreater = 1
    ELSE
        IF oidHigh = maxOidHigh THEN
            IF oid > maxOid THEN
                oidIsGreater = 1
            END IF
        END IF
    END IF
    IF oidIsGreater = 1 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 48), oid)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 88), oidHigh)
    END IF

    IF objType = 1 THEN
        LET ownerTable AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountSlotOwnerAddress()
        LET owner AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ownerTable, committedDataSlot * 8))
        IF owner <> ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
            ' A row already loaded this generation owns the real live slot for this committed
            ' identity -- share it, exactly matching what a live in-session reflink already looks
            ' like, instead of paying for (and diverging from) a private copy.
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 24), owner)
            LET rc AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), owner * 8))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), owner * 8), rc + 1)
        ELSE
            ' First row this mount to reference this committed identity -- becomes the owner.
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 24), index)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ownerTable, committedDataSlot * 8), index)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), index * 8), 1)
            LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 120))
            IF ArcFSExtentTreeLoadDataSlotIntoRow(extentTreeRoot, committedDataSlot, index) = 0 THEN
                RETURN 0
            END IF
        END IF
    ELSE
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 24), index)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSlotRefCountAddress(), index * 8), 1)
    END IF

    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 9: replaces Phase B's own flat `objectRoot + index` mount loop. `objectRoot`
' now names the Object Tree's root NODE sector; walking it (ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto, whose
' own per-node checksum verification stands in for the old per-record check) yields every active
' object's real entry, which then populates the SAME fixed in-memory table every other function in
' this file still reads and writes completely unchanged. Cross-checks the collected count against
' the checkpoint's own recorded objectCount as a free extra integrity check the flat-array version
' never had -- a tree that silently dropped or duplicated a branch would be caught here.
FUNCTION ArcFSLoadObjects() AS BOOL
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET rootSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 8))
    LET expectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 16))

    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.5: reset the mount-time slot-ownership table before populating any row
    ' this call -- ArcFSPopulateObjectRow consults/updates it to restore real cross-OID dataSlot
    ' sharing (see that function's own header note).
    LET ownerReset AS U64 = 0
    WHILE ownerReset < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountSlotOwnerAddress(), ownerReset * 8), ArcFSMaxObjects())
        ownerReset = ownerReset + 1
    WEND

    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(rootSector, ArcFSTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress())
    IF collectedCount <> expectedCount THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeSortedAddress()
    LET index AS U64 = 0
    WHILE index < collectedCount
        LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, index * 40)
        LET oid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(src)
        LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 8))
        LET size AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 16))
        LET committedDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 24))
        LET oidHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 32))
        IF ArcFSPopulateObjectRow(index, oid, objType, size, committedDataSlot, oidHigh) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        index = index + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase: replaces the old flat `namespaceRoot + index` mount
' loop with a real tree walk, exactly mirroring ArcFSLoadObjects' own Phase J pattern. `namespaceRoot
' = 0` (namespaceCount = 0) is the legitimate "no entries at all" case -- see this section's own
' header note above -- and is handled before ever touching `namespaceRoot` as a sector.
FUNCTION ArcFSLoadNamespace() AS BOOL
    LET namespaceCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 32))
    IF namespaceCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET namespaceRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 24))

    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(namespaceRoot, ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress())
    IF collectedCount <> namespaceCount THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress()
    LET index AS U64 = 0
    WHILE index < collectedCount
        LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, index * 56)
        LET parentOID AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(src)
        LET nameLength AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 40))
        LET childOID AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 48))
        IF ArcFSPopulateNamespaceRow(index, parentOID, ADDRESS.Offset(src, 8), nameLength, childOID) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        index = index + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7.1: loads the active checkpoint's own Allocation Bitmap into the in-memory
' mirror, checksum-verified like every other tree this checkpoint references. Payload is
' ArcFSMaxTrackedSectors() (508) raw bytes; the checksum covers exactly that range and lives in
' the last 4 bytes of the same 512-byte sector.
' RFC-0043 Phase U: reads ArcFSBitmapSectorCount() CONSECUTIVE sectors starting at the checkpoint's
' own bitmapSector field, each one independently self-checksummed (508 payload bytes + a 4-byte
' CRC-32C checksum -- the exact same per-sector shape the old single-sector bitmap always used,
' simply repeated once per sector instead of covering the whole span with one checksum). A
' checksum failure on ANY sector fails the whole load closed, matching every other tree/record read
' in this file's own "any corruption anywhere means don't trust any of it" discipline.
FUNCTION ArcFSLoadBitmap() AS BOOL
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET bitmapBaseSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 96))
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    LET bitmapAddr AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAllocationBitmapAddress()
    LET sectorCount AS U64 = ArcFSBitmapSectorCount()
    LET s AS U64 = 0
    WHILE s < sectorCount
        IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(bitmapBaseSector + s, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 508))
        LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 508)
        IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        LET destBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(bitmapAddr, s * 508)
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < 508
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, i), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, i)))
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        s = s + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0043 Phase U: serializes the CURRENT in-memory Allocation Bitmap into ArcFSBitmapSectorCount()
' consecutive real sectors starting at `baseSector`, writing each directly (the write-side
' counterpart of ArcFSLoadBitmap above) -- called by ArcFS.PrepareCommit/ArcFS.FormatVolume once
' every allocation this commit needed (including the bitmap's own new span and the new
' checkpoint's) has already been marked in the in-memory copy, so every serialized sector is fully
' self-consistent the moment it is written. `baseSector` replaces the old implicit "always sector
' 4" assumption -- FormatVolume still passes 4 (the bitmap's own fixed starting sector, unchanged
' by this phase), but ArcFS.PrepareCommit passes a freshly bump-allocated span each commit, exactly
' like the single-sector version already did for its own one sector.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteBitmapRecord(scratch AS MMIOPTR, baseSector AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET bitmapAddr AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAllocationBitmapAddress()
    LET sectorCount AS U64 = ArcFSBitmapSectorCount()
    LET s AS U64 = 0
    WHILE s < sectorCount
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < 512
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, i), 0)
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        LET srcBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(bitmapAddr, s * 508)
        i = 0
        WHILE i < 508
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, i), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(srcBase, i)))
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 508) AND 4294967295
        MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 508), checksum)
        IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(baseSector + s, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        s = s + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Requirement (RFC-0039 Section 10's ACTIVATE step, made concrete): resets Phase A's in-memory
' tables, then loads a real BlockDevice-backed image into them. Fails closed -- returns FALSE,
' leaving whatever Initialize() alone produced (an empty tree with just the root directory) -- on
' any magic mismatch, checksum failure, or over-capacity table, exactly like RFC-0038's FAT32.Mount
' before it.
FUNCTION ArcFS.MountImage() AS BOOL
    ArcFS.Initialize()
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 48), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 88), 0)
    ' RFC-0042 Section 7.2: every mount attempt starts from a clean ReadOnlySafety slate -- a real
    ' bug this increment's own fixture caught on its first attempt: an unrecognized ro-compat bit
    ' from an EARLIER mount left the flag armed forever afterward, since nothing in this function
    ' ever cleared it (only ArcFS.MountImageSafe did, defensively, before calling this). A later,
    ' genuinely clean remount (flags restored to 0) still reported read-only and refused real
    ' commits -- found and fixed before shipping, not a latent gap left for later.
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress(), 0)

    IF ArcFSReadSuperblockRing() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    ' RFC-0042 Section 7.2: refuse outright on an unrecognized INCOMPAT bit; arm ReadOnlySafety
    ' (the same flag RFC-0039 Phase F's own repair-gate mechanism already established) on an
    ' unrecognized RO-COMPAT bit with no unrecognized incompat bit; otherwise mount normally --
    ' including the all-zero-flags case, every volume this build has ever formatted, unchanged.
    LET featureCheck AS U64 = ArcFSCheckFeatureFlags(MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSLastSelectedFeatureFlagsAddress()))
    IF featureCheck = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF featureCheck = 2 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress(), 1)
    END IF
    IF ArcFSReadCheckpoint() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSLoadBitmap() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSLoadObjects() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    ' Phase D: every loaded object claimed data-pool slots 0..objectCount-1 (ArcFSLoadOneObject's
    ' own index-as-slot convention); bump the allocator past all of them here, BEFORE
    ' ArcFSLoadAttributes runs below. RFC-0042 Section 12 finding: ArcFSPopulateAttributeRow now
    ' calls ArcFSAllocateDataSlot() (via ArcFSExtentTreeLoadDataSlotIntoRow's own fresh-live-slot
    ' caller) for a String/Blob-typed row, and object-row indices and attribute-row indices are
    ' NOT the same domain (ArcFSMaxAttributes() > ArcFSMaxObjects()) -- so an attribute row can no
    ' longer safely reuse its own row index as a live slot identity the way a first-mount FILE row
    ' does. This bump used to happen only once, at the very end of this function (see the second,
    ' now-removed copy of this same line below the old ArcFSLoadAttributes call) -- moved here,
    ' one call earlier, so a genuinely fresh ArcFSAllocateDataSlot() call inside ArcFSLoadAttributes
    ' can never collide with a slot an already-loaded object row still owns.
    LET loadedObjectCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 16))
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress(), loadedObjectCount)
    IF ArcFSLoadNamespace() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    ' RFC-0040 Section 12.3: loads AFTER objects, since resolving an attribute entry's owning row
    ' needs the object table already populated (ArcFSPopulateAttributeRow's own header note).
    IF ArcFSLoadAttributes() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    ' RFC-0040 Section 10: resume allocation past the highest 128-bit OID this generation
    ' actually contains, not just its low half -- the same carry-on-wrap arithmetic
    ' ArcFSAllocateOid() itself uses, so a volume mounted with a genuinely nonzero high-water
    ' mark resumes allocating correctly instead of silently wrapping the low half back to a
    ' value already in use.
    LET maxOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 48))
    LET maxOidHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 88))
    LET nextLow AS U64 = maxOid + 1
    LET nextHigh AS U64 = maxOidHigh
    ' Mirrors ArcFSAllocateOid()'s own carry-and-skip-past-zero rule exactly (see that function's
    ' own header note for why 0 is reserved) -- a volume whose true highest OID happens to be
    ' 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (low) must resume allocation the same way a live wrap would, not at the
    ' reserved 0 sentinel. Found by this increment's own round-trip fixture, not by inspection:
    ' this path computes "one past the max seen" independently of ArcFSAllocateOid's own
    ' increment, so the two had drifted out of sync until this fix.
    IF nextLow = 0 THEN
        nextHigh = maxOidHigh + 1
        nextLow = 1
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextOidAddress(), nextLow)
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextOidHighAddress(), nextHigh)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' Phase C: formatter and transactional writer (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase C -- "allocation
' tree; copy-on-write metadata; commit protocol; durable checkpoint publication; crash-injection
' tests").
' ==========================================================================================
'
' Two capabilities, both new:
'
' 1. ArcFS.FormatVolume() -- RFC-0039 Section 40's Formatting, made concrete: writes a brand-new
'    generation-1 volume (superblock + checkpoint + a lone root-directory object record) to a
'    BlockDevice that Phase B's ArcFS.MountImage can then read back.
'
' 2. ArcFS.PrepareCommit() / ArcFS.PublishCommit() / ArcFS.CommitImage() -- RFC-0039 Section
'    15.2's commit protocol, made concrete: takes whatever Phase A's in-memory tables currently
'    hold (mutated by ordinary CreateFile/CreateDirectory/Rename/Remove/HandleWrite calls, exactly
'    as before -- Phase C changes nothing about how the tree is edited, only how it becomes
'    durable) and writes it to the BlockDevice as a brand-new generation, in the copy-on-write
'    sense RFC-0039 Section 5.2 requires: EVERY object/namespace/data record this commit needs is
'    written to a FRESH sector the previous committed generation never used, and the previously
'    committed checkpoint's own sectors are never modified in place. The single point where the
'    new generation becomes authoritative is one single-sector write: the superblock's
'    checkpointSector field. Splitting "prepare" (everything except that one write) from
'    "publish" (exactly that one write) is what makes the crash-injection tests below possible
'    without needing to simulate a real power cut: calling PrepareCommit and then simply not
'    calling PublishCommit IS the crash -- the old generation remains the only one any subsequent
'    ArcFS.MountImage() call can ever see, because nothing on disk points at the new one yet.
'
' Documented Phase C scope reductions, stated up front, in the same spirit as every prior phase's
' own header note:
'
' 1. "Allocation tree" here is a conservative monotonic bump allocator (ArcFSMountStateAddress
'    offset 64, ratcheted forward by every ReadCheckpoint/PrepareCommit call), not a real
'    allocation tree with free-space reclamation. It never reuses a sector, so it trivially
'    satisfies RFC-0039 Section 29's "no committed generation may describe the same physical
'    block as simultaneously allocated to incompatible owners" -- correct, but wasteful: an old
'    generation's now-unreachable sectors are never reclaimed. RFC-0039 Section 78's own Phase E
'    ("Snapshots and reclamation... safe reclamation") is explicitly where that belongs; this
'    phase's job is proving the commit protocol is atomic, not proving space efficiency.
' 2. "Flush" is a no-op. RFC-0038's RAMDisk.WriteSectors is an ordinary synchronous memory copy --
'    there is no volatile device write-back cache to order against in this milestone's model, so
'    RFC-0039 Section 15.2's "ensure durability boundary reached" / "flush/order the checkpoint
'    write" steps collapse to "the write already happened." A real block device's distinct
'    completion-to-cache vs. durable-completion semantics (RFC-0039 Section 34) is RFC-0038's own
'    future work, not something this phase can prove against a RAM disk.
' 3. Object/namespace/data extent writes are NOT individually copy-on-write against each other
'    within one commit (there is no intra-transaction rollback) -- if PrepareCommit fails partway
'    through (e.g. a RAMDisk.WriteSectors call returns FALSE because the device is too small), the
'    already-written sectors for that attempt are simply abandoned as unreachable garbage, exactly
'    like an aborted transaction per RFC-0039 Section 15.3 -- they were never reachable from any
'    checkpoint (the previous generation's checkpoint is untouched, and the new one is never
'    written), so this is correct, not merely convenient.
' 4. No multi-writer/multi-transaction concurrency (RFC-0039 Section 32) -- there is exactly one
'    in-memory tree and one BlockDevice, matching every prior phase's "one instance" pattern.

' ArcFSWriteNamespaceRecord (the old flat per-record writer, one namespace record per sector) was
' REMOVED this phase, genuinely superseded by ArcFSWriteOneNamespaceLeafNode -- matching Phase J's
' own precedent (that phase's own report records removing ArcFSWriteObjectRecord/ArcFSLoadOneObject
' the same way) of deleting truly-replaced functions rather than leaving dead code behind.

' RFC-0040 Section 8.3: gained `previousCheckpointSector` -- the sector of the checkpoint
' committed immediately before this one (0 for generation 1, which has no predecessor), forming
' a backward-linked chain of checkpoints independent of any snapshot. Record grew from 48 to 56
' payload bytes; the checksum (still CRC-32C, RFC-0040 Section 8.4) shifted from offset 48 to
' offset 56 to make room for it. Every EXISTING field (generation..dataRegionStart) keeps its
' original offset, so every reader that only cared about those fields needed no further change.
' RFC-0040 Section 7.1: gained `allocationBitmapSector` -- the sector holding this checkpoint's
' OWN version of the Allocation Bitmap, copy-on-write like every other tree this checkpoint
' references -- and `highWaterMark`, the EXACT next-never-used-sector cursor at commit time.
' Phase C's own reader used to re-derive a conservative overestimate of this from
' `dataRegionStart + objectCount*8`, which only worked because every FILE's data extent was
' guaranteed contiguous immediately after dataRegionStart. Real reuse (this phase's whole point)
' breaks that guarantee -- an extent can now legitimately sit BELOW dataRegionStart, reused from
' reclaimed space -- so the exact value must be stored durably, not approximated. Payload grew
' from 56 to 72 bytes; the checksum shifted from offset 56 to offset 72 to make room. Every
' EXISTING field keeps its original offset.
' RFC-0040 Section 12.1: gained `attributeTreeRoot`/`attributeTreeCount` -- the Attribute Tree's
' own root sector and entry count, the exact two fields RFC-0039 Section 15.1 already named as
' required and this reference implementation had always omitted. Payload grew from 72 to 88
' bytes; the checksum shifted from offset 72 to offset 88 to make room -- the fourth time this
' record's checksum has moved as fields grew (56->64->72->88), the same "checksum always covers
' everything before it, and moves with it" convention every prior growth already used. Every
' EXISTING field keeps its original offset.
' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: gained `extentTreeRoot`/`extentTreeCount` -- the Extent Tree's own root
' sector and total leaf-entry count, the same shape every prior tree-growth already used. Payload
' grew from 88 to 104 bytes; the checksum shifted from offset 88 to offset 104 to make room -- the
' fifth time this record's checksum has moved as fields grew (56->64->72->88->104), the same
' "checksum always covers everything before it, and moves with it" convention every prior growth
' already used. Every EXISTING field keeps its original offset.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord(scratch AS MMIOPTR, generation AS U64, objectRoot AS U64, objectCount AS U64, namespaceRoot AS U64, namespaceCount AS U64, dataRegionStart AS U64, previousCheckpointSector AS U64, allocationBitmapSector AS U64, highWaterMark AS U64, attributeTreeRoot AS U64, attributeTreeCount AS U64, extentTreeRoot AS U64, extentTreeCount AS U64) AS U64
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < 512
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(scratch, generation)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8), objectRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 16), objectCount)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 24), namespaceRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32), namespaceCount)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 40), dataRegionStart)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 48), previousCheckpointSector)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 56), allocationBitmapSector)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 64), highWaterMark)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 72), attributeTreeRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 80), attributeTreeCount)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 88), extentTreeRoot)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 96), extentTreeCount)
    ' RFC-0042 Section 8.2: every checkpoint this build writes is now self-describing --
    ' recordLength (currently always 112: the 104 legacy bytes plus this field itself, since no
    ' field beyond it exists yet) appended right where the checksum used to sit, checksum moved to
    ' immediately after it. See ArcFSVerifyCheckpointChecksum's own header note for why this can't
    ' simply move to offset 0, and why a reader can still tell this apart from every checkpoint any
    ' build before this RFC ever wrote.
    LET recordLength AS U64 = 112
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 104), recordLength)
    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, recordLength) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, recordLength), checksum)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 15.1: magic bumped to "ARCFSB02" (FMV2). RFC-0040 Section 8.4: gained a
' checksum-algorithm-identifier field at offset 36 (the 4 bytes RFC-0039's own superblock layout
' already left unused between logicalBlockSize and totalBlocks -- no existing field moved) so a
' future format revision can change the algorithm without breaking this one's readers; 1 means
' CRC-32C. The checksum itself stays at offset 64 covering the same first-64-bytes range as
' before -- only which algorithm computes it changed.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteSuperblockRecord(scratch AS MMIOPTR, checkpointSector AS U64, totalBlocks AS U64) AS U64
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < 512
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 0), 65)    ' 'A'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 1), 82)    ' 'R'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 2), 67)    ' 'C'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 3), 70)    ' 'F'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 4), 83)    ' 'S'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 5), 66)    ' 'B'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 6), 48)    ' '0'
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 7), 50)    ' '2' -- FMV2, was '1' (FMV1)
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8), 2)    ' format major -- FMV2, was 1 (FMV1)
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 12), 0)   ' format minor
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 16), 42)  ' volume UUID placeholder, high half
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 24), 1)   ' volume UUID placeholder, low half
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32), 512)
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 36), 1)   ' checksum-algorithm-identifier: 1 = CRC-32C
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 40), totalBlocks)
    ' RFC-0043 Phase U: the first real, nonzero value this field has ever held -- every volume this
    ' code formats now genuinely uses a multi-sector Allocation Bitmap, so the incompat bit that
    ' says so is set unconditionally, not gated behind a caller choice.
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 48), ArcFSFeatureMultiSectorBitmap())
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 56), checkpointSector)
    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 64) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 64), checksum)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7.2: consults the Allocation Bitmap for a free 8-sector run BELOW the current
' high-water mark before ever growing past it -- real reuse, not just bookkeeping. Every FILE
' still owns exactly one fixed 8-sector extent (Phase A's own scope reduction, unchanged this
' phase -- variable-length/sparse extents are RFC-0040 Section 11, Phase K), so "find 8
' consecutive free sectors" is the whole search. Falls back to growing the SHARED high-water mark
' (mount state offset 64) only when no existing free run is large enough -- the same boundary
' metadata placement in ArcFS.PrepareCommit already advances past its own span first, so the two
' mechanisms can never allocate the same sector.
FUNCTION ArcFSAllocateDataExtent() AS U64
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET highWaterMark AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64))
    LET candidate AS U64 = 4
    WHILE candidate + 8 <= highWaterMark
        LET runIsFree AS U64 = 1
        LET offset AS U64 = 0
        WHILE offset < 8
            IF ArcFSBitmapGet(candidate + offset) <> 0 THEN
                runIsFree = 0
            END IF
            offset = offset + 1
        WEND
        IF runIsFree = 1 THEN
            offset = 0
            WHILE offset < 8
                ArcFSBitmapSet(candidate + offset, 1)
                offset = offset + 1
            WEND
            RETURN candidate
        END IF
        candidate = candidate + 1
    WEND

    LET newExtent AS U64 = highWaterMark
    LET offset2 AS U64 = 0
    WHILE offset2 < 8
        ArcFSBitmapSet(newExtent + offset2, 1)
        offset2 = offset2 + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64), highWaterMark + 8)
    RETURN newExtent
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0040 Section 9: growable Object Tree, a real copy-on-write B+tree replacing the flat
' `objectRoot + index` record array every prior phase used. Scoped to the Object Tree only --
' the Namespace Tree stays a flat array this increment; see .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-phase-j.md
' for why that split is correct, not a shortcut.
'
' This implementation BULK-BUILDS a fresh, minimally-shaped tree from the complete current key
' set every commit, rather than incrementally inserting into and rebalancing an existing on-disk
' tree. That is a deliberate departure from RFC-0040 Section 9.3's literal "split on insert into a
' full node" framing, not an oversight: this whole reference implementation already rewrites every
' other tree/record fresh every commit (RFC-0039 Section 5.2's copy-on-write discipline, true
' since Phase C), so there is no "existing on-disk node" to incrementally mutate in the first
' place. A bulk-built tree is byte-for-byte indistinguishable in FORMAT from an incrementally-
' built one (same node types, same self-describing headers, same checksums, same routing shape --
' RFC-0040 Section 9.1's on-disk contract is fully satisfied); only the algorithm that PRODUCES a
' new generation's tree differs. This also makes merge/rebalance moot by construction: there is no
' "delete leaving a node under-occupied" scenario to repair, because every rebuild is already
' minimally and correctly packed from the live key set.
'
' Node occupancy (ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity, 4) is deliberately small so a handful of real
' objects -- well under ArcFSMaxObjects()'s own 64-row ceiling -- provably produces a real
' multi-node, multi-level tree under QEMU. Internal nodes store real, correct routing keys (key[i]
' = the smallest key reachable through child[i]) even though this phase's own readers only ever
' walk every child rather than descending via key comparison -- see this file's own report for why
' full key-guided search is a named, deferred optimization, not a correctness gap.
' ==========================================================================================

' RFC-0040 Section 9: a tree node and a file's data extent are the identical size (8 sectors,
' 4096 bytes) -- the SAME real allocator Phase I built for data extents, reused unchanged rather
' than duplicated, since "find 8 free consecutive sectors, or grow" is exactly what a node needs
' too.
FUNCTION ArcFSAllocateTreeNode() AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSAllocateDataExtent()
END FUNCTION

' Copies every currently active row from the existing fixed in-memory Object Table (UNCHANGED
' this phase -- every ordinary ArcFS.CreateFile/Reflink/etc. call still mutates this same table
' exactly as it always has) into ArcFSTreeSortedAddress(), 40 bytes each: [oid][type][size]
' [dataSlot][oidHigh] (the row's own field layout, not yet the on-disk record's -- extentSector is
' still unknown at this point; it is only assigned when a leaf is actually written). Then sorts
' that copy ascending by oid (selection sort, at most ArcFSMaxObjects()=64 entries, correctness
' over speed matching every other small-table pass in this file). Returns the active count.
FUNCTION ArcFSGatherSortedActiveObjects() AS U64
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeSortedAddress()
    LET count AS U64 = 0
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
        LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
        IF objType <> 0 THEN
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, count * 40)
            MEMORY.Write64(dst, MEMORY.Read64(base))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 8), objType)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 16), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 24), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 32), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 32)))
            count = count + 1
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET minIndex AS U64 = i
        LET j AS U64 = i + 1
        WHILE j < count
            LET candidateOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, j * 40))
            LET minOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 40))
            IF candidateOid < minOid THEN
                minIndex = j
            END IF
            j = j + 1
        WEND
        IF minIndex <> i THEN
            LET a AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, i * 40)
            LET b AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 40)
            LET k AS U64 = 0
            WHILE k < 40
                LET av AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(a, k))
                LET bv AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(b, k))
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(a, k), bv)
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(b, k), av)
                k = k + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN count
END FUNCTION

' Builds one leaf node from `count` consecutive entries in the sorted working copy starting at
' `sortedOffset`. Returns the node's own sector, or 0 on failure (sector 0 is the superblock ring,
' never a legitimate node sector, the same "0 is impossible, so 0 is failure" convention this
' codebase already uses for OIDs).
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteOneLeafNode(sortedOffset AS U64, count AS U64) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    LET nodeSize AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeSizeBytes()
    LET z AS U64 = 0
    WHILE z < nodeSize
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, z), 0)
        z = z + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(node, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8), count)

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeSortedAddress()
    LET entryIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE entryIndex < count
        LET srcOffset AS U64 = (sortedOffset + entryIndex) * 40
        LET oid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset))
        LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + 8))
        LET size AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + 16))
        LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + 24))
        LET oidHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + 32))

        ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: this field now stores the object's own dataSlot directly, not an
        ' extent-list sector -- the real chunk extents themselves were already allocated and
        ' written by ArcFSBuildExtentTree (called BEFORE ArcFSBuildObjectTree in
        ' ArcFS.PrepareCommit's own sequencing), keyed by dataSlot so a reflinked-but-undiverged
        ' pair's shared slot contributes real extents exactly once, not once per OID. A
        ' non-FILE object (objType <> 1) has no chunks and simply carries dataSlot's raw value
        ' through unused, matching the old field's own "0 for directories" behavior exactly.

        LET dstOffset AS U64 = 24 + entryIndex * 40
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset), oid)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + 8), objType)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + 16), size)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + 24), dataSlot)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + 32), oidHigh)
        entryIndex = entryIndex + 1
    WEND

    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092), checksum)

    LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateTreeNode()
    IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    RETURN nodeSector
END FUNCTION

' Builds one internal node from `count` consecutive (key, childSector) pairs in `levelSource`
' starting at `pairOffset`. Real routing keys, real child pointers -- see this section's own
' header note on why this phase's own readers don't yet need them for key-guided descent.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode(levelSource AS MMIOPTR, pairOffset AS U64, count AS U64) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    LET nodeSize AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeSizeBytes()
    LET z AS U64 = 0
    WHILE z < nodeSize
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, z), 0)
        z = z + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(node, 2)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8), count)

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET entryIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE entryIndex < count
        LET srcOffset AS U64 = (pairOffset + entryIndex) * 16
        LET key AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelSource, srcOffset))
        LET childSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelSource, srcOffset + 8))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + entryIndex * 8), key)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8 + entryIndex * 8), childSector)
        entryIndex = entryIndex + 1
    WEND

    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092), checksum)

    LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateTreeNode()
    IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    RETURN nodeSector
END FUNCTION

' Builds a complete Object Tree from the CURRENT active in-memory rows and returns its root
' node's sector (0 on failure). Bottom-up bulk load: partition the sorted key set into leaves,
' then repeatedly partition the level below into the level above, until exactly one node remains.
FUNCTION ArcFSBuildObjectTree() AS U64
    LET totalCount AS U64 = ArcFSGatherSortedActiveObjects()
    IF totalCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()

    LET levelA AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeLevelAAddress()
    LET levelB AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeLevelBAddress()

    LET levelCount AS U64 = 0
    LET offset AS U64 = 0
    WHILE offset < totalCount
        LET groupSize AS U64 = capacity
        IF offset + groupSize > totalCount THEN
            groupSize = totalCount - offset
        END IF
        LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneLeafNode(offset, groupSize)
        IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
        LET firstKey AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSTreeSortedAddress(), offset * 40))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16), firstKey)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector)
        levelCount = levelCount + 1
        offset = offset + groupSize
    WEND

    LET useAAsCurrent AS BOOL = 1
    WHILE levelCount > 1
        LET currentLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
        LET nextLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelB
        IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
            currentLevel = levelB
            nextLevel = levelA
        END IF

        LET nextLevelCount AS U64 = 0
        LET pairOffset AS U64 = 0
        WHILE pairOffset < levelCount
            LET groupSize2 AS U64 = capacity
            IF pairOffset + groupSize2 > levelCount THEN
                groupSize2 = levelCount - pairOffset
            END IF
            LET nodeSector2 AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode(currentLevel, pairOffset, groupSize2)
            IF nodeSector2 = 0 THEN RETURN 0
            LET firstKey2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(currentLevel, pairOffset * 16))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16), firstKey2)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector2)
            nextLevelCount = nextLevelCount + 1
            pairOffset = pairOffset + groupSize2
        WEND

        levelCount = nextLevelCount
        IF useAAsCurrent = 1 THEN
            useAAsCurrent = 0
        ELSE
            useAAsCurrent = 1
        END IF
    WEND

    LET finalLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
    IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
        finalLevel = levelB
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(finalLevel, 8))
END FUNCTION

' Recursively walks the tree rooted at `nodeSector`, appending every leaf entry (40 bytes each,
' the same [oid][type][size][extentSector][oidHigh] shape the old flat on-disk record used) into
' `destBase`, tracked via the shared counter at `destCountAddress`. Every node's own checksum is
' verified before its contents are trusted, exactly like every other on-disk record in this file.
' Internal nodes are handled by reading every child sector into an ORDINARY LOCAL variable --
' which lives on THIS call's own stack frame -- before any recursive call touches the shared node
' scratch buffer again; reading a child lazily from the shared buffer after a sibling's recursion
' has already overwritten it with different content would be a real aliasing bug. The four
' unrolled child reads below correspond exactly to ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()'s current value
' (4) and would need updating together if that constant ever changed.
FUNCTION ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            IF destCount >= ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
                RETURN 0
            END IF
            LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 40)
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 40)
            LET b AS U64 = 0
            WHILE b < 40
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b)))
                b = b + 1
            WEND
            MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child0, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child1, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child2, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child3, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7 (reachability), extended by Section 9: a generation's own tree NODES
' (root, internal, leaf -- 8 sectors each) are now also potentially reused/scattered sectors, the
' same reason Phase I had to widen reachability past a simple contiguous range for DATA extents.
' Left unfixed, a live tree node could be reported unreachable and freed out from under the
' generation still using it -- silent corruption, not a crash. This answers "does `targetSector`
' belong to the tree rooted at `nodeSector`" directly: true if it falls within the CURRENT node's
' own 8-sector span, true if it's a leaf entry's own dataSlot has a real extent (via the SEPARATE
' Extent Tree rooted at `extentTreeRoot`, RFC-0040 Section 11.1 -- threaded through every
' recursive call since a peeked, non-live generation's own Extent Tree root is not necessarily the
' currently-mounted one), otherwise recurse into children (same local-variable child-caching
' discipline as ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto, for the same aliasing reason).
FUNCTION ArcFSTreeContainsSector(nodeSector AS U64, extentTreeRoot AS U64, targetSector AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF targetSector >= nodeSector THEN
        IF targetSector < nodeSector + 8 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    END IF

    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 40)
            LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 8))
            IF objType = 1 THEN
                LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 24))
                IF ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot(extentTreeRoot, dataSlot, targetSector) = 1 THEN
                    RETURN 1
                END IF
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeContainsSector(child0, extentTreeRoot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeContainsSector(child1, extentTreeRoot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeContainsSector(child2, extentTreeRoot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSTreeContainsSector(child3, extentTreeRoot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 39/40's own defect-tolerant scrub needs a DIFFERENT walk than
' ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto: that one fails the whole walk closed on the first checksum
' mismatch (correct for mounting, where an untrustworthy tree must not be trusted at all), but a
' scrubber's entire job is to keep counting defects and finish scanning everything it still can.
' This variant does that: a corrupt LEAF still contributes zero entries but is otherwise harmless
' to its siblings; a corrupt INTERNAL/ROOT node is a real, structurally different failure mode
' from the old flat array's -- its own checksum failing means every OID reachable only through it
' is genuinely unscannable this pass (there is no way to know how many children it had or where),
' so that whole subtree is skipped and counted as exactly one defect, not silently treated as
' empty. Returns the number of defects found in this node's own subtree.
FUNCTION ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            IF destCount < ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
                LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 40)
                LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 40)
                LET b AS U64 = 0
                WHILE b < 40
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b)))
                    b = b + 1
                WEND
                MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    LET totalDefects AS U64 = 0
    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child0, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child1, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child2, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child3, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    RETURN totalDefects
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase: the Namespace Tree, replicating the Object Tree's own
' now-proven pattern (bulk-built fresh every commit, 4096-byte self-describing checksummed nodes,
' the SAME shared allocator and internal-node infrastructure) onto the second of the two tables
' Phase J deliberately left as a flat array. Attribute Tree remains flat -- see this section's own
' scope note below.
'
' A namespace leaf entry is 56 bytes: [parentOid U64][name 32 bytes][nameLength U64][childOid
' U64] -- the SAME shape ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress's own in-memory row already uses (minus the
' trailing `active` flag, which has no on-disk meaning), so ArcFSWriteName/ArcFSNameMatches both
' work unchanged against a tree entry exactly as they already do against a live row.
'
' Internal nodes are NOT duplicated for this tree -- ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode's own (key,
' childSector) pair layout is completely generic across any tree in this format (it was already
' written that way for the Object Tree, taking a raw `levelSource` pointer rather than anything
' object-specific), so this tree's own internal levels are built and walked by literally the SAME
' function, using ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity() (not ArcFSNamespaceTreeNodeCapacity()) for the
' fanout that function itself bakes into its fixed layout. Only the LEAF shape differs between the
' two trees, so only leaf-level build/walk functions are duplicated below.
'
' A real, load-bearing difference from the Object Tree that Phase J never had to handle: the
' Namespace Tree can be GENUINELY EMPTY (a freshly formatted volume has zero namespace entries --
' root itself has no namespace record, it IS the implicit root -- and removing every remaining
' entry returns to this same state), whereas the Object Tree never is (root is always at least one
' active object). Sector 0 is the superblock ring's own first sector, never a legitimate tree
' root, so `namespaceRoot = 0` is the explicit sentinel for "zero entries" throughout this file --
' every consumer below checks `namespaceCount = 0` (or the tree-build/gather count itself) BEFORE
' ever trying to interpret `namespaceRoot` as a real sector, the same "0 is impossible, so 0 is
' failure/empty" convention this codebase already uses for OIDs and tree/extent sectors alike.
' ==========================================================================================

' Copies every ACTIVE row of the in-memory namespace table into the tree-build scratch, sorted by
' (parentOid, childOid) -- an arbitrary but well-defined total order, not name-lexicographic
' (STRING has no comparison operations on this backend beyond byte-equality, RFC-0039 Section 5's
' own long-standing limitation), and not yet consulted for key-guided descent anyway (the same
' scope reduction the Object Tree's own routing keys already carry). Returns the count gathered --
' legitimately 0 on a namespace with no entries at all, unlike ArcFSGatherSortedActiveObjects
' (root always contributes at least one).
FUNCTION ArcFSGatherSortedActiveNamespaceEntries() AS U64
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress()
    LET count AS U64 = 0
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 56))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, count * 56)
            MEMORY.Write64(dst, MEMORY.Read64(base))
            LET k AS U64 = 0
            WHILE k < 32
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 8 + k), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8 + k)))
                k = k + 1
            WEND
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 40), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 48), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 48)))
            count = count + 1
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET minIndex AS U64 = i
        LET j2 AS U64 = i + 1
        WHILE j2 < count
            LET candParent AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, j2 * 56))
            LET candChild AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, j2 * 56 + 48))
            LET minParent AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 56))
            LET minChild AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 56 + 48))
            LET candIsLess AS BOOL = 0
            IF candParent < minParent THEN
                candIsLess = 1
            ELSE
                IF candParent = minParent THEN
                    IF candChild < minChild THEN
                        candIsLess = 1
                    END IF
                END IF
            END IF
            IF candIsLess = 1 THEN
                minIndex = j2
            END IF
            j2 = j2 + 1
        WEND
        IF minIndex <> i THEN
            LET a AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, i * 56)
            LET b AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 56)
            LET kk AS U64 = 0
            WHILE kk < 56
                LET av AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(a, kk))
                LET bv AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(b, kk))
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(a, kk), bv)
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(b, kk), av)
                kk = kk + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN count
END FUNCTION

' Builds one namespace leaf node from `count` consecutive 56-byte entries in the sorted working
' copy starting at `sortedOffset`. Returns the node's own sector, or 0 on failure.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteOneNamespaceLeafNode(sortedOffset AS U64, count AS U64) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    LET nodeSize AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeSizeBytes()
    LET z AS U64 = 0
    WHILE z < nodeSize
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, z), 0)
        z = z + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(node, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8), count)

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress()
    LET entryIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE entryIndex < count
        LET srcOffset AS U64 = (sortedOffset + entryIndex) * 56
        LET dstOffset AS U64 = 24 + entryIndex * 56
        LET b2 AS U64 = 0
        WHILE b2 < 56
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + b2), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + b2)))
            b2 = b2 + 1
        WEND
        entryIndex = entryIndex + 1
    WEND

    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092), checksum)

    LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateTreeNode()
    IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    RETURN nodeSector
END FUNCTION

' Builds a complete Namespace Tree from the CURRENT active in-memory rows and returns its root
' node's sector. Returns 0 if there are no active namespace rows at all -- the caller (ArcFS.
' PrepareCommit) MUST check ArcFSGatherSortedActiveNamespaceEntries' own count (or simply the
' active row count) itself and skip calling this entirely in that case, exactly like it must for
' ArcFSBuildAndWriteExtentList's own "0 is failure" convention elsewhere -- a genuinely empty
' namespace is not a failure here, so this function does not attempt to distinguish the two itself.
FUNCTION ArcFSBuildNamespaceTree() AS U64
    LET totalCount AS U64 = ArcFSGatherSortedActiveNamespaceEntries()
    IF totalCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSNamespaceTreeNodeCapacity()

    LET levelA AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeLevelAAddress()
    LET levelB AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeLevelBAddress()

    LET levelCount AS U64 = 0
    LET offset AS U64 = 0
    WHILE offset < totalCount
        LET groupSize AS U64 = capacity
        IF offset + groupSize > totalCount THEN
            groupSize = totalCount - offset
        END IF
        LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneNamespaceLeafNode(offset, groupSize)
        IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
        LET firstKey AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress(), offset * 56))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16), firstKey)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector)
        levelCount = levelCount + 1
        offset = offset + groupSize
    WEND

    ' Internal levels reuse ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode UNCHANGED -- see this section's own header
    ' note on why internal-node fanout is shared, generic infrastructure, not per-tree.
    LET internalCapacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET useAAsCurrent AS BOOL = 1
    WHILE levelCount > 1
        LET currentLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
        LET nextLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelB
        IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
            currentLevel = levelB
            nextLevel = levelA
        END IF

        LET nextLevelCount AS U64 = 0
        LET pairOffset AS U64 = 0
        WHILE pairOffset < levelCount
            LET groupSize2 AS U64 = internalCapacity
            IF pairOffset + groupSize2 > levelCount THEN
                groupSize2 = levelCount - pairOffset
            END IF
            LET nodeSector2 AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode(currentLevel, pairOffset, groupSize2)
            IF nodeSector2 = 0 THEN RETURN 0
            LET firstKey2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(currentLevel, pairOffset * 16))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16), firstKey2)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector2)
            nextLevelCount = nextLevelCount + 1
            pairOffset = pairOffset + groupSize2
        WEND

        levelCount = nextLevelCount
        IF useAAsCurrent = 1 THEN
            useAAsCurrent = 0
        ELSE
            useAAsCurrent = 1
        END IF
    WEND

    LET finalLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
    IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
        finalLevel = levelB
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(finalLevel, 8))
END FUNCTION

' Recursively walks the tree rooted at `nodeSector`, appending every 56-byte namespace leaf entry
' into `destBase`. Fails closed on the first checksum mismatch -- correct for mounting, matching
' ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto's own contract exactly, just for the namespace leaf shape.
FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            IF destCount >= ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
                RETURN 0
            END IF
            LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 56)
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 56)
            LET b3 AS U64 = 0
            WHILE b3 < 56
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b3), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b3)))
                b3 = b3 + 1
            WEND
            MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child0, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child1, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child2, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child3, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Defect-tolerant counterpart, exactly like ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant's own contract: a
' corrupt leaf contributes zero entries; a corrupt internal/root node makes its whole subtree
' genuinely unrecoverable this pass and is counted as exactly one defect, not silently empty.
FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            IF destCount < ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
                LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 56)
                LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 56)
                LET b4 AS U64 = 0
                WHILE b4 < 56
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b4), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b4)))
                    b4 = b4 + 1
                WEND
                MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    LET totalDefects AS U64 = 0
    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child0, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child1, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child2, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child3, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    RETURN totalDefects
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7 reachability, extended to the Namespace Tree exactly like
' ArcFSTreeContainsSector already extends it to the Object Tree: true if `targetSector` falls
' within the CURRENT node's own 8-sector span, otherwise recurse into children. No leaf-entry
' extent branch is needed here (unlike the Object Tree's own version) -- a namespace record names
' no additional sector beyond the node holding it.
FUNCTION ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector(nodeSector AS U64, targetSector AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF targetSector >= nodeSector THEN
        IF targetSector < nodeSector + 8 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    END IF

    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector(child0, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector(child1, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector(child2, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector(child3, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' Populates one in-memory namespace row from a walked tree entry -- the Namespace Tree's own
' counterpart to ArcFSPopulateObjectRow, called from ArcFSLoadNamespace below.
FUNCTION ArcFSPopulateNamespaceRow(index AS U64, parentOid AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64, childOid AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF nameLength > ArcFSMaxNameLength() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET row AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(index)
    MEMORY.Write64(row, parentOid)
    ArcFSWriteName(row, nameBuffer, nameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 40), nameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 48), childOid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(row, 56), 1)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0040 Section 12: the Attribute Tree, the third and final tree Section 9 originally named
' (Object and Namespace both real B+trees since Phase J; this closes the set). Same "prove the
' hardest case once, replicate" discipline as the Namespace Tree's own addendum -- a real on-disk
' B+tree, bulk-built fresh every commit, sharing the SAME internal-node infrastructure
' (ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode, ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()) both other trees already reuse.
'
' An attribute leaf entry is 40 bytes: [oidLow U64][oidHigh U64][type U64][valueLow
' U64][valueHigh U64] -- keyed by the owning object's real OID (both halves persisted, though
' sorting/comparison below uses only the low half, the same limitation
' ArcFSGatherSortedActiveObjects' own sort already carries -- see that function's own header for
' why this is a real, if currently unreachable, gap).
'
' Like the Namespace Tree, the Attribute Tree is very often GENUINELY EMPTY -- most objects have
' no attribute set at all, not just on a freshly formatted volume -- so `attributeTreeRoot = 0` is
' the same "0 is impossible, so 0 is the empty-tree sentinel" convention, checked via
' `attributeTreeCount = 0` before ever treating it as a sector, everywhere the checkpoint's
' attribute fields are consumed.
' ==========================================================================================

' Copies every ACTIVE attribute row into the tree-build scratch directly -- RFC-0040 Section 12.1
' made the Attribute Table independently (oid, attributeId)-indexed, no longer parallel to the
' object table (a single object can now have more than one active row), so this walks the
' Attribute Table's own `active` flags rather than cross-referencing an object-row index. Sorted by
' (OID low half, attributeId) so on-disk tree order stays deterministic and every key is unique
' even across an object's multiple attributes -- OID low half comparison matches
' ArcFSGatherSortedActiveObjects' own already-established limitation. Returns the count gathered --
' legitimately 0 whenever no attribute is currently set anywhere, the common case.
FUNCTION ArcFSGatherSortedActiveAttributes() AS U64
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress()
    LET count AS U64 = 0
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxAttributes()
        LET attrBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 24))
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, count * 48)
            MEMORY.Write64(dst, MEMORY.Read64(attrBase))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 8), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 8)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 16), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 16)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 24), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 32)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 32), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 40)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 40), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 48)))
            count = count + 1
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET minIndex AS U64 = i
        LET j2 AS U64 = i + 1
        WHILE j2 < count
            LET candOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, j2 * 48))
            LET candAttr AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, j2 * 48 + 16))
            LET minOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 48))
            LET minAttr AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 48 + 16))
            LET swap AS BOOL = 0
            IF candOid < minOid THEN
                swap = 1
            ELSE
                IF candOid = minOid THEN
                    IF candAttr < minAttr THEN
                        swap = 1
                    END IF
                END IF
            END IF
            IF swap <> 0 THEN
                minIndex = j2
            END IF
            j2 = j2 + 1
        WEND
        IF minIndex <> i THEN
            LET a AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, i * 48)
            LET b AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, minIndex * 48)
            LET k AS U64 = 0
            WHILE k < 48
                LET av AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(a, k))
                LET bv AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(b, k))
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(a, k), bv)
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(b, k), av)
                k = k + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN count
END FUNCTION

' Builds one attribute leaf node from `count` consecutive 48-byte entries in the sorted working
' copy starting at `sortedOffset`. Returns the node's own sector, or 0 on failure.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteOneAttributeLeafNode(sortedOffset AS U64, count AS U64) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    LET nodeSize AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeSizeBytes()
    LET z AS U64 = 0
    WHILE z < nodeSize
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, z), 0)
        z = z + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(node, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8), count)

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress()
    LET entryIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE entryIndex < count
        LET srcOffset AS U64 = (sortedOffset + entryIndex) * 48
        LET dstOffset AS U64 = 24 + entryIndex * 48
        LET b2 AS U64 = 0
        WHILE b2 < 48
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + b2), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + b2)))
            b2 = b2 + 1
        WEND
        entryIndex = entryIndex + 1
    WEND

    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092), checksum)

    LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateTreeNode()
    IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    RETURN nodeSector
END FUNCTION

' Builds a complete Attribute Tree from the CURRENT active in-memory attribute rows and returns
' its root node's sector. Returns 0 if there are no attributes set at all -- the caller (ArcFS.
' PrepareCommit) MUST check the active attribute count itself and skip calling this entirely in
' that case, exactly like it already does for ArcFSBuildNamespaceTree.
FUNCTION ArcFSBuildAttributeTree() AS U64
    LET totalCount AS U64 = ArcFSGatherSortedActiveAttributes()
    IF totalCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSAttributeTreeNodeCapacity()

    LET levelA AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeTreeLevelAAddress()
    LET levelB AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeTreeLevelBAddress()

    LET levelCount AS U64 = 0
    LET offset AS U64 = 0
    WHILE offset < totalCount
        LET groupSize AS U64 = capacity
        IF offset + groupSize > totalCount THEN
            groupSize = totalCount - offset
        END IF
        LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneAttributeLeafNode(offset, groupSize)
        IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
        LET firstKey AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress(), offset * 48))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16), firstKey)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector)
        levelCount = levelCount + 1
        offset = offset + groupSize
    WEND

    ' Internal levels reuse ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode UNCHANGED -- see this section's own header
    ' note (and the Namespace Tree's, which established the same reuse first) on why internal-node
    ' fanout is shared, generic infrastructure, not per-tree.
    LET internalCapacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET useAAsCurrent AS BOOL = 1
    WHILE levelCount > 1
        LET currentLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
        LET nextLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelB
        IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
            currentLevel = levelB
            nextLevel = levelA
        END IF

        LET nextLevelCount AS U64 = 0
        LET pairOffset AS U64 = 0
        WHILE pairOffset < levelCount
            LET groupSize2 AS U64 = internalCapacity
            IF pairOffset + groupSize2 > levelCount THEN
                groupSize2 = levelCount - pairOffset
            END IF
            LET nodeSector2 AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode(currentLevel, pairOffset, groupSize2)
            IF nodeSector2 = 0 THEN RETURN 0
            LET firstKey2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(currentLevel, pairOffset * 16))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16), firstKey2)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector2)
            nextLevelCount = nextLevelCount + 1
            pairOffset = pairOffset + groupSize2
        WEND

        levelCount = nextLevelCount
        IF useAAsCurrent = 1 THEN
            useAAsCurrent = 0
        ELSE
            useAAsCurrent = 1
        END IF
    WEND

    LET finalLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
    IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
        finalLevel = levelB
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(finalLevel, 8))
END FUNCTION

' Recursively walks the tree rooted at `nodeSector`, appending every 48-byte attribute leaf entry
' into `destBase`. Fails closed on the first checksum mismatch -- correct for mounting.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            IF destCount >= ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
                RETURN 0
            END IF
            LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 48)
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 48)
            LET b3 AS U64 = 0
            WHILE b3 < 48
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b3), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b3)))
                b3 = b3 + 1
            WEND
            MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child0, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child1, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child2, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child3, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Defect-tolerant counterpart, exactly like the Object/Namespace Tree's own: a corrupt leaf
' contributes zero entries; a corrupt internal/root node makes its whole subtree genuinely
' unrecoverable this pass and is counted as exactly one defect.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            IF destCount < ArcFSMaxAttributes() THEN
                LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 48)
                LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 48)
                LET b4 AS U64 = 0
                WHILE b4 < 48
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b4), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b4)))
                    b4 = b4 + 1
                WEND
                MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    LET totalDefects AS U64 = 0
    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child0, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child1, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child2, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child3, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    RETURN totalDefects
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7 reachability, extended to the Attribute Tree exactly like the Namespace
' Tree's own ContainsSector: true if `targetSector` falls within the CURRENT node's own 8-sector
' span, otherwise recurse into children. No leaf-entry extent branch needed -- an attribute record
' names no additional sector beyond the node holding it.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttributeTreeContainsSector(nodeSector AS U64, targetSector AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF targetSector >= nodeSector THEN
        IF targetSector < nodeSector + 8 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    END IF

    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeContainsSector(child0, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeContainsSector(child1, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeContainsSector(child2, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSAttributeTreeContainsSector(child3, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' Populates one in-memory attribute row from a walked tree entry, at `index` -- the tree walk's own
' sequential position, exactly matching how ArcFSPopulateObjectRow and the Namespace Tree's own
' populate function already use their own walk-sequential index as the destination row (RFC-0040
' Section 12.1's multi-attribute model retired the old "row = the owning object's row" convention,
' since one object can now own more than one row; ArcFSMaxAttributes() is always >= any real
' collectedCount, exactly like every other tree's own load loop already relies on). Still checks
' the owning object actually exists (ArcFSFindObjectRow, resolvable because attributes load AFTER
' objects in ArcFS.MountImage's own sequence) and fails closed if not -- unlike an empty tree, an
' attribute entry naming a genuinely nonexistent object is not a state this reference
' implementation's own write path can ever produce, so failing closed here is correct, matching
' this project's established discipline of never silently guessing.
FUNCTION ArcFSPopulateAttributeRow(index AS U64, oidLow AS U64, oidHigh AS U64, attributeId AS U64, attrType AS U64, valueLow AS U64, valueHigh AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF ArcFSFindObjectRow(oidLow) = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(index)
    MEMORY.Write64(base, oidLow)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8), oidHigh)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), attributeId)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 32), attrType)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40), valueLow)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 48), valueHigh)

    ' RFC-0042 Section 12: for a String/Blob-typed entry, `valueLow` as READ FROM DISK is the
    ' committed dataSlot, not yet a live one -- exactly the same committed-vs-live distinction
    ' ArcFSPopulateObjectRow already handles for a FILE row's own dataSlot. Unlike a file, an
    ' attribute-owned slot is NEVER reflinked (see ArcFSGatherUniqueDataSlots's own header note),
    ' so there is no owner-table sharing case to consider here. It also CANNOT reuse its own
    ' attribute-row index as the live slot identity the way a first-time file owner reuses its own
    ' object-row index (ArcFSPopulateObjectRow's `ELSE` branch) -- that trick only works for files
    ' because the object-row-index domain and the data-pool-slot domain are the SAME size
    ' (ArcFSMaxObjects()); the attribute-row-index domain is larger (ArcFSMaxAttributes()), so
    ' reusing it here would silently collide with (or overrun) real object-owned live slots. A
    ' genuinely fresh live slot is allocated instead, exactly like ArcFS.SetStringAttribute does at
    ' write time -- safe because ArcFS.MountImage now bumps ArcFSNextDataSlotAddress() past every
    ' object-claimed slot BEFORE calling ArcFSLoadAttributes (see that function's own header note).
    LET isVariable AS BOOL = 0
    IF attrType = ArcFSAttrTypeString() THEN
        isVariable = 1
    END IF
    IF attrType = ArcFSAttrTypeBlob() THEN
        isVariable = 1
    END IF
    IF isVariable = 1 THEN
        LET liveSlot AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateDataSlot()
        IF liveSlot = ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 120))
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeLoadDataSlotIntoRow(extentTreeRoot, valueLow, liveSlot) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 40), liveSlot)
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 12.3: replaces Phase D's own "never persisted" gap with a real tree walk,
' exactly mirroring ArcFSLoadNamespace's own pattern. attributeTreeCount = 0 (the common case,
' most objects have no attribute) is handled before ever treating attributeTreeRoot as a sector.
FUNCTION ArcFSLoadAttributes() AS BOOL
    LET attributeTreeCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 112))
    IF attributeTreeCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET attributeTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 104))

    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(attributeTreeRoot, ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress())
    IF collectedCount <> attributeTreeCount THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress()
    LET index AS U64 = 0
    WHILE index < collectedCount
        LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, index * 48)
        LET oidLow AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(src)
        LET oidHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 8))
        LET attributeId AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 16))
        LET attrType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 24))
        LET valueLow AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 32))
        LET valueHigh AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 40))
        IF ArcFSPopulateAttributeRow(index, oidLow, oidHigh, attributeId, attrType, valueLow, valueHigh) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        index = index + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0040 Section 11.1/11.5: a real, variable-count Extent Tree replacing Phase K's fixed
' 512-byte 4-slot extent LIST, and genuine on-disk reflink sharing as a direct structural
' consequence of how it is keyed. See .agents/reports/aps-arcfs-extent-tree-reflink.md for the
' full design and why this scope; this header note covers only the load-bearing decision.
'
' A FILE object's Object Tree leaf entry (previously `extentListSector`) now stores its own
' `dataSlot` directly -- the SAME identity (RFC-0039 Phase D) two reflinked-but-undiverged OIDs
' already share in memory. The Extent Tree itself is a SEPARATE B+tree (same node format as the
' Object/Namespace/Attribute Trees: self-describing, checksummed, bulk-rebuilt fresh every commit
' from the live in-memory state), whose leaf entries are keyed by (dataSlot, chunkIndex), not by
' OID. Building it ONCE PER UNIQUE dataSlot -- not once per object row -- is what makes on-disk
' reflink sharing real: two rows still sharing an undiverged slot contribute exactly one set of
' chunk extents, not two. ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot (unchanged, RFC-0040 Section 11.5's own text)
' already gives a diverging write a NEW dataSlot; the next commit's Extent Tree build then
' naturally treats it as a distinct key, producing its own separate extents -- "generalized to
' operate on Extent Tree entries instead of fixed data-pool slots" by construction, with zero
' changes needed inside ArcFSEnsurePrivateSlot itself.
'
' Deliberate deviation from Section 11.5's literal "Shared Extent Refcount" field: none is
' persisted. Reachability (ArcFSTreeContainsSector/ArcFS.IsSectorReachable) already answers
' "is this sector referenced by anything still reachable" via a live graph scan, and a
' dataSlot-keyed Extent Tree entry is reachable exactly when ANY object referencing that dataSlot
' is reachable -- correct by construction, with no separate counter that could drift out of sync
' with the graph it is supposed to describe. The same judgment RFC-0040 Section 14.1's own
' lower-OID-not-generation-number deviation already made for this codebase (see Phase M's report).
' ==========================================================================================

' Scans every active FILE object row for its dataSlot, deduplicating so a shared, undiverged slot
' (RFC-0039 Phase D reflink) appears exactly once -- the dedup step that makes on-disk reflink
' sharing real (see this section's own header note). Writes the unique values, ascending, into
' `dest` (up to ArcFSMaxObjects() U64 values) and returns how many.
FUNCTION ArcFSGatherUniqueDataSlots(dest AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    LET count AS U64 = 0
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
        LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
        IF objType = 1 THEN
            LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24))
            LET alreadyHave AS BOOL = 0
            LET k AS U64 = 0
            WHILE k < count
                IF MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, k * 8)) = dataSlot THEN
                    alreadyHave = 1
                END IF
                k = k + 1
            WEND
            IF alreadyHave = 0 THEN
                MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, count * 8), dataSlot)
                count = count + 1
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    ' RFC-0042 Section 12: a String/Blob-typed attribute row's own valueLow is ALSO a real
    ' data-pool slot index (see ArcFSSetVariableAttribute) -- gathered here too, so the Extent
    ' Tree persists its real chunk content exactly like a file's own dataSlot. An integer/boolean/
    ' timestamp/UUID-typed row's valueLow is a plain value, never a slot index, so it never reaches
    ' this branch. Attribute-owned slots are never reflinked (each SetStringAttribute/SetBlob-
    ' Attribute call allocates its own fresh slot), so the dedup check below never actually fires
    ' for one in practice -- run anyway, for the same reason every other pass in this function
    ' already runs it unconditionally: uniform, simple, and correct even if that ever changes.
    LET attrRow AS U64 = 0
    WHILE attrRow < ArcFSMaxAttributes()
        LET attrBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(attrRow)
        LET attrActive AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 24))
        IF attrActive <> 0 THEN
            LET attrType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 32))
            LET isVariable AS BOOL = 0
            IF attrType = ArcFSAttrTypeString() THEN
                isVariable = 1
            END IF
            IF attrType = ArcFSAttrTypeBlob() THEN
                isVariable = 1
            END IF
            IF isVariable = 1 THEN
                LET attrDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(attrBase, 40))
                LET attrAlreadyHave AS BOOL = 0
                LET ak AS U64 = 0
                WHILE ak < count
                    IF MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, ak * 8)) = attrDataSlot THEN
                        attrAlreadyHave = 1
                    END IF
                    ak = ak + 1
                WEND
                IF attrAlreadyHave = 0 THEN
                    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, count * 8), attrDataSlot)
                    count = count + 1
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        attrRow = attrRow + 1
    WEND

    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET minIndex AS U64 = i
        LET j AS U64 = i + 1
        WHILE j < count
            LET candidate AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, j * 8))
            LET minVal AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, minIndex * 8))
            IF candidate < minVal THEN
                minIndex = j
            END IF
            j = j + 1
        WEND
        IF minIndex <> i THEN
            LET a AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, i * 8))
            LET b AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, minIndex * 8))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, i * 8), b)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, minIndex * 8), a)
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN count
END FUNCTION

' A pure, disk-I/O-free count of how many (dataSlot, chunkIndex) entries
' ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries will produce -- never fails, matching
' ArcFSGatherSortedActiveObjects's own no-failure shape. Exists so ArcFSBuildExtentTree can tell
' "genuinely nothing to do" (this returns 0) apart from "gather/allocate/write failed partway"
' (this returns nonzero but the real gather below returns a DIFFERENT, smaller count) -- the same
' distinction every other Build*Tree function's own failure checks already preserve.
FUNCTION ArcFSCountTouchedChunks() AS U64
    LET uniqueSlots AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeUniqueSlotsAddress()
    LET uniqueCount AS U64 = ArcFSGatherUniqueDataSlots(uniqueSlots)
    LET total AS U64 = 0
    LET u AS U64 = 0
    WHILE u < uniqueCount
        LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(uniqueSlots, u * 8))
        LET c AS U64 = 0
        WHILE c < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
            IF ArcFSChunkTouchedGet(dataSlot, c) <> 0 THEN
                total = total + 1
            END IF
            c = c + 1
        WEND
        u = u + 1
    WEND
    RETURN total
END FUNCTION

' Writes real extents for exactly the chunks ArcFSChunkTouchedGet reports touched, ONCE PER UNIQUE
' dataSlot (see this section's own header note on why per-slot, not per-object, is the whole
' reflink-sharing mechanism) -- an untouched chunk stays a real sparse hole: no allocation, no
' write, no Extent Tree entry at all. Appends one (dataSlot, chunkIndex, extentSector, reserved)
' entry per real chunk, already in ascending (dataSlot, chunkIndex) order by construction (unique
' slots are gathered pre-sorted; chunks within a slot are visited ascending), so no separate sort
' step is needed before the bulk tree build below. Returns the total entry count -- callers that
' need to distinguish a genuine failure from "nothing to do" should compare against
' ArcFSCountTouchedChunks()'s own independent, I/O-free count first (see ArcFSBuildExtentTree).
FUNCTION ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries() AS U64
    LET uniqueSlots AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeUniqueSlotsAddress()
    LET uniqueCount AS U64 = ArcFSGatherUniqueDataSlots(uniqueSlots)
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress()
    LET slotSize AS U64 = ArcFSSlotSizeBytes()
    LET entryCount AS U64 = 0
    LET u AS U64 = 0
    WHILE u < uniqueCount
        LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(uniqueSlots, u * 8))
        LET slotBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), dataSlot * slotSize)
        LET c AS U64 = 0
        WHILE c < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
            IF ArcFSChunkTouchedGet(dataSlot, c) <> 0 THEN
                LET extentSector AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateDataExtent()
                IF extentSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
                LET chunkSrc AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(slotBase, c * ArcFSFileCapacityBytes())
                IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(extentSector, 8, chunkSrc) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
                LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, entryCount * 32)
                MEMORY.Write64(dst, dataSlot)
                MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 8), c)
                MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 16), extentSector)
                MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 24), 0)
                entryCount = entryCount + 1
            END IF
            c = c + 1
        WEND
        u = u + 1
    WEND
    RETURN entryCount
END FUNCTION

' Builds one Extent Tree leaf node from `count` consecutive, already-real entries starting at
' `sortedOffset` -- unlike the Object Tree's own leaf writer, no allocation happens here; every
' entry's extent was already allocated and written by ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries above.
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteOneExtentLeafNode(sortedOffset AS U64, count AS U64) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    LET nodeSize AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeSizeBytes()
    LET z AS U64 = 0
    WHILE z < nodeSize
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, z), 0)
        z = z + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(node, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8), count)

    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress()
    LET entryIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE entryIndex < count
        LET srcOffset AS U64 = (sortedOffset + entryIndex) * 32
        LET dstOffset AS U64 = 24 + entryIndex * 32
        LET b AS U64 = 0
        WHILE b < 32
            MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(node, dstOffset + b), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, srcOffset + b)))
            b = b + 1
        WEND
        entryIndex = entryIndex + 1
    WEND

    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092), checksum)

    LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateTreeNode()
    IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    RETURN nodeSector
END FUNCTION

' Builds a complete Extent Tree from the CURRENT active in-memory data-pool slots and returns its
' root node's sector (0 if no FILE currently has any real, touched chunk -- a legitimate "no
' extents at all" case, matching every other tree's own 0-is-the-empty-tree-sentinel convention).
' Same bottom-up bulk load as ArcFSBuildObjectTree/ArcFSBuildNamespaceTree/ArcFSBuildAttributeTree
' (see ArcFSBuildObjectTree's own header note for the full rationale); reuses
' ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode unchanged, exactly like every other tree in this file already does.
' The total entry count is recorded into ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress() as a side channel
' (ArcFSLastBuiltExtentTreeCount reads it back) -- the same "companion address" idiom
' ArcFSLastAllocatedOidHigh already established, needed because ArcFS.PrepareCommit needs the
' real count for the checkpoint record and calling ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries a SECOND time
' would double-allocate and double-write every chunk (ArcFSAllocateDataExtent's own
' reuse-before-growth search advances on every call, so a second gather would not even reproduce
' the same extent sectors the first one just wrote).
FUNCTION ArcFSLastBuiltExtentTreeCount() AS U64
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSBuildExtentTree() AS U64
    LET expectedCount AS U64 = ArcFSCountTouchedChunks()
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF expectedCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET totalCount AS U64 = ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries()
    IF totalCount <> expectedCount THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress(), totalCount)
    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSExtentTreeNodeCapacity()

    LET levelA AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeLevelAAddress()
    LET levelB AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeLevelBAddress()

    LET levelCount AS U64 = 0
    LET offset AS U64 = 0
    WHILE offset < totalCount
        LET groupSize AS U64 = capacity
        IF offset + groupSize > totalCount THEN
            groupSize = totalCount - offset
        END IF
        LET nodeSector AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneExtentLeafNode(offset, groupSize)
        IF nodeSector = 0 THEN RETURN 0
        LET firstKey AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress(), offset * 32))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16), firstKey)
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(levelA, levelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector)
        levelCount = levelCount + 1
        offset = offset + groupSize
    WEND

    LET useAAsCurrent AS BOOL = 1
    WHILE levelCount > 1
        LET currentLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
        LET nextLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelB
        IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
            currentLevel = levelB
            nextLevel = levelA
        END IF

        LET nextLevelCount AS U64 = 0
        LET pairOffset AS U64 = 0
        WHILE pairOffset < levelCount
            LET groupSize2 AS U64 = capacity
            IF pairOffset + groupSize2 > levelCount THEN
                groupSize2 = levelCount - pairOffset
            END IF
            LET nodeSector2 AS U64 = ArcFSWriteOneInternalNode(currentLevel, pairOffset, groupSize2)
            IF nodeSector2 = 0 THEN RETURN 0
            LET firstKey2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(currentLevel, pairOffset * 16))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16), firstKey2)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nextLevel, nextLevelCount * 16 + 8), nodeSector2)
            nextLevelCount = nextLevelCount + 1
            pairOffset = pairOffset + groupSize2
        WEND

        levelCount = nextLevelCount
        IF useAAsCurrent = 1 THEN
            useAAsCurrent = 0
        ELSE
            useAAsCurrent = 1
        END IF
    WEND

    LET finalLevel AS MMIOPTR = levelA
    IF useAAsCurrent = 0 THEN
        finalLevel = levelB
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(finalLevel, 8))
END FUNCTION

' Recursively walks the Extent Tree rooted at `nodeSector`, appending every leaf entry (32 bytes:
' [dataSlot][chunkIndex][extentSector][reserved]) into `destBase`, tracked via the shared counter
' at `destCountAddress` -- structurally identical to ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto (see that
' function's own header note for the aliasing-safety reasoning behind reading child sectors into
' local variables before recursing), sized for up to ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
' (256) entries -- every touched chunk across every currently active slot.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            ' RFC-0042 Section 11: was a bare literal 256, the same stale-bound-literal risk fixed
            ' in ArcFSReadCheckpoint's own extentTreeCount check -- re-derived from the same named
            ' constants here too, this phase.
            IF destCount >= ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() THEN
                RETURN 0
            END IF
            LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 32)
            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 32)
            LET b AS U64 = 0
            WHILE b < 32
                MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b)))
                b = b + 1
            WEND
            MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child0, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child1, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child2, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(child3, destBase, destCountAddress) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 39/40's own defect-tolerant scrub, extended to the Extent Tree exactly like
' ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant/ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant already do for
' the Object/Attribute Trees: a corrupt leaf contributes zero entries but is otherwise harmless to
' its siblings; a corrupt internal/root node makes every entry reachable only through it genuinely
' unscannable this pass, counted as exactly one defect, not silently treated as empty. Wired into
' ArcFSScanGeneration as its own pass -- closes this project's own previously-named "the Extent
' Tree has no defect-tolerant scrub coverage" gap.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(nodeSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET destCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(destCountAddress)
            ' RFC-0042 Section 11: was a bare literal 256, re-derived from the same named
            ' constants as this file's other extent-tree bound checks, this phase.
            IF destCount < ArcFSMaxObjects() * ArcFSMaxFileChunks() THEN
                LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 32)
                LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, destCount * 32)
                LET b2 AS U64 = 0
                WHILE b2 < 32
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, b2), MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(src, b2)))
                    b2 = b2 + 1
                WEND
                MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, destCount + 1)
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    LET totalDefects AS U64 = 0
    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child0, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child1, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child2, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        totalDefects = totalDefects + ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(child3, destBase, destCountAddress)
    END IF
    RETURN totalDefects
END FUNCTION

' Loads exactly one dataSlot's real chunks from the Extent Tree into `destRow`'s own data-pool
' slot -- the mount-time counterpart of ArcFSGatherSortedExtentEntries. Zeroes the ENTIRE
' destination slot and resets its touched bitmap first, so a hole reads as zero (RFC-0040 Section
' 11.3) rather than whatever stale bytes an earlier occupant of that row's own slot left behind,
' exactly like Phase K's own ArcFSLoadExtentListIntoPool did. Does a fresh full-tree collect on
' every call (once per FILE object being mounted) rather than caching -- correctness over speed,
' the same tradeoff every other pass in this file already makes at this project's test scale.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeLoadDataSlotIntoRow(extentTreeRoot AS U64, dataSlot AS U64, destRow AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET slotSize AS U64 = ArcFSSlotSizeBytes()
    LET destBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSDataPoolAddress(), destRow * slotSize)
    LET z AS U64 = 0
    WHILE z < slotSize
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, z), 0)
        z = z + 1
    WEND
    LET c AS U64 = 0
    WHILE c < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
        ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(destRow, c, 0)
        c = c + 1
    WEND

    IF extentTreeRoot = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(extentTreeRoot, ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress())
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress()
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < collectedCount
        LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, i * 32)
        LET entrySlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(entry)
        IF entrySlot = dataSlot THEN
            LET chunkIndex AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 8))
            LET extentSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 16))
            IF extentSector <> 0 THEN
                IF chunkIndex < ArcFSMaxFileChunks() THEN
                    LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, chunkIndex * ArcFSFileCapacityBytes())
                    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(extentSector, 8, dst) = 0 THEN RETURN 0
                    ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(destRow, chunkIndex, 1)
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7/9's own reachability widening, extended to the Extent Tree: a sector belongs
' to `dataSlot`'s real storage if it IS one of the Extent Tree's own node sectors (unconditional,
' matching ArcFSTreeContainsSector's own node-span check -- if ANY reachable object needs this
' tree at all, every node in it is reachable, regardless of which object's own check happens to
' walk it) or falls within a LEAF ENTRY matching `dataSlot` specifically (filtered -- a different
' object's own chunks must not make an unrelated slot's sector look reachable).
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot(nodeSector AS U64, dataSlot AS U64, targetSector AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF nodeSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF targetSector >= nodeSector THEN
        IF targetSector < nodeSector + 8 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    END IF

    LET node AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNodeScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(nodeSector, 8, node) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 4092))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(node, 4092)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nodeType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(node)
    LET keyCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(node, 8))

    IF nodeType = 1 THEN
        LET i AS U64 = 0
        WHILE i < keyCount
            LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + i * 32)
            LET entrySlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(entry)
            IF entrySlot = dataSlot THEN
                LET extentSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 16))
                IF extentSector <> 0 THEN
                    IF targetSector >= extentSector THEN
                        IF targetSector < extentSector + 8 THEN RETURN 1
                    END IF
                END IF
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET capacity AS U64 = ArcFSObjectTreeNodeCapacity()
    LET childrenBase AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(node, 24 + capacity * 8)
    LET child0 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(childrenBase)
    LET child1 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 8))
    LET child2 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 16))
    LET child3 AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(childrenBase, 24))

    IF keyCount >= 1 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot(child0, dataSlot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 2 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot(child1, dataSlot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 3 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot(child2, dataSlot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF keyCount >= 4 THEN
        IF ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot(child3, dataSlot, targetSector) = 1 THEN RETURN 1
    END IF
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' Re-walks the WHOLE Extent Tree fresh and returns `dataSlot`'s chunkIndex'th real chunk sector (0
' = hole, dataSlot not found, or the tree itself failed checksum -- indistinguishable to this
' helper's caller, matching how every other reader in this file treats 0 as "nothing here," never
' a live sector). The read-side counterpart of ArcFSExtentTreeContainsSectorForSlot, used by
' ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap below.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentTreeChunkAt(extentTreeRoot AS U64, dataSlot AS U64, chunkIndex AS U64) AS U64
    IF extentTreeRoot = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(extentTreeRoot, ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress())
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress()
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < collectedCount
        LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, i * 32)
        LET entrySlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(entry)
        IF entrySlot = dataSlot THEN
            LET entryChunk AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 8))
            IF entryChunk = chunkIndex THEN
                RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 16))
            END IF
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 39's "extent overlap detection," now expressed directly against dataSlot
' identity rather than a pairwise fixed-list comparison: two DIFFERENT dataSlots claiming the same
' physical sector is a genuine defect (real corruption -- this Extent Tree build never produces
' that on its own); the SAME dataSlot appearing in both callers is INTENTIONAL sharing (an
' undiverged reflink), not a defect, and is rejected up front rather than ever being compared
' against itself.
FUNCTION ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap(extentTreeRoot AS U64, dataSlotA AS U64, dataSlotB AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF dataSlotA = dataSlotB THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF extentTreeRoot = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
        LET a AS U64 = ArcFSExtentTreeChunkAt(extentTreeRoot, dataSlotA, i)
        IF a <> 0 THEN
            LET j AS U64 = 0
            WHILE j < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
                LET b AS U64 = ArcFSExtentTreeChunkAt(extentTreeRoot, dataSlotB, j)
                IF b <> 0 THEN
                    IF ArcFSRangesOverlap(a, 8, b, 8) = 1 THEN
                        RETURN 1
                    END IF
                END IF
                j = j + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' Requirement (RFC-0039 Section 40): formats a brand-new ArcFS volume on whatever BlockDevice
' ArcFS.ActiveBlockDevice() currently selects (RFC-0043 Phase S) -- generation 1, a lone
' root-directory object (OID 1, matching
' ArcFS.Initialize's own in-memory root), no namespace entries yet. Resets the in-memory tables to
' match via ArcFS.Initialize() so the caller's view is consistent with what was just written.
' RFC-0040 Section 8.1: sectors 0-3 are now the superblock ring, not a single sector 0. RFC-0040
' Section 7.1: sector 4 is now the Allocation Bitmap, not the checkpoint -- every fixed initial
' sector shifts forward by one more versus Phase H's own layout (checkpoint: 4 -> 5; root object:
' 5 -> 6; namespaceRoot/metadataEnd: 6 -> 7). Generation 1 has no predecessor, so its checkpoint's
' previousCheckpointSector (RFC-0040 Section 8.3) is 0. The initial bitmap marks sectors 0-6
' (ring, itself, checkpoint, root object) allocated; everything else starts free.
' RFC-0040 Section 9: sectors 0-3 (ring), 4 (bitmap), 5 (checkpoint) are reserved UP FRONT, before
' the root's own Object Tree leaf node is built -- ArcFSBuildObjectTree's own allocator
' (ArcFSAllocateTreeNode -> ArcFSAllocateDataExtent) needs the bitmap and high-water mark already
' correctly reflecting those 6 sectors, or it could try to hand the root's own node one of them
' back. The root leaf lands at sector 6 (spanning 6-13, 8 sectors); everything else starts at 14.
FUNCTION ArcFS.FormatVolume() AS BOOL
    ArcFS.Initialize()
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    LET totalBlocks AS U64 = ArcFSBlockSectorCount()
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()

    LET maxTracked AS U64 = ArcFSMaxTrackedSectors()
    LET clearIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE clearIndex < maxTracked
        ArcFSBitmapSet(clearIndex, 0)
        clearIndex = clearIndex + 1
    WEND
    ' RFC-0040 Section 13.2 / RFC-0043 Phase U: the Health Record, right after the checkpoint,
    ' joins sectors 0-3 (ring), 4..(ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector()-1) (Allocation Bitmap, now
    ' multi-sector), and the checkpoint itself as PERMANENTLY reserved -- one fixed record for the
    ' whole volume's life, not a per-generation tree entry, so it is never subject to reclaim/reuse
    ' the way ordinary data/metadata sectors are.
    LET reserveIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE reserveIndex < (ArcFSHealthRecordSector() + 1)
        ArcFSBitmapSet(reserveIndex, 1)
        reserveIndex = reserveIndex + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64), ArcFSHealthRecordSector() + 1)
    ArcFSWriteHealthRecord(scratch, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

    LET rootSector AS U64 = ArcFSBuildObjectTree()
    IF rootSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET afterTree AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64))

    IF ArcFSWriteBitmapRecord(scratch, 4) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    ' RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase: namespaceRoot=0 is the explicit "zero entries"
    ' sentinel (see the Namespace Tree section's own header note above) -- a fresh volume's
    ' namespace genuinely has no entries yet (root itself has no namespace record; it IS the
    ' implicit root), so there is no tree to build or root sector to name. RFC-0040 Section 12.1:
    ' attributeTreeRoot=0/attributeTreeCount=0 the same way -- a fresh volume's root object has no
    ' attribute set on it either. RFC-0040 Section 11.1: extentTreeRoot=0/extentTreeCount=0 too --
    ' a fresh volume's lone root object is a DIRECTORY, which has no chunks at all.
    ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord(scratch, 1, rootSector, 1, 0, 0, afterTree, 0, 4, afterTree, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector(), 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    ' Every ring copy is byte-identical; write the same freshly built superblock content to all
    ' 4 fixed ring sectors, in a fixed order (RFC-0040 Section 8.2).
    ArcFSWriteSuperblockRecord(scratch, ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector(), totalBlocks)
    LET ringSlot AS U64 = 0
    WHILE ringSlot < 4
        IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(ringSlot, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        ringSlot = ringSlot + 1
    WEND

    MEMORY.Write64(state, ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector())
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 8), rootSector)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 16), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 24), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 32), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 40), afterTree)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 48), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 56), 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64), afterTree)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 80), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 88), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 96), 4)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 104), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 112), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 15.2 steps 1-6: allocate fresh sectors for every active object/namespace
' record and every FILE's data extent, write them all, then write the new checkpoint record
' itself -- all to sectors the previously committed generation never touched. Does NOT touch the
' superblock; the previous generation remains the only one visible to a fresh ArcFS.MountImage()
' call until ArcFS.PublishCommit() runs. On any failure, clears the pending-commit marker first so
' a caller can never accidentally publish a half-written attempt.
'
' Phase F addition (RFC-0039 Section 38): refuses outright while ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress is
' set -- a structural defect was found in the mounted generation, and ordinary mutation on top of
' a known-bad tree is exactly what ReadOnlySafety exists to prevent. ArcFS.RepairCommit is the
' one sanctioned way through this guard.
FUNCTION ArcFS.PrepareCommit() AS BOOL
    IF MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress()) <> 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 72), 0)

    LET activeObjectCount AS U64 = 0
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        LET objActive AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row), 8))
        IF objActive <> 0 THEN
            activeObjectCount = activeObjectCount + 1
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    LET activeNamespaceCount AS U64 = 0
    row = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows()
        LET nsActive AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(row), 56))
        IF nsActive <> 0 THEN
            activeNamespaceCount = activeNamespaceCount + 1
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    ' RFC-0040 Section 12: same active-count-first discipline as the Namespace Tree above.
    LET activeAttributeCount AS U64 = 0
    row = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxAttributes()
        LET attrActive AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSAttributeRowAddress(row), 24))
        IF attrActive <> 0 THEN
            activeAttributeCount = activeAttributeCount + 1
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND

    ' RFC-0040 Section 9/11/12: every tree is built (and every sector each needs self-allocated
    ' and self-marked in the bitmap, via ArcFSAllocateTreeNode) BEFORE the bitmap/checkpoint
    ' sectors themselves are allocated below, so none has to guess its own size upfront the way
    ' the old flat-array formula did. The Extent Tree is built FIRST, before the Object Tree --
    ' every real chunk extent is allocated and written here, and ArcFSBuildObjectTree's own leaf
    ' writer (RFC-0040 Section 11.1) now just copies each row's dataSlot through unchanged, no
    ' longer allocating anything itself. expectedExtentCount = 0 is the legitimate "no FILE has
    ' any real, touched chunk" case (same convention every other tree's own active-count-first
    ' check already uses) -- distinguished from a genuine allocate/write failure (root still 0,
    ' but expectedExtentCount > 0) via ArcFSCountTouchedChunks()'s own independent, I/O-free
    ' pre-count, the same reason activeNamespaceCount/activeAttributeCount are computed here
    ' rather than trusted from whatever the corresponding Build*Tree call happens to return.
    LET expectedExtentCount AS U64 = ArcFSCountTouchedChunks()
    LET newExtentTreeRoot AS U64 = ArcFSBuildExtentTree()
    LET newExtentTreeCount AS U64 = ArcFSLastBuiltExtentTreeCount()
    IF expectedExtentCount > 0 THEN
        IF newExtentTreeRoot = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF

    LET newObjectRoot AS U64 = ArcFSBuildObjectTree()
    IF newObjectRoot = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET newNamespaceRoot AS U64 = 0
    IF activeNamespaceCount > 0 THEN
        newNamespaceRoot = ArcFSBuildNamespaceTree()
        IF newNamespaceRoot = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF

    LET newAttributeRoot AS U64 = 0
    IF activeAttributeCount > 0 THEN
        newAttributeRoot = ArcFSBuildAttributeTree()
        IF newAttributeRoot = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF

    ' RFC-0040 Section 7.1 / RFC-0043 Phase U: allocate the bitmap's OWN new span (now
    ' ArcFSBitmapSectorCount() consecutive sectors, not one), then the checkpoint's own single
    ' sector, both via the shared high-water mark -- and mark all of them allocated in the
    ' in-memory bitmap BEFORE serializing it, so the image written to disk is already
    ' self-consistent about its own existence and the checkpoint that references it. Every commit
    ' still gets a genuinely FRESH bitmap span (real copy-on-write, matching the same discipline
    ' every other tree/record in this file already follows) -- the old span stays valid and
    ' unreferenced-but-intact on disk until ArcFS.PublishCommit atomically repoints the superblock
    ' ring at the new checkpoint, at which point a future ArcFS.ReclaimGeneration call is what
    ' actually frees it, exactly like every other superseded generation's own sectors.
    LET newBitmapSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64))
    LET bitmapSpan AS U64 = ArcFSBitmapSectorCount()
    LET spanIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE spanIndex < bitmapSpan
        ArcFSBitmapSet(newBitmapSector + spanIndex, 1)
        spanIndex = spanIndex + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64), newBitmapSector + bitmapSpan)
    LET newCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64))
    ArcFSBitmapSet(newCheckpointSector, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64), newCheckpointSector + 1)

    IF ArcFSWriteBitmapRecord(scratch, newBitmapSector) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET oldGeneration AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 56))
    LET newGeneration AS U64 = oldGeneration + 1
    ' RFC-0040 Section 8.3: the checkpoint this commit is about to supersede -- state offset 0
    ' still holds it here, since PublishCommit (not PrepareCommit) is what overwrites it.
    LET previousCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(state)
    LET finalHighWaterMark AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 64))
    ' `dataRegionStart` (6th positional) is vestigial -- retired by Phase I's own real
    ' highWaterMark field, no reader still derives anything from it (see ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord's
    ' own header note) -- finalHighWaterMark is passed as a stable, always-defined placeholder.
    ArcFSWriteCheckpointRecord(scratch, newGeneration, newObjectRoot, activeObjectCount, newNamespaceRoot, activeNamespaceCount, finalHighWaterMark, previousCheckpointSector, newBitmapSector, finalHighWaterMark, newAttributeRoot, activeAttributeCount, newExtentTreeRoot, newExtentTreeCount)
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(newCheckpointSector, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 72), newCheckpointSector)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 56), newGeneration)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 96), newBitmapSector)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 15.2 step 7: the single atomic publication point. Rewrites the superblock so
' its checkpointSector field points at the checkpoint ArcFS.PrepareCommit just wrote -- one
' single-sector write, matching the commit-ordering requirement that the new generation must not
' be "exposed as durable" any earlier than this. Refuses if no commit is currently prepared.
' RFC-0040 Section 8.2: publication now writes the new superblock content to all 4 ring copies,
' in a fixed order (0, 1, 2, 3), before the transaction counts as durably published. A crash
' partway through this loop still leaves every already-written ring copy correctly pointing at
' the NEW checkpoint and every not-yet-reached copy still correctly pointing at the OLD one --
' never a torn single copy, and ArcFSReadSuperblockRing's own highest-generation-wins selection
' means the new generation activates as soon as even one copy has been updated, with the
' remaining copies self-healing to match on the next successful publish.
FUNCTION ArcFS.PublishCommit() AS BOOL
    LET state AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSMountStateAddress()
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    LET pendingCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 72))
    IF pendingCheckpointSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET totalBlocks AS U64 = ArcFSBlockSectorCount()
    ArcFSWriteSuperblockRecord(scratch, pendingCheckpointSector, totalBlocks)
    LET ringSlot AS U64 = 0
    WHILE ringSlot < 4
        IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(ringSlot, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        ringSlot = ringSlot + 1
    WEND

    MEMORY.Write64(state, pendingCheckpointSector)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(state, 72), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Convenience: prepare then publish in one call, for the ordinary (non-crash-injection) case.
' Returns TRUE only if the ENTIRE commit -- including publication -- succeeded.
FUNCTION ArcFS.CommitImage() AS BOOL
    IF ArcFS.PrepareCommit() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN ArcFS.PublishCommit()
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' Phase E: snapshots and reclamation (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase E -- "snapshot roots;
' generation pinning; shared extent accounting; safe reclamation").
' ==========================================================================================
'
' A snapshot (RFC-0039 Section 21) is a persistent reference to a committed generation that
' survives later commits. Because Phase C's bump allocator NEVER reuses a sector, every past
' checkpoint's bytes are still physically present on the BlockDevice for as long as this milestone
' exists -- nothing has ever been destroyed. What a snapshot actually buys, and the entire reason
' Phase E exists, is the SAFETY ANALYSIS a future reclaiming allocator would need before it is
' allowed to destroy anything: ArcFS.IsSectorReachable / ArcFS.ReclaimableSectorCount compute
' exactly which sectors are safe to reuse (reachable from neither the active checkpoint nor any
' live snapshot) -- "generation pinning" and "shared extent accounting," proven correct here, ready
' for a future allocator to consult. Teaching the allocator to actually REUSE that space is
' deliberately NOT done in this phase; see the scope-reduction note below for why.
'
' A critical design decision, stated up front: snapshots in this implementation are STRICTLY
' READ-ONLY and are NEVER loaded into the live Object/Namespace/data-pool tables Phase A defined --
' those tables are the WRITE PATH's own state, the thing ArcFS.PrepareCommit reads FROM. Mounting a
' snapshot into them (the obvious-looking shortcut: just reuse ArcFS.MountImage against the
' snapshot's checkpoint sector) would silently repoint ArcFSMountStateAddress's own "active
' checkpoint" and "next free sector" bookkeeping at the OLD generation's much smaller numbers --
' the very next ArcFS.CommitImage() call would then allocate sectors that a newer, real generation
' already occupies, corrupting it. Instead, every snapshot-read function below (ArcFS.
' SnapshotResolve, ArcFS.SnapshotReadFile) works by scanning the snapshot's own on-disk records
' directly via ArcFSBlockReadSectors, exactly mirroring Phase B's own loader logic but writing
' nothing into the live tables and touching no write-path state at all. Slower per call, but the
' live tree's integrity can never depend on which snapshot was most recently inspected.
'
' Documented Phase E scope reductions, in the same spirit as every prior phase's own header note:
'
' 1. No real space reuse. ArcFS.ReclaimableSectorCount() proves WHAT could be reclaimed; nothing in
'    this phase teaches ArcFS.PrepareCommit's bump allocator to actually consult it and reuse those
'    sectors. That changes the bump allocator's core "never reuse a sector" invariant Phase C
'    established specifically to avoid a free-list's own crash-safety questions -- a real free-list
'    allocator needs the same careful crash-injection analysis Phase C gave the commit protocol
'    itself, and is deliberately left for a later increment.
' 2. Fixed-capacity snapshot table (8 slots), matching every "one instance / small fixed table"
'    pattern this whole project chain has used.
' 3. A snapshot pins exactly one checkpoint (one generation); there is no retention policy,
'    labeling, or creation-time metadata beyond generation + checkpoint sector (RFC-0039 Section
'    21's "human label... creator/authority metadata... optional descriptive attributes" are not
'    implemented -- the same STRING-has-no-operations limitation Phase A's own scope reduction #4
'    already gave for why labels are awkward on this backend).
' 4. ArcFS.ReclaimableSectorCount() is a straightforward O(sectors x live-references) scan, not an
'    interval index -- fine at this milestone's tiny (sub-300-sector) test scale, not a production
'    algorithm.

FUNCTION ArcFSSnapshotTableAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34119680))   ' 0x208A000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSNextSnapshotIdAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34123776))   ' 0x208B000
END FUNCTION

' Scratch state for "peeking" an arbitrary checkpoint's fields WITHOUT touching the live mount
' state (ArcFSMountStateAddress is write-path state; this is a read-only side table). 80 bytes:
' [0]objectRoot [8]objectCount [16]namespaceRoot [24]namespaceCount [32]dataRegionStart
' [40]foundSize [48]foundExtentSector [56]allocationBitmapSector [64]attributeTreeRoot
' [72]attributeTreeCount -- the middle two (40/48) are scratch outputs for
' ArcFSSnapshotFindObjectRecord below, reusing this same block rather than a separate one; the
' rest were added as ArcFSGenerationMetadataRangeContains/ArcFSGenerationContainsSector and (this
' phase, RFC-0040 Section 12) the Attribute Tree each needed their own checkpoint field captured
' here.
FUNCTION ArcFSPeekStateAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34127872))   ' 0x208C000
END FUNCTION

' A full 4096-byte scratch buffer for ArcFS.SnapshotReadFile below -- an extent is always 8
' sectors (4096 bytes), which does not fit the 512-byte ArcFSSectorScratchAddress used for single
' metadata records, and reading it straight into the CALLER's buffer would overrun that buffer
' whenever maxBytes < 4096 (every fixture in this chain calls with a smaller maxBytes).
FUNCTION ArcFSSnapshotExtentScratchAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34131968))   ' 0x208D000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSMaxSnapshots() AS U64
    RETURN 8
END FUNCTION

' Snapshot Tree (RFC-0039 Section 16.7, scaled to a fixed table). Row layout, 32 bytes:
' [active U64][snapshotId U64][generation U64][checkpointSector U64].
FUNCTION ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot AS U64) AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSnapshotTableAddress(), slot * 32)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindFreeSnapshotRow() AS U64
    LET slot AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxSnapshots()
    WHILE slot < ArcFSMaxSnapshots()
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot))
        IF active = 0 THEN
            IF found = ArcFSMaxSnapshots() THEN
                found = slot
            END IF
        END IF
        slot = slot + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSFindSnapshotRowById(snapshotId AS U64) AS U64
    LET slot AS U64 = 0
    LET found AS U64 = ArcFSMaxSnapshots()
    WHILE slot < ArcFSMaxSnapshots()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET storedId AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
            IF storedId = snapshotId THEN
                found = slot
            END IF
        END IF
        slot = slot + 1
    WEND
    RETURN found
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAllocateSnapshotId() AS U64
    LET nextId AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNextSnapshotIdAddress())
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNextSnapshotIdAddress(), nextId + 1)
    RETURN nextId + 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 21: "Creating a snapshot SHALL create a persistent reference to a committed
' generation/root set without copying all referenced file data." Pins the CURRENTLY ACTIVE
' checkpoint -- state offset 0, kept continuously in sync by ArcFS.PublishCommit -- so it survives
' any number of later commits. Returns the new snapshot ID (never 0), or 0 on failure (the
' snapshot table is full, or nothing has ever been committed/mounted yet).
FUNCTION ArcFS.CreateSnapshot() AS U64
    LET activeCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSMountStateAddress())
    IF activeCheckpointSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET generation AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 56))

    LET slot AS U64 = ArcFSFindFreeSnapshotRow()
    IF slot = ArcFSMaxSnapshots() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET snapshotId AS U64 = ArcFSAllocateSnapshotId()
    LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot)
    MEMORY.Write64(base, 1)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8), snapshotId)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), generation)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24), activeCheckpointSector)
    RETURN snapshotId
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 21: "Deleting a snapshot removes its root reference transactionally." Removing
' the pin does not touch any BlockDevice sector -- it only makes the generation eligible for
' ArcFS.IsSectorReachable to report FALSE, i.e. eligible for a future allocator's reclamation.
FUNCTION ArcFS.DeleteSnapshot(snapshotId AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET slot AS U64 = ArcFSFindSnapshotRowById(snapshotId)
    IF slot = ArcFSMaxSnapshots() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Reads and validates an arbitrary checkpoint sector (active or a snapshot's -- this function does
' not care which) into ArcFSPeekStateAddress, WITHOUT touching ArcFSMountStateAddress. Mirrors
' ArcFSReadCheckpoint's own validation exactly (same field offsets, same checksum coverage).
FUNCTION ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(checkpointSector, 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF ArcFSVerifyCheckpointChecksum(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET peek AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSPeekStateAddress()
    MEMORY.Write64(peek, MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 8), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 16)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 16), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 24)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 24), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 32), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 40)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 56), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 56)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 64), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 72)))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 72), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 80)))
    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: the Extent Tree's own root sector, gained this phase -- needed by
    ' ArcFSGenerationContainsSector/ArcFS.SnapshotReadFile/ArcFSFindExtentOverlaps, all of which
    ' operate on an arbitrary PEEKED (possibly non-live) generation, not necessarily the live
    ' mount's own extentTreeRoot.
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 80), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 88)))
    ' The Extent Tree's own entry count -- needed by ArcFSScanGeneration's own Pass 7 (Extent Tree
    ' scrub coverage) to cross-check the tolerant walk's own collected count against what the
    ' PEEKED generation's checkpoint actually claims, the same "bounded by collected, not claimed"
    ' discipline every other pass in this file already establishes.
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 88), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 96)))
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase: narrowed FURTHER than Phase J left it -- now just the
' Allocation Bitmap's own sector and the checkpoint, since BOTH the Object Tree (Phase J) and the
' Namespace Tree (this increment) are no longer necessarily contiguous with anything;
' ArcFSTreeContainsSector/ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector are the authoritative checks for tree
' sectors now (see ArcFSGenerationContainsSector below). Bitmap/checkpoint placement is the one
' remaining contiguous-append pair (ArcFS.PrepareCommit allocates them back to back, immediately
' before writing the checkpoint that references the bitmap sector), so [min(allocationBitmapSector,
' checkpointSector), max(...)] still correctly covers both.
FUNCTION ArcFSGenerationMetadataRangeContains(checkpointSector AS U64, sector AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET allocationBitmapSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 56))
    LET lo AS U64 = allocationBitmapSector
    LET hi AS U64 = checkpointSector
    IF checkpointSector < allocationBitmapSector THEN
        lo = checkpointSector
        hi = allocationBitmapSector
    END IF
    IF sector < lo THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF sector > hi THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7: with real data-extent reuse, a generation's used sectors are no longer a
' single contiguous range -- its own metadata (allocation bitmap, checkpoint) still is
' (ArcFSGenerationMetadataRangeContains remains correct for exactly that narrow span), but its
' Object Tree, Namespace Tree, and FILE data extents can each live anywhere the shared allocator
' independently placed them, including sectors BELOW that metadata span if reused from reclaimed
' space. A sector is reachable via a generation if it falls in that generation's metadata range, OR
' within its Object Tree, OR within its Namespace Tree (RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase --
' namespaceCount = 0 is the legitimate "no namespace tree at all" case, short-circuited before ever
' treating namespaceRoot=0 as a sector).
FUNCTION ArcFSGenerationContainsSector(checkpointSector AS U64, sector AS U64) AS BOOL
    IF ArcFSGenerationMetadataRangeContains(checkpointSector, sector) = 1 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET objectRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPeekStateAddress())
    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: the Extent Tree, a fourth tree needing the same reachability widening
    ' the Object/Namespace/Attribute Trees already got -- its own root was added to the peek state
    ' block this phase (offset 80).
    LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 80))
    IF ArcFSTreeContainsSector(objectRoot, extentTreeRoot, sector) = 1 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET namespaceCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 24))
    IF namespaceCount <> 0 THEN
        LET namespaceRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 16))
        IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeContainsSector(namespaceRoot, sector) = 1 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    END IF
    ' RFC-0040 Section 12: the Attribute Tree, the third tree needing the same reachability
    ' widening the Object and Namespace Trees already got -- its own root/count were added to the
    ' peek state block this phase (offsets 64/72).
    LET attributeCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 72))
    IF attributeCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET attributeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 64))
    RETURN ArcFSAttributeTreeContainsSector(attributeRoot, sector)
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 78's "generation pinning": a sector is reachable -- and therefore MUST NOT be
' reclaimed -- iff it falls within the ACTIVE checkpoint's own generation range, or within any
' LIVE snapshot's pinned generation range. Nothing else pins a generation in this implementation.
FUNCTION ArcFS.IsSectorReachable(sector AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET activeCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSMountStateAddress())
    IF activeCheckpointSector <> 0 THEN
        IF ArcFSGenerationContainsSector(activeCheckpointSector, sector) = 1 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    END IF

    LET slot AS U64 = 0
    WHILE slot < ArcFSMaxSnapshots()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET snapCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24))
            IF ArcFSGenerationContainsSector(snapCheckpointSector, sector) = 1 THEN
                RETURN 1
            END IF
        END IF
        slot = slot + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 78's "safe reclamation": counts every sector between the volume's first real
' generation (sector 1) and the bump allocator's current high-water mark that ArcFS.
' IsSectorReachable reports unreachable -- i.e. sectors a future reclaiming allocator could safely
' reuse today. Does not reclaim anything itself; see this section's own scope-reduction note #1.
' RFC-0043 Phase W: a real, pre-existing bug found and fixed here -- this function never checked
' ArcFSBitmapGet(sector) before counting a sector "reclaimable," unlike ArcFS.ReclaimGeneration's
' own (correct) logic just below. An ALREADY-FREE sector (never allocated, or freed by an earlier
' reclaim) is trivially "unreachable" too -- nothing references a free sector -- so without this
' guard, any volume that had ever freed even one sector below its own high-water mark would report
' a permanently nonzero count with nothing left to actually reclaim, no matter how many real
' reclaim passes ran. Never caught before RFC-0043 Phase W's own long-session fixture: every prior
' caller either never checked this function's return value after a real reclaim actually freed
' something, or tested volumes with no sub-high-water-mark free sectors at the time of the call.
FUNCTION ArcFS.ReclaimableSectorCount() AS U64
    LET highWaterMark AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 64))
    LET reclaimable AS U64 = 0
    ' RFC-0040 Section 8.1: sectors 0-3 are the fixed superblock ring, permanently reserved, never
    ' a candidate for reclamation -- the scan now starts at 4 (was 1 under FMV1's single-sector
    ' superblock), the first sector any generation's own data can actually occupy.
    LET sector AS U64 = 4
    WHILE sector < highWaterMark
        IF ArcFSBitmapGet(sector) <> 0 THEN
            IF ArcFS.IsSectorReachable(sector) = 0 THEN
                reclaimable = reclaimable + 1
            END IF
        END IF
        sector = sector + 1
    WEND
    RETURN reclaimable
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 7.3: "Reclamation is an ordinary commit... an ordinary commit whose only
' content is: for every block in a generation ArcFS.IsSectorReachable reports unreachable, mark it
' free in the Allocation Tree. There SHALL be no separate, non-transactional reclamation code
' path." This function does exactly that ONE thing -- mutate the in-memory bitmap, marking every
' currently-allocated-but-unreachable sector free -- and nothing else. It does NOT call
' ArcFS.PrepareCommit/PublishCommit itself; the caller does, exactly like every other mutation
' (ArcFS.CreateFile, ArcFS.Remove, ...) already works in this implementation. That is what makes
' this a real transaction and not a separate mechanism: the SAME commit protocol Phase C proved
' crash-safe governs reclamation too, automatically, by construction. Returns the count of sectors
' this pass freed.
FUNCTION ArcFS.ReclaimGeneration() AS U64
    LET highWaterMark AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 64))
    LET reclaimed AS U64 = 0
    LET sector AS U64 = 4
    WHILE sector < highWaterMark
        IF ArcFSBitmapGet(sector) <> 0 THEN
            IF ArcFS.IsSectorReachable(sector) = 0 THEN
                ArcFSBitmapSet(sector, 0)
                reclaimed = reclaimed + 1
            END IF
        END IF
        sector = sector + 1
    WEND
    RETURN reclaimed
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 30's "snapshot-retained bytes": total sectors that are live ONLY because a
' snapshot pins them (i.e. excluding whatever the active checkpoint's own range already accounts
' for -- a snapshot of the CURRENTLY active generation retains nothing additional). Generation
' ranges never overlap by construction (each commit starts at the bump allocator's then-current
' high-water mark, strictly above every earlier range), so this is a plain sum, no double-counting
' possible.
FUNCTION ArcFS.SnapshotRetainedSectorCount() AS U64
    LET activeCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSMountStateAddress())
    LET retained AS U64 = 0
    LET slot AS U64 = 0
    WHILE slot < ArcFSMaxSnapshots()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot)
        LET active AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
        IF active <> 0 THEN
            LET snapCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24))
            IF snapCheckpointSector <> activeCheckpointSector THEN
                IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(snapCheckpointSector) = 1 THEN
                    LET objectRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPeekStateAddress())
                    LET lo AS U64 = objectRoot
                    LET hi AS U64 = snapCheckpointSector
                    IF snapCheckpointSector < objectRoot THEN
                        lo = snapCheckpointSector
                        hi = objectRoot
                    END IF
                    retained = retained + (hi - lo + 1)
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        slot = slot + 1
    WEND
    RETURN retained
END FUNCTION

' --- Read-only snapshot inspection (RFC-0039 Section 21: "Initial ArcFS snapshots SHALL be
' read-only"). Scans the snapshot's own on-disk records directly, exactly mirroring Phase B's
' loader logic -- but never writes into the live Object/Namespace tables and never touches
' write-path state (see this section's own header for why that separation is load-bearing, not
' style). ---

' RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase: `namespaceRoot` now names the Namespace Tree's root
' node sector, not a flat array's start. Finds a namespace record by (parentOid, name) by walking
' the whole tree (ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto, whose own per-node checksums stand in
' for the old per-record check) and linear-scanning the collected entries -- `namespaceCount` is
' kept as a parameter for signature compatibility with this function's one caller, used only as
' the "0 = genuinely empty, skip the walk entirely" short-circuit (namespaceRoot=0 would otherwise
' never be a valid tree read), not to bound the scan itself.
FUNCTION ArcFSSnapshotFindNamespaceRecord(namespaceRoot AS U64, namespaceCount AS U64, parentOid AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64) AS U64
    IF namespaceCount = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(namespaceRoot, ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress())
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress()
    LET index AS U64 = 0
    WHILE index < collectedCount
        LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, index * 56)
        LET storedParent AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(entry)
        IF storedParent = parentOid THEN
            LET storedLength AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 40))
            IF ArcFSNameMatches(entry, storedLength, nameBuffer, nameLength) = 1 THEN
                RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 48))
            END IF
        END IF
        index = index + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 9: `objectRoot` now names the Object Tree's root node sector, not a flat
' array's start. Finds an object record by OID by walking the whole tree (ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto,
' whose own per-node checksums stand in for the old per-record check) and linear-scanning the
' collected entries -- `objectCount` is kept as a parameter for signature compatibility with this
' function's one caller but is no longer needed to bound the scan, since the walk is self-bounding.
' Writes size and extent sector into ArcFSPeekStateAddress offsets 40/48 on success.
FUNCTION ArcFSSnapshotFindObjectRecord(objectRoot AS U64, objectCount AS U64, oid AS U64) AS BOOL
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesInto(objectRoot, ArcFSTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress()) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress())
    LET sorted AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeSortedAddress()
    LET index AS U64 = 0
    WHILE index < collectedCount
        LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sorted, index * 40)
        LET storedOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(entry)
        IF storedOid = oid THEN
            LET peek AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSPeekStateAddress()
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 40), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 16)))
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 48), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 24)))
            RETURN 1
        END IF
        index = index + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' Colon-delimited path resolution against a snapshot's pinned generation -- the read-only
' counterpart to ArcFS.Resolve, walking the SAME root-relative syntax (RFC-0039 Section 9.1) but
' consulting on-disk namespace records instead of the live table at every step.
FUNCTION ArcFS.SnapshotResolve(snapshotId AS U64, pathBuffer AS MMIOPTR, pathLength AS U64) AS U64
    LET slot AS U64 = ArcFSFindSnapshotRowById(snapshotId)
    IF slot = ArcFSMaxSnapshots() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET checkpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot), 24))
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET namespaceRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 16))
    LET namespaceCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 24))

    IF pathLength = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET firstByte AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(pathBuffer)
    IF firstByte <> 58 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF pathLength = 1 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    LET current AS U64 = 1
    LET cursor AS U64 = 1
    WHILE cursor < pathLength
        LET componentStart AS U64 = cursor
        LET scanning AS U64 = 1
        WHILE scanning = 1
            IF cursor >= pathLength THEN
                scanning = 0
            ELSE
                LET b AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(pathBuffer, cursor))
                IF b = 58 THEN
                    scanning = 0
                ELSE
                    cursor = cursor + 1
                END IF
            END IF
        WEND
        LET componentLength AS U64 = cursor - componentStart
        IF componentLength = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        LET componentPtr AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(pathBuffer, componentStart)
        LET nextOid AS U64 = ArcFSSnapshotFindNamespaceRecord(namespaceRoot, namespaceCount, current, componentPtr, componentLength)
        IF nextOid = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
        current = nextOid
        cursor = cursor + 1
    WEND
    RETURN current
END FUNCTION

' Reads a FILE object's content directly from a snapshot's pinned generation -- the read-only
' counterpart to ArcFS.OpenHandle + ArcFS.HandleRead, in one call since a snapshot handle has no
' write side to justify a separate open/close lifecycle.
FUNCTION ArcFS.SnapshotReadFile(snapshotId AS U64, oid AS U64, buffer AS MMIOPTR, maxBytes AS U64) AS U64
    LET slot AS U64 = ArcFSFindSnapshotRowById(snapshotId)
    IF slot = ArcFSMaxSnapshots() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET checkpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot), 24))
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET objectRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPeekStateAddress())
    LET objectCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 8))
    IF ArcFSSnapshotFindObjectRecord(objectRoot, objectCount, oid) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET size AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 40))
    LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 48))
    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: the PINNED generation's own Extent Tree root, not necessarily the
    ' live mount's -- ArcFSPeekCheckpoint (called above via ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector))
    ' already populated this at peek offset 80.
    LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 80))
    LET toRead AS U64 = maxBytes
    IF toRead > size THEN
        toRead = size
    END IF

    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: reconstruct the requested prefix chunk by chunk from the Extent Tree
    ' -- a hole (no matching entry) contributes real zero bytes, matching the live read path's own
    ' "a hole reads as zero" contract. Only reads a chunk off the BlockDevice when the requested
    ' range actually reaches it.
    LET fullExtent AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSnapshotExtentScratchAddress()
    LET chunkBytes AS U64 = ArcFSFileCapacityBytes()
    LET written AS U64 = 0
    LET chunkIndex AS U64 = 0
    WHILE chunkIndex < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
        IF written < toRead THEN
            LET chunkSector AS U64 = ArcFSExtentTreeChunkAt(extentTreeRoot, dataSlot, chunkIndex)
            LET haveChunk AS BOOL = 0
            IF chunkSector <> 0 THEN
                IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(chunkSector, 8, fullExtent) <> 0 THEN
                    haveChunk = 1
                END IF
            END IF
            LET k AS U64 = 0
            WHILE k < chunkBytes
                IF written < toRead THEN
                    LET v AS U8 = 0
                    IF haveChunk = 1 THEN
                        v = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(fullExtent, k))
                    END IF
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(buffer, written), v)
                    written = written + 1
                END IF
                k = k + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        chunkIndex = chunkIndex + 1
    WEND
    RETURN written
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' Phase F: recovery and health (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase F -- "scrub; offline checker; repair
' planning; ReadOnlySafety activation; inspector health interface").
' ==========================================================================================
'
' ArcFSScanGeneration is the one real structural-validation pass every public function in this
' section builds on. Given a checkpoint sector, it re-verifies every object and namespace
' record's checksum (RFC-0039 Section 39's "checksum verification"), performs a real graph
' reachability analysis from root through the namespace tree ("namespace reachability analysis" /
' "orphan/unreachable object detection" -- not merely "does some record name me as a child," a
' full transitive-from-root check via relaxation), and detects overlapping file extents ("extent
' overlap detection"). It reads directly off the BlockDevice via ArcFSBlockReadSectors, exactly like
' Phase E's snapshot-read functions, and touches no live table -- a scrub must be safely callable
' without disturbing whatever is currently mounted.
'
' RFC-0039 Section 38's ReadOnlySafety is made concrete, not just reported: ArcFS.MountImageSafe
' mounts normally, then runs the same scan against the newly active generation; if it finds any
' defect, the volume is still fully READABLE (the committed checkpoint is not itself corrupt) but
' ArcFS.PrepareCommit now refuses every ordinary mutation attempt until ArcFS.RepairCommit (the one
' sanctioned exception) both fixes the defect and successfully re-verifies a clean scan.
'
' RFC-0039 Section 39's Inspect/Plan/Apply/Verify repair staging is implemented for exactly one
' repair case, chosen because RFC-0039 itself names the correct outcome: "When data cannot be
' reattached to its original namespace, recovery SHOULD preserve it in a clearly identified
' recovery namespace/object collection with provenance metadata." ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans
' (Apply) re-parents every orphaned object into a root-level `:lost+found` directory under a
' synthetic `orphan-<oid>` name -- the OID itself IS the provenance record, since it is exactly
' the identity the object had before it lost its namespace entry. ArcFS.RepairCommit (Apply's own
' commit, then Verify) is the only function in this codebase allowed to commit while
' ReadOnlySafety is set, because fixing that exact condition is its entire purpose; it re-arms
' ReadOnlySafety if a fresh scan afterward still finds a defect, rather than trusting the repair
' blindly.
'
' Documented Phase F scope reductions, in the same spirit as every prior phase's own header note:
'
' 1. ArcFS.GetHealthState() collapses RFC-0039 Section 36's full state list (Healthy, Degraded,
'    ReadOnlySafety, NeedsScrub, NeedsOfflineCheck, Corrupt, Unavailable) down to three: Healthy
'    (0), Degraded (1, defects found but the volume is readable), Corrupt (2, the superblock
'    itself is unreadable or invalid). The distinctions this collapses away (ReadOnlySafety vs.
'    NeedsScrub vs. NeedsOfflineCheck) are real per RFC-0039 but are not independently
'    OBSERVABLE from anything this implementation currently tracks beyond "defects found" -- a
'    finer state machine would need scrub-history/repair-history persistence this phase does not
'    add (see reduction #3).
' 2. Repair covers exactly one defect class (orphaned objects). Extent-overlap defects are
'    DETECTED (ArcFSScanGeneration counts them) but this phase has no repair for them -- a real
'    fix would need to choose which of two overlapping objects is authoritative, which is a
'    policy decision RFC-0039 does not make for this implementation to invent silently (Section
'    5.3: "Implementations MUST NOT guess silently").
' 3. No scrub/repair HISTORY is persisted (RFC-0039 Section 36's own "scrub history... repair
'    history" health-detail fields). Every function in this section computes its answer fresh,
'    live, each call.
' 4. ArcFSScanGeneration is O(objectCount^2 + objectCount x namespaceCount), matching Phase E's
'    own documented performance scope reduction -- fine at this milestone's tiny test scale.
' 5. Extent-overlap detection's own logic is exercised by direct review and the fixture's own
'    orphan-repair scenario exercises every OTHER pass in ArcFSScanGeneration end to end, but no
'    fixture in this phase constructs a deliberately overlapping-extent image to exercise that
'    ONE specific defect class under QEMU -- stated plainly rather than silently left untested.

FUNCTION ArcFSVisitedOidListAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34136064))   ' 0x208E000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSVisitedCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34136576))   ' 0x208E200
END FUNCTION

' Scratch object array populated fresh by every ArcFSScanGeneration call, up to ArcFSMaxObjects()
' entries, 24 bytes each: [oid][type][extentSector]. type = 0 marks a slot whose on-disk record
' failed its checksum (already counted as a defect) -- every later pass skips it, matching the
' Object Table's own free/invalid convention.
FUNCTION ArcFSScanObjectArrayAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34136832))   ' 0x208E300
END FUNCTION

' Scratch namespace array, up to ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() entries, 16 bytes each: [parentOid]
' [childOid]. parentOid = 0 marks a slot whose on-disk record failed its checksum (already
' counted as a defect; 0 is a safe sentinel since no real object ever has OID 0).
FUNCTION ArcFSScanNamespaceArrayAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34138368))   ' 0x208E900
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSDefectCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34140416))   ' 0x208F100
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 38: non-zero means the mounted generation has a known structural defect and
' ArcFS.PrepareCommit MUST refuse ordinary mutation until ArcFS.RepairCommit both fixes it and
' re-verifies a clean scan.
FUNCTION ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34140672))   ' 0x208F200
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSRepairNameScratchAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(34140928))   ' 0x208F300
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 13.1: two per-scan flags ArcFSScanGeneration sets fresh every call, read back
' by ArcFS.GetHealthState's own widened seven-state mapping. [0]structuralDefectCount -- how many
' of this scan's defects came from Pass 1/2's own tree-node CHECKSUM/structural corruption
' (unlike an orphan or an extent overlap, this implementation has no repair for a tree node whose
' bytes are simply gone) vs. the REPAIRABLE classes (orphans, extent overlaps). [8]
' checkpointUnreadable -- set when the scan could not even peek the checkpoint itself, meaning
' Pass 1-5 never ran at all; a genuinely more severe condition than any defect COUNT they might
' have found, since the online scan cannot say anything more about this generation's contents.
FUNCTION ArcFSHealthStateAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35192832))   ' 0x2190000
END FUNCTION

' Up to 32 (lowOid, highOid) overlapping-extent pairs, 16 bytes each -- ArcFSFindExtentOverlaps'
' own output for ArcFS.RepairResolveExtentOverlaps (RFC-0040 Section 14.1) to act on.
FUNCTION ArcFSExtentOverlapPairsAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35196928))   ' 0x2191000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSExtentOverlapPairCountAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35201024))   ' 0x2192000
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 13.2: accumulates however many objects THIS repair sequence has fixed so far
' (ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans and ArcFS.RepairResolveExtentOverlaps both add their own count in),
' independent of which repair function(s) a caller invokes or in what order -- ArcFS.RepairCommit
' reads and persists the total into the Health Record only upon a successful, RE-VERIFIED commit,
' then resets this to 0, so the next repair sequence starts clean.
FUNCTION ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(35205120))   ' 0x2193000
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSVisitedReset() AS U64
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSVisitedCountAddress(), 0)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSIsOidVisited(oid AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET count AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSVisitedCountAddress())
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET v AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSVisitedOidListAddress(), i * 8))
        IF v = oid THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSMarkOidVisited(oid AS U64) AS U64
    IF ArcFSIsOidVisited(oid) = 1 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET count AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSVisitedCountAddress())
    IF count >= ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSVisitedOidListAddress(), count * 8), oid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSVisitedCountAddress(), count + 1)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSAddDefect() AS U64
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSDefectCountAddress(), MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSDefectCountAddress()) + 1)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 13.1: a STRUCTURAL defect (tree-node checksum/corruption) is always ALSO
' counted via ArcFSAddDefect (the total stays a true superset, unchanged meaning for every
' existing caller that only checks "> 0"), but is tracked separately here too, since this
' implementation has no repair for it -- unlike an orphan or an extent overlap, a corrupt node's
' bytes are simply gone. ArcFS.GetHealthState's own widened mapping uses this to report Corrupt
' distinctly from Degraded/ReadOnlySafety (repairable-only defects).
FUNCTION ArcFSAddStructuralDefect() AS U64
    ArcFSAddDefect()
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSHealthStateAddress(), MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSHealthStateAddress()) + 1)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSRangesOverlap(aStart AS U64, aLen AS U64, bStart AS U64, bLen AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET aEnd AS U64 = aStart + aLen - 1
    LET bEnd AS U64 = bStart + bLen - 1
    IF aEnd < bStart THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF bEnd < aStart THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' The one real structural-validation pass this whole section builds on. Given a checkpoint
' sector, returns the total defect count found: checksum failures, unreachable (orphaned)
' objects, and overlapping file extents. Touches no live table -- entirely disk-scan-based, like
' Phase E's own snapshot-read functions.
FUNCTION ArcFSScanGeneration(checkpointSector AS U64) AS U64
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSDefectCountAddress(), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSHealthStateAddress(), 0)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHealthStateAddress(), 8), 0)
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) = 0 THEN
        ArcFSAddDefect()
        ' RFC-0040 Section 13.1: the checkpoint itself couldn't even be read/verified -- Pass 1-5
        ' never ran at all, a more severe condition than any defect COUNT they might have found.
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHealthStateAddress(), 8), 1)
        RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSDefectCountAddress())
    END IF
    LET peek AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSPeekStateAddress()
    LET objectRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(peek)
    LET objectCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 8))
    LET namespaceRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 16))
    LET namespaceCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 24))
    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: this generation's own Extent Tree root, for Pass 5 below.
    LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 80))

    ' Pass 1 (RFC-0040 Section 9): walks the Object Tree with the DEFECT-TOLERANT variant --
    ' a corrupt node's own subtree is skipped and counted as one defect rather than aborting the
    ' whole scrub, matching this pass's own long-standing "keep scanning everything else" job.
    ' Collected entries are then re-shaped into the scratch object array Pass 3-5 already expect
    ' (24 bytes: [oid][type][dataSlot] -- RFC-0040 Section 11.1's own object-row identity, not an
    ' extent-list sector; see ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap for what Pass 5 does with it).
    LET objArray AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSScanObjectArrayAddress()
    LET sortedTemp AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeSortedAddress()
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    LET treeDefects AS U64 = ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(objectRoot, sortedTemp, ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress())
    LET d AS U64 = 0
    WHILE d < treeDefects
        ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
        d = d + 1
    WEND
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress())
    IF collectedCount <> objectCount THEN
        ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
    END IF

    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < collectedCount
        LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sortedTemp, i * 40)
        LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, i * 24)
        MEMORY.Write64(entry, MEMORY.Read64(src))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 8), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 8)))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 16), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 24)))
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    ' Pass 2 (RFC-0040 Section 9, finishing the phase): validates the Namespace Tree the same way
    ' Pass 1 validates the Object Tree -- a defect-tolerant walk, each node's checksum checked
    ' exactly once here, never again during the relaxation below, so a genuinely corrupt node is
    ' counted as exactly one defect, not once per pass. namespaceCount = 0 is the legitimate
    ' "no entries at all" case (see the Namespace Tree section's own header note) -- the walk is
    ' skipped entirely then, since namespaceRoot=0 is never a valid tree read.
    LET nsArray AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSScanNamespaceArrayAddress()
    LET nsSortedTemp AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceTreeSortedAddress()
    LET collectedNsCount AS U64 = 0
    IF namespaceCount > 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
        LET nsTreeDefects AS U64 = ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(namespaceRoot, nsSortedTemp, ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress())
        LET nd AS U64 = 0
        WHILE nd < nsTreeDefects
            ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
            nd = nd + 1
        WEND
        collectedNsCount = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSNamespaceTreeCollectCountAddress())
    END IF
    IF collectedNsCount <> namespaceCount THEN
        ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
    END IF

    LET j AS U64 = 0
    WHILE j < collectedNsCount
        LET nsSrc AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(nsSortedTemp, j * 56)
        LET nsEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(nsArray, j * 16)
        MEMORY.Write64(nsEntry, MEMORY.Read64(nsSrc))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsEntry, 8), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsSrc, 48)))
        j = j + 1
    WEND

    ' Pass 3: namespace reachability from root, via relaxation over the ALREADY-validated
    ' in-memory namespace array (no further disk I/O). Converges within objectCount iterations:
    ' every iteration either marks at least one previously-unvisited object visited, or nothing
    ' changes and no further iteration could help. Bounded by collectedNsCount, not the
    ' checkpoint's own claimed namespaceCount -- if Pass 2 found tree corruption, only entries
    ' actually collected have real data in nsArray (the same "bounded by collected, not claimed"
    ' discipline Pass 1/4 already established for the Object Tree).
    ArcFSVisitedReset()
    ArcFSMarkOidVisited(1)
    LET pass AS U64 = 0
    WHILE pass < objectCount
        LET k AS U64 = 0
        WHILE k < collectedNsCount
            LET nsEntry2 AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(nsArray, k * 16)
            LET parentOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(nsEntry2)
            IF parentOid <> 0 THEN
                LET childOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsEntry2, 8))
                IF ArcFSIsOidVisited(parentOid) = 1 THEN
                    ArcFSMarkOidVisited(childOid)
                END IF
            END IF
            k = k + 1
        WEND
        pass = pass + 1
    WEND

    ' Pass 4: orphan detection -- every valid object (excluding root) not reached above. Bounded
    ' by collectedCount, not the checkpoint's own claimed objectCount -- if Pass 1 found tree
    ' corruption, only entries actually collected have real data in objArray.
    LET m AS U64 = 0
    WHILE m < collectedCount
        LET objEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, m * 24)
        LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(objEntry, 8))
        IF objType <> 0 THEN
            LET oid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(objEntry)
            IF oid <> 1 THEN
                IF ArcFSIsOidVisited(oid) = 0 THEN
                    ArcFSAddDefect()
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        m = m + 1
    WEND

    ' Pass 5: extent overlap -- every pair of valid FILE objects, counted once per overlapping
    ' pair (RFC-0039 Section 39's "extent overlap detection," now checked directly against the
    ' Extent Tree via ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap -- RFC-0040 Section 11.1). Two DIFFERENT objects with
    ' the SAME dataSlot are an undiverged reflink (intentional sharing, not a defect) --
    ' ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap itself rejects that case up front, so it is not special-cased here.
    ' Also bounded by collectedCount.
    LET p AS U64 = 0
    WHILE p < collectedCount
        LET pEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, p * 24)
        LET pType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(pEntry, 8))
        IF pType = 1 THEN
            LET pDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(pEntry, 16))
            LET q AS U64 = p + 1
            WHILE q < collectedCount
                LET qEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, q * 24)
                LET qType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(qEntry, 8))
                IF qType = 1 THEN
                    LET qDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(qEntry, 16))
                    IF ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap(extentTreeRoot, pDataSlot, qDataSlot) = 1 THEN
                        ArcFSAddDefect()
                    END IF
                END IF
                q = q + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        p = p + 1
    WEND

    ' Pass 6 (RFC-0040 Section 12): validates the Attribute Tree the same way Pass 1/2 validate
    ' the Object/Namespace Trees -- ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant already existed
    ' (built alongside the tree itself, Phase L) but was never actually wired into a scan pass
    ' until now, closing a real, previously-named gap. attributeCount = 0 is the legitimate "no
    ' attributes at all" case -- the walk is skipped entirely then, since attributeRoot=0 is never
    ' a valid tree read.
    LET attributeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 64))
    LET attributeCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 72))
    IF attributeCount > 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
        LET attrTreeDefects AS U64 = ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(attributeRoot, ArcFSAttributeTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress())
        LET ad AS U64 = 0
        WHILE ad < attrTreeDefects
            ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
            ad = ad + 1
        WEND
        LET collectedAttrCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSAttributeTreeCollectCountAddress())
        IF collectedAttrCount <> attributeCount THEN
            ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
        END IF
    END IF

    ' Pass 7 (RFC-0040 Section 11.1): validates the Extent Tree the same way -- a new tolerant
    ' walk (ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant), closing this project's own previously-named
    ' "the Extent Tree has no defect-tolerant scrub coverage" gap. extentTreeRoot=0 (no FILE has
    ' any real, touched chunk) is the legitimate empty case, matching every other tree's own
    ' 0-is-the-empty-tree-sentinel convention.
    LET extentTreeCountForScrub AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(peek, 88))
    IF extentTreeRoot <> 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
        LET extentTreeDefects AS U64 = ArcFSExtentTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(extentTreeRoot, ArcFSExtentTreeSortedAddress(), ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress())
        LET ed AS U64 = 0
        WHILE ed < extentTreeDefects
            ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
            ed = ed + 1
        WEND
        LET collectedExtentCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSExtentTreeCollectCountAddress())
        IF collectedExtentCount <> extentTreeCountForScrub THEN
            ArcFSAddStructuralDefect()
        END IF
    END IF

    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSDefectCountAddress())
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 20.4: online read-only integrity scrub of the currently ACTIVE generation.
' Safely callable at any time -- reads the superblock itself fresh, touches no live table.
' Phase H scope boundary, stated up front like every prior phase's own header note: this reads
' ring slot 0 only, not the full 4-copy ring ArcFSReadSuperblockRing uses for activation. Ring-
' wide consistency scrubbing (detecting and reporting a ring copy that has silently gone stale or
' corrupt even though activation itself still succeeds via a different copy) is RFC-0040 Section
' 14's Expanded Repair Coverage, which is Phase M's job, not Phase H's Format Foundation.
' ==========================================================================================
' RFC-0040 Section 13: the Full Health Model. A small fixed-size Health Record (Section 13.2 --
' deliberately NOT a tree, matching that section's own "RFC-0039 Section 36's own health-detail
' fields are bounded and low-cardinality" reasoning), one PERMANENT sector reserved right next to
' the superblock ring, updated in place (not copy-on-write -- there is exactly one Health Record
' per volume, not one per generation): [0]lastScrubGeneration [8]lastScrubResult (that scrub's own
' total defect count) [16]lastRepairGeneration [24]lastRepairSummary (objects
' reattached/resolved) [32]cumulativeChecksumErrors [40]cumulativeIoErrors [48]checksum (CRC-32C
' over [0,48)). "Cumulative" is scoped to what ArcFSScanGeneration itself observes (structural
' checksum failures and sector-read failures during a scan), not an omniscient instrument of
' every low-level read/write call site in this file -- an honestly-named scope reduction, not a
' silent gap.
' ==========================================================================================

' RFC-0043 Phase U: the fixed initial layout shifts forward once more, the same way it already did
' at RFC-0040 Phase H (checkpoint 4->5, root object 5->6) -- sectors 0-3 (ring), 4..
' (4+ArcFSBitmapSectorCount()-1) (Allocation Bitmap, now multi-sector), then the checkpoint
' (ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector() below), then this Health Record, one sector each.
FUNCTION ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector() AS U64
    RETURN 4 + ArcFSBitmapSectorCount()
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthRecordSector() AS U64
    RETURN ArcFSCheckpointBaseSector() + 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSWriteHealthRecord(scratch AS MMIOPTR, lastScrubGeneration AS U64, lastScrubResult AS U64, lastRepairGeneration AS U64, lastRepairSummary AS U64, cumulativeChecksumErrors AS U64, cumulativeIoErrors AS U64) AS U64
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < 512
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, i), 0)
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(scratch, lastScrubGeneration)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8), lastScrubResult)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 16), lastRepairGeneration)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 24), lastRepairSummary)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32), cumulativeChecksumErrors)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 40), cumulativeIoErrors)
    LET checksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 48) AND 4294967295
    MEMORY.Write32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 48), checksum)
    IF ArcFSBlockWriteSectors(ArcFSHealthRecordSector(), 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Reads and checksum-verifies the Health Record into `scratch`, leaving its fields there for the
' caller to extract -- mirrors ArcFSPeekCheckpoint's own "read-only side query, no live state"
' style. Returns 0 (all fields effectively zero/unavailable) if the record cannot be read or
' fails its checksum, e.g. an FMV2 volume old enough to predate this phase's own reservation of
' sector 6 -- a real, honestly-handled edge every reader below treats as "no history yet," not a
' crash.
FUNCTION ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch AS MMIOPTR) AS BOOL
    IF ArcFSBlockReadSectors(ArcFSHealthRecordSector(), 1, scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET storedChecksum AS U64 = MEMORY.Read32(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 48))
    LET computedChecksum AS U64 = ArcFSCrc32C(scratch, 48)
    IF storedChecksum <> computedChecksum THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetLastScrubGeneration() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(scratch)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetLastScrubResult() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetLastRepairGeneration() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 16))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetLastRepairSummary() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 24))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetCumulativeChecksumErrors() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 32))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFS.GetCumulativeIoErrors() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    RETURN MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 40))
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 20.4: online read-only integrity scrub of the currently ACTIVE generation.
' Safely callable at any time -- reads the superblock itself fresh, touches no live MOUNT state.
' RFC-0040 Section 13.1/14, finishing what Phase H's own header note already named as deferred:
' widened from ring slot 0 only to the real full 4-copy ring selection (ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint,
' side-effect-free), so a scrub run against a volume whose slot 0 has gone stale still correctly
' finds and scans the SAME generation `ArcFS.MountImage` itself would activate, not silently a
' different, wrong one. Also records this scrub's own result into the persisted Health Record
' (Section 13.2) -- non-transactionally, immediately, matching that section's own framing ("a
' scrub's own findings are not committed... scrubbing stays read-only"); a crash between this
' write and whatever the caller does next simply leaves the LAST real scrub's own honest result
' recorded, never a fabricated one.
FUNCTION ArcFS.ScrubVolume() AS U64
    LET checkpointSector AS U64 = ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint()
    IF checkpointSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET defects AS U64 = ArcFSScanGeneration(checkpointSector)

    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    LET generation AS U64 = 0
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) <> 0 THEN
        generation = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 8))
    END IF
    LET priorChecksumErrors AS U64 = ArcFS.GetCumulativeChecksumErrors()
    LET priorIoErrors AS U64 = ArcFS.GetCumulativeIoErrors()
    LET priorRepairGeneration AS U64 = ArcFS.GetLastRepairGeneration()
    LET priorRepairSummary AS U64 = ArcFS.GetLastRepairSummary()
    LET structuralDefects AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSHealthStateAddress())
    LET checkpointUnreadable AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHealthStateAddress(), 8))
    LET newChecksumErrors AS U64 = priorChecksumErrors + structuralDefects
    LET newIoErrors AS U64 = priorIoErrors + checkpointUnreadable
    ArcFSWriteHealthRecord(scratch, generation, defects, priorRepairGeneration, priorRepairSummary, newChecksumErrors, newIoErrors)

    RETURN defects
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthHealthy() AS U64
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthDegraded() AS U64
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthReadOnlySafety() AS U64
    RETURN 2
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthNeedsScrub() AS U64
    RETURN 3
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthNeedsOfflineCheck() AS U64
    RETURN 4
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthCorrupt() AS U64
    RETURN 5
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ArcFSHealthUnavailable() AS U64
    RETURN 6
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 36's HealthReporter, all seven RFC-0040 Section 13.1 states, derived from what
' this reference implementation can genuinely observe (this function ALWAYS performs a fresh
' scrub, exactly like its 3-state predecessor -- see ArcFS.GetHealthStateCached below for the one
' state, NeedsScrub, that requires NOT scrubbing to be reachable at all):
'   Unavailable       -- no ring copy yields a trustworthy checkpoint at all (mirrors
'                        ArcFS.ActivateSystemVolume's own established "0 = Unavailable" meaning).
'   NeedsOfflineCheck -- a ring copy validates but DISAGREES with the winning generation
'                        (ArcFSRingIsConsistent, a real anomaly the online scan of the active
'                        generation cannot explain), OR (currently unreachable through THIS
'                        function's own call path, kept as a genuine defensive fallback -- see
'                        ArcFSScanGeneration's own tracking) the selected checkpoint couldn't be
'                        peeked at scan time, meaning Pass 1-5 never ran at all.
'   Corrupt           -- the scan completed but found STRUCTURAL (tree-node) corruption -- unlike
'                        an orphan or an extent overlap, this implementation has no repair for a
'                        node whose bytes are simply gone.
'   ReadOnlySafety    -- only repairable-class defects (orphans, extent overlaps) were found, AND
'                        ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress is currently armed (RFC-0039 Section 38).
'   Degraded          -- the same repairable-only defects, but the flag is NOT (yet) armed --
'                        Section 13.1's own literal "distinctly from a general Degraded finding
'                        that has not (yet) armed that flag."
'   Healthy           -- zero defects of any kind.
FUNCTION ArcFS.GetHealthState() AS U64
    LET checkpointSector AS U64 = ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint()
    IF checkpointSector = 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthUnavailable()
    END IF
    ' RFC-0040 Section 13.1/14: ring-wide consistency is checked BEFORE the scan itself -- a real,
    ' independent anomaly the scan's own read of the ACTIVE generation cannot detect or explain
    ' (see ArcFSRingIsConsistent's own header note). A stale-but-unreadable copy does not trigger
    ' this (Section 8.2's own already-tolerated self-healing case); only a copy that VALIDATES but
    ' disagrees does.
    IF ArcFSRingIsConsistent() = 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthNeedsOfflineCheck()
    END IF
    LET defects AS U64 = ArcFS.ScrubVolume()
    ' The checkpoint-unreadable-during-scan case is currently unreachable through this function's
    ' OWN call path (ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint already guarantees a selected checkpoint is
    ' peekable -- the same validation runs in both places), but ArcFSScanGeneration's own
    ' defensive tracking of it is kept regardless (a genuine, honest safeguard, not dead code
    ' removed for tidiness -- a future caller of ArcFSScanGeneration with an unvalidated sector
    ' would still need it) and folded into the SAME NeedsOfflineCheck state here for completeness.
    LET checkpointUnreadable AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSHealthStateAddress(), 8))
    IF checkpointUnreadable <> 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthNeedsOfflineCheck()
    END IF
    LET structuralDefects AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSHealthStateAddress())
    IF structuralDefects > 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthCorrupt()
    END IF
    IF defects = 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthHealthy()
    END IF
    IF MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress()) <> 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthReadOnlySafety()
    END IF
    RETURN ArcFSHealthDegraded()
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 13.1's NeedsScrub, the one state ArcFS.GetHealthState (which ALWAYS scrubs
' fresh, preserving its own pre-existing "always accurate, no caching" contract unchanged) can
' never itself return. This is a genuinely different query: the persisted Health Record's own
' `lastScrubGeneration` is durable across mounts (Section 13.2), so a volume this function reports
' NeedsScrub for has NEVER been scrubbed even ONCE in its whole real history, not merely "not yet
' this session" -- a fresh format sets it to 0 for exactly this reason. Every other state comes
' from the SAME persisted record (last known result), not a fresh scan -- cheap, matching a
' HealthReporter's own "status query," not "perform work," framing.
FUNCTION ArcFS.GetHealthStateCached() AS U64
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    IF ArcFSReadHealthRecord(scratch) = 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthNeedsScrub()
    END IF
    LET lastScrubGeneration AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(scratch)
    IF lastScrubGeneration = 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthNeedsScrub()
    END IF
    LET lastScrubResult AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(scratch, 8))
    IF lastScrubResult = 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthHealthy()
    END IF
    IF MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress()) <> 0 THEN
        RETURN ArcFSHealthReadOnlySafety()
    END IF
    RETURN ArcFSHealthDegraded()
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 37/38's activation + ReadOnlySafety, made concrete: 0 = failed to mount at
' all (Section 37's activation could not even establish a trustworthy committed checkpoint), 1 =
' mounted read-write (healthy), 2 = mounted READ-ONLY (Section 38: the committed checkpoint is
' trustworthy and fully readable, but a structural defect makes further mutation unsafe until
' repaired). Sets ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress, which ArcFS.PrepareCommit consults below.
FUNCTION ArcFS.MountImageSafe() AS U64
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFS.MountImage() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    ' RFC-0040 Section 13.2: reuses ArcFS.ScrubVolume (not a direct ArcFSScanGeneration call as
    ' before) so an ordinary safe-mount's own scan ALSO records into the persisted Health Record --
    ' the more commonly exercised path than an explicit ArcFS.ScrubVolume() call, and correctly
    ' re-derives the SAME checkpoint sector ArcFS.MountImage just activated (ArcFSSelectRingCheckpoint
    ' is deterministic; nothing changed between the two calls).
    LET defects AS U64 = ArcFS.ScrubVolume()
    IF defects > 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress(), 1)
        RETURN 2
    END IF
    ' RFC-0042 Section 7.2: ArcFS.MountImage itself may already have armed ReadOnlySafety (an
    ' unrecognized ro-compat feature bit), with no scrub-detected defect involved at all -- checked
    ' AFTER the scrub above so a genuine defect still reports through that path first, matching
    ' this function's own established precedent of naming the more specific reason.
    IF MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress()) <> 0 THEN
        RETURN 2
    END IF
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Internal: attaches an EXISTING object (one that already has an OID and an object-table row) to
' a new namespace location -- unlike ArcFSCreateEntry, this creates no object, and unlike
' ArcFS.Rename, this does not require an existing active namespace row to relocate (an orphan, by
' definition, has none). Used only by ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans below.
FUNCTION ArcFSAttachExistingObject(parentOid AS U64, nameBuffer AS MMIOPTR, nameLength AS U64, childOid AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET freeNsRow AS U64 = ArcFSFindFreeNamespaceRow()
    IF freeNsRow = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET nsBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSNamespaceRowAddress(freeNsRow)
    MEMORY.Write64(nsBase, parentOid)
    ArcFSWriteName(nsBase, nameBuffer, nameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 40), nameLength)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 48), childOid)
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(nsBase, 56), 1)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' Formats `value` as ASCII decimal digits at `dest`, returning the digit count written. A small,
' generic, reusable utility -- needed because ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans below must synthesize a
' name like "orphan-42" and STRING has no such formatting operation on this backend (Phase A's
' own scope reduction #4).
FUNCTION ArcFSWriteDecimal(dest AS MMIOPTR, value AS U64) AS U64
    IF value = 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write8(dest, 48)
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    LET v AS U64 = value
    LET count AS U64 = 0
    WHILE v > 0
        LET digit AS U64 = v MOD 10
        LET digitByte AS U8 = digit + 48
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, count), digitByte)
        v = v \ 10
        count = count + 1
    WEND
    LET lo AS U64 = 0
    LET hi AS U64 = count - 1
    WHILE lo < hi
        LET a AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, lo))
        LET b AS U8 = MEMORY.Read8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, hi))
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, lo), b)
        MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(dest, hi), a)
        lo = lo + 1
        hi = hi - 1
    WEND
    RETURN count
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0039 Section 39's repair, Apply step, for exactly one defect class (see this section's own
' scope reduction #2): every orphaned object in the LIVE mounted tree is re-parented into a
' root-level `:lost+found` directory (created if it does not already exist) under a synthetic
' `orphan-<oid>` name. Operates on the live in-memory tables, not the disk directly -- unlike the
' read-only scan functions above, repair's whole point is to eventually commit a fix, and only the
' live tree is ever committed. Returns the count of objects reattached.
FUNCTION ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans() AS U64
    LET nameBuf AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSRepairNameScratchAddress()
    LET lostFoundName AS MMIOPTR = nameBuf
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 0), 108)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 1), 111)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 2), 115)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 3), 116)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 4), 43)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 5), 102)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 6), 111)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 7), 117)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 8), 110)
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(lostFoundName, 9), 100)

    LET lostFoundOid AS U64 = ArcFS.Lookup(1, lostFoundName, 10)
    IF lostFoundOid = 0 THEN
        lostFoundOid = ArcFS.CreateDirectory(1, lostFoundName, 10)
        IF lostFoundOid = 0 THEN
            RETURN 0
        END IF
    END IF

    LET synthName AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 16)
    LET repaired AS U64 = 0
    LET row AS U64 = 0
    WHILE row < ArcFSMaxObjects()
        LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
        LET objType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 8))
        IF objType <> 0 THEN
            LET oid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(base)
            IF oid <> 1 THEN
                IF ArcFSFindNamespaceRowByChild(oid) = ArcFSMaxNamespaceRows() THEN
                    MEMORY.Write8(synthName, 111)
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 1), 114)
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 2), 112)
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 3), 104)
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 4), 97)
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 5), 110)
                    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 6), 45)
                    LET digitCount AS U64 = ArcFSWriteDecimal(ADDRESS.Offset(synthName, 7), oid)
                    LET totalLen AS U64 = 7 + digitCount
                    IF ArcFSAttachExistingObject(lostFoundOid, synthName, totalLen, oid) = 1 THEN
                        repaired = repaired + 1
                    END IF
                END IF
            END IF
        END IF
        row = row + 1
    WEND
    ' RFC-0040 Section 13.2: accumulates into the pending repair summary ArcFS.RepairCommit will
    ' persist -- see ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress's own header note.
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress(), MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress()) + repaired)
    RETURN repaired
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 14.1: extent-overlap repair. Walks the Object Tree rooted at `checkpointSector`
' (a fresh, dedicated tolerant walk -- NOT reusing ArcFSScanGeneration's own Pass 1/5, which only
' COUNT defects; this needs the actual offending PAIRS) and records every overlapping FILE pair
' into `destBase` as (lowOid, highOid), 16 bytes each. Returns the pair count.
FUNCTION ArcFSFindExtentOverlaps(checkpointSector AS U64, destBase AS MMIOPTR, destCountAddress AS MMIOPTR) AS U64
    MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, 0)
    IF ArcFSPeekCheckpoint(checkpointSector) = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET objectRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPeekStateAddress())
    ' RFC-0040 Section 11.1: this generation's own Extent Tree root.
    LET extentTreeRoot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSPeekStateAddress(), 80))
    LET objArray AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSScanObjectArrayAddress()
    LET sortedTemp AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSTreeSortedAddress()
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress(), 0)
    ArcFSTreeCollectAllEntriesTolerant(objectRoot, sortedTemp, ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress())
    LET collectedCount AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSTreeCollectCountAddress())

    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < collectedCount
        LET src AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(sortedTemp, i * 40)
        LET entry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, i * 24)
        MEMORY.Write64(entry, MEMORY.Read64(src))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 8), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 8)))
        MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(entry, 16), MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(src, 24)))
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    LET pairCount AS U64 = 0
    LET p AS U64 = 0
    WHILE p < collectedCount
        LET pEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, p * 24)
        LET pType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(pEntry, 8))
        IF pType = 1 THEN
            LET pOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(pEntry)
            LET pDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(pEntry, 16))
            LET q AS U64 = p + 1
            WHILE q < collectedCount
                LET qEntry AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(objArray, q * 24)
                LET qType AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(qEntry, 8))
                IF qType = 1 THEN
                    LET qOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(qEntry)
                    LET qDataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(qEntry, 16))
                    IF ArcFSDataSlotsOverlap(extentTreeRoot, pDataSlot, qDataSlot) = 1 THEN
                        IF pairCount < 32 THEN
                            ' RFC-0040 Section 14.1's own literal policy ("lower generation number
                            ' is authoritative") is NOT directly implementable here -- no per-entry
                            ' generation number exists anywhere in this format's leaf entries, only
                            ' the volume's own single global generation counter. Deviation, stated
                            ' plainly: this implementation instead uses the LOWER OID as
                            ' authoritative -- an arbitrary but well-defined, deterministic total
                            ' order, the same "not yet semantically meaningful, just deterministic"
                            ' precedent the Namespace/Attribute Trees' own sort keys already use.
                            LET lowOid AS U64 = pOid
                            LET highOid AS U64 = qOid
                            IF qOid < pOid THEN
                                lowOid = qOid
                                highOid = pOid
                            END IF
                            LET dst AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(destBase, pairCount * 16)
                            MEMORY.Write64(dst, lowOid)
                            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(dst, 8), highOid)
                            pairCount = pairCount + 1
                        END IF
                    END IF
                END IF
                q = q + 1
            WEND
        END IF
        p = p + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(destCountAddress, pairCount)
    RETURN pairCount
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 14.1's Apply step: for every overlapping pair ArcFSFindExtentOverlaps found
' against the CURRENTLY ACTIVE checkpoint, clears the offending (higher-OID) object's LIVE data --
' `size` reset to 0 and every chunk marked untouched, so the NEXT commit's own
' ArcFSBuildAndWriteExtentList writes a fresh, all-hole extent list for it (an EMPTY file, not
' deleted, matching RFC-0039 Section 39's own "recovery SHOULD preserve it... with provenance
' metadata" policy applied to metadata even when the data itself cannot be trusted -- the OID
' itself remains the provenance record, unchanged, the same reasoning
' ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans' own synthetic naming already relies on). Operates on the live
' in-memory tables, like every other repair function -- only the live tree is ever committed.
' Returns the count of objects cleared.
FUNCTION ArcFS.RepairResolveExtentOverlaps() AS U64
    LET activeCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSMountStateAddress())
    LET pairsBase AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSExtentOverlapPairsAddress()
    LET pairCount AS U64 = ArcFSFindExtentOverlaps(activeCheckpointSector, pairsBase, ArcFSExtentOverlapPairCountAddress())

    LET cleared AS U64 = 0
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < pairCount
        LET pair AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(pairsBase, i * 16)
        LET highOid AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(pair, 8))
        LET row AS U64 = ArcFSFindObjectRow(highOid)
        IF row <> ArcFSMaxObjects() THEN
            LET base AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSObjectRowAddress(row)
            MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 16), 0)
            LET dataSlot AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(base, 24))
            LET c AS U64 = 0
            WHILE c < ArcFSMaxFileChunks()
                ArcFSChunkTouchedSet(dataSlot, c, 0)
                c = c + 1
            WEND
            cleared = cleared + 1
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress(), MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress()) + cleared)
    RETURN cleared
END FUNCTION

' RFC-0040 Section 14.2: allocation-tree (Allocation Bitmap) reconciliation. Recomputes, for every
' currently-tracked sector, whether it is REACHABLE (ArcFS.IsSectorReachable -- already the
' authoritative "from the active checkpoint OR any live snapshot" answer RFC-0039 Section 29
' calls for, so this naturally covers "every reachable generation" including snapshots with no
' separate walk needed) and reconciles the bitmap toward that recomputed truth CONSERVATIVELY --
' only ever marking a sector ALLOCATED when the bitmap wrongly had it free (the dangerous
' direction: a live sector a future allocation could otherwise overwrite), matching the RFC's own
' literal "more things marked allocated, never fewer." The opposite direction (bitmap says
' allocated but nothing reaches it) is Reclaim's own job (ArcFS.ReclaimGeneration), not this
' pass's -- reconciliation and reclamation are deliberately kept as two separate, single-purpose
' operations. Mutates only the in-memory bitmap, like every other repair/reclaim function in this
' file -- the caller commits separately, the same "no separate non-transactional repair code
' path" discipline Phase I's own ReclaimGeneration already established. Returns the count of
' sectors corrected.
FUNCTION ArcFS.RepairReconcileAllocationBitmap() AS U64
    LET highWaterMark AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 64))
    LET corrected AS U64 = 0
    LET sector AS U64 = 4
    WHILE sector < highWaterMark
        IF ArcFS.IsSectorReachable(sector) = 1 THEN
            IF ArcFSBitmapGet(sector) = 0 THEN
                ArcFSBitmapSet(sector, 1)
                corrected = corrected + 1
            END IF
        END IF
        sector = sector + 1
    WEND
    RETURN corrected
END FUNCTION

' The one sanctioned exception to the ReadOnlySafety guard below: repair's entire purpose is
' fixing the defect that caused ReadOnlySafety in the first place, so it must be able to commit
' while that flag is set. Temporarily clears the flag, attempts an ordinary commit, then re-scans
' the newly active generation and re-arms the flag if a defect still remains -- RFC-0039 Section
' 39's own Apply -> Verify staging, concretely: Verify is not assumed, it is re-run for real.
' RFC-0040 Section 13.2: upon a successful, RE-VERIFIED commit, persists the accumulated pending
' repair summary (every ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans/RepairResolveExtentOverlaps call since the
' last successful RepairCommit) into the Health Record, then consumes it (resets to 0) -- a crash
' before this point leaves the PREVIOUS repair's own honest record in place, never a fabricated
' one, the same "only record durable success" discipline ArcFS.ScrubVolume's own write already
' follows.
FUNCTION ArcFS.RepairCommit() AS BOOL
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress(), 0)
    IF ArcFS.CommitImage() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET activeCheckpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSMountStateAddress())
    LET defects AS U64 = ArcFSScanGeneration(activeCheckpointSector)
    IF defects > 0 THEN
        MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSReadOnlySafetyAddress(), 1)
        RETURN 0
    END IF

    LET generation AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 56))
    LET pendingSummary AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress())
    LET scratch AS MMIOPTR = ArcFSSectorScratchAddress()
    LET lastScrubGeneration AS U64 = ArcFS.GetLastScrubGeneration()
    LET lastScrubResult AS U64 = ArcFS.GetLastScrubResult()
    LET cumulativeChecksumErrors AS U64 = ArcFS.GetCumulativeChecksumErrors()
    LET cumulativeIoErrors AS U64 = ArcFS.GetCumulativeIoErrors()
    ArcFSWriteHealthRecord(scratch, lastScrubGeneration, lastScrubResult, generation, pendingSummary, cumulativeChecksumErrors, cumulativeIoErrors)
    MEMORY.Write64(ArcFSPendingRepairSummaryAddress(), 0)
    RETURN 1
END FUNCTION

' ==========================================================================================
' Phase G: system integration (RFC-0039 Section 78, Phase G -- "namespace attachment; system
' volume use; recovery environment support; update snapshots; graphical storage tooling;
' ArcoBASIC bindings").
' ==========================================================================================
'
' Phase G's own list is mostly integration surface with OTHER Arcology subsystems, not additional
' ArcFS format/protocol work -- and most of those subsystems do not exist yet anywhere in this
' repository. Rather than inventing fake infrastructure to claim a hollow "done," this section
' honestly separates what ArcFS can genuinely deliver on its own from what it cannot, item by
' item, matching RFC-0039 Section 80's own "Required Pre-Implementation Dependencies" list (this
' RFC already names these as open dependencies, not something this phase silently discovered):
'
' - "Update snapshots" (Section 51's own illustrative workflow: CREATE snapshot "pre-update" ->
'   WRITE new content -> VERIFY -> COMMIT -> MARK boot generation) is squarely within ArcFS's own
'   reach, given Phase E's snapshot infrastructure and Phase C's commit protocol -- ArcFS.
'   RollbackToSnapshot below is the concrete, missing half of that workflow (undoing a bad update),
'   and IS implemented and proven in this phase.
' - "ArcoBASIC bindings" are, in this specific implementation, already satisfied BY CONSTRUCTION:
'   every ArcFS.* function in this file already IS a native ArcoBASIC-callable entry point. This
'   compiler has no separate host/guest language boundary or FFI layer -- there is no additional
'   "binding" artifact to build. RFC-0039 Section 43's own illustrative syntax (`FILE.OPEN(...)`,
'   `.READTEXT()`, object-inspection properties) describes a friendlier OO-flavored surface this
'   implementation does not wrap ArcFS.* in, but that is a naming/ergonomics choice, not a missing
'   capability -- and RFC-0039 itself never mandates that exact surface, only that ArcoBASIC be
'   "pleasant to use... without exposing raw on-disk machinery for ordinary tasks" (Section 43's
'   own framing), which every ArcFS.* function already is.
' - "Namespace attachment" (RFC-0039 Section 10's DISCOVER/CREATE/ATTACH/VERIFY/ACTIVATE lifecycle
'   against an Arcology namespace LOCATION) has no concrete target to attach TO: this repository
'   has no implemented Arcology object/namespace facility outside ArcFS's own (confirmed the same
'   way RFC-0038's own implementation confirmed RFC-0017's Substrate Resource Model has no
'   concrete freestanding callable surface anywhere in this repository -- Phase A's own scope
'   reduction #6 cites the identical finding). Building one would be separate, real scope creep:
'   RFC-0017's/an Arcology namespace RFC's own implementation, not ArcFS's.
' - "System volume use" presumes a boot path that selects and activates a system ArcFS volume
'   before the rest of the OS starts -- no such boot-time volume-selection mechanism exists
'   anywhere in this project yet (every ArcFS fixture in this whole chain formats or mounts its
'   own volume explicitly, from a fresh boot, for exactly that one fixture).
' - "Recovery environment support" presumes a distinct recovery boot mode/environment -- this
'   project has no second boot path or recovery-specific entry point anywhere; every fixture boots
'   the same ordinary UEFI entry Main() every other freestanding fixture in this project does.
' - "Graphical storage tooling" presumes a GUI/inspector shell -- this project's `stdlib/
'   graphics_primitives.abas`/`graphics_substrate.abas` exist for pixel-level drawing, but nothing
'   resembling a storage-inspector UI (RFC-0039 Section 44's own illustrative volume-inspector
'   text layout) exists to extend, and building one is a UI-application project, not ArcFS's own.
'
' None of these five items can be honestly claimed "implemented" without either fabricating a
' target for ArcFS to integrate with or silently redefining what the RFC asked for -- both are
' exactly what RFC-0039 Section 79.2's own "No silent architectural substitution" rule forbids.
' They are left explicitly OPEN, not implemented, not attempted.

' RFC-0039 Section 51's "update snapshots" rollback: republishes a PREVIOUSLY SNAPSHOTTED
' generation as the active checkpoint -- a real, atomic undo. Reuses ArcFS.PublishCommit's exact
' mechanism (stage a checkpoint sector as "pending," then perform the one single-sector superblock
' write that makes it active) rather than duplicating that logic -- the only difference from an
' ordinary commit's publish step is WHICH checkpoint sector gets staged: a snapshot's old one,
' instead of a freshly prepared new one. Does not itself refresh the live in-memory tables -- a
' caller that wants those to reflect the rolled-back generation calls ArcFS.MountImage()
' afterward, exactly as after any other change to the active checkpoint.
FUNCTION ArcFS.RollbackToSnapshot(snapshotId AS U64) AS BOOL
    LET slot AS U64 = ArcFSFindSnapshotRowById(snapshotId)
    IF slot = ArcFSMaxSnapshots() THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    LET checkpointSector AS U64 = MEMORY.Read64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSSnapshotRowAddress(slot), 24))
    MEMORY.Write64(ADDRESS.Offset(ArcFSMountStateAddress(), 72), checkpointSector)
    RETURN ArcFS.PublishCommit()
END FUNCTION

' --- Phase G addendum: "system volume use," narrowed honestly. RFC-0039 Section 10's own
' attachment lifecycle is DISCOVER -> CREATE -> ATTACH -> VERIFY -> ACTIVATE -> ATTACH namespace.
' The LAST step (attach to an Arcology namespace location) stays explicitly out of scope -- no
' such namespace exists in this repository, unchanged from this section's own header above. The
' FIRST step (DISCOVER: enumerate real UEFI Block IO Protocol handles instead of relying on the
' fixed-address RAM-disk preload every fixture in this whole chain uses) also stays out of scope
' here -- it is RFC-0038's own named future work (that RFC's Section 17.5 stop condition
' explicitly warns against improvising new UEFI protocol bindings as a side effect of unrelated
' work, and this addendum does not violate that).
'
' What IS genuinely new: RFC-0039 Section 37's activation checklist and Section 38's
' ReadOnlySafety, turned into a real BOOT POLICY rather than a set of tools a human runs by hand.
' Phase F built ScrubVolume/GetHealthState/MountImageSafe/RepairReattachOrphans/RepairCommit as
' separate primitives a caller composes manually (exactly what every Phase F fixture does).
' ArcFS.ActivateSystemVolume composes them automatically, the way a real boot sequence would:
' mount safely, and if a recoverable structural defect is found, repair it and re-verify BEFORE
' ever surfacing ReadOnlySafety to whatever comes next in the boot sequence -- self-healing at
' boot, with read-only as the honest fallback only when automatic repair genuinely cannot clear
' the defect, never a silent guess (RFC-0039 Section 5.3).

' Returns 0 = Unavailable (no trustworthy committed checkpoint could be established at all), 1 =
' Healthy and read-write (no defect found, or one was found and automatically repaired this
' boot), 2 = ReadOnlySafety (a defect was found and automatic repair could not fully clear it).
FUNCTION ArcFS.ActivateSystemVolume() AS U64
    LET result AS U64 = ArcFS.MountImageSafe()
    IF result = 0 THEN
        RETURN 0
    END IF
    IF result = 1 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF

    ' result = 2 (ReadOnlySafety): attempt the one repair this project implements, automatically,
    ' then re-activate to CONFIRM it actually cleared the defect -- never trust a repair blindly,
    ' the same discipline ArcFS.RepairCommit itself already applies to its own commit.
    ArcFS.RepairReattachOrphans()
    IF ArcFS.RepairCommit() = 0 THEN
        RETURN 2
    END IF

    LET reactivated AS U64 = ArcFS.MountImageSafe()
    IF reactivated = 1 THEN
        RETURN 1
    END IF
    RETURN 2
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SerialByte(value AS U8) AS U64
    LET port AS IOPORT = PORT.Address(1016)
    LET statusPort AS IOPORT = PORT.Offset(port, 5)
    LET attempts AS U32 = 0
    WHILE (PORT.ReadByte(statusPort) AND 32) = 0
        CPU.Pause
        attempts = attempts + 1
        IF attempts >= 10000 THEN
            RETURN 1
        END IF
    WEND
    PORT.WriteByte(port, value)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SerialInit() AS U64
    LET base AS IOPORT = PORT.Address(1016)
    PORT.WriteByte(PORT.Offset(base, 1), 0)
    PORT.WriteByte(PORT.Offset(base, 3), 128)
    PORT.WriteByte(base, 1)
    PORT.WriteByte(PORT.Offset(base, 1), 0)
    PORT.WriteByte(PORT.Offset(base, 3), 3)
    PORT.WriteByte(PORT.Offset(base, 2), 199)
    PORT.WriteByte(PORT.Offset(base, 4), 11)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' Prints "ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL " followed by up to 4 conditional trailing bytes then CRLF --
' distinct 4-letter completion/failure codes over serial, matching render-and-halt.abas's own
' established dual-report pattern (a real GOP device flips OVMF's own ConOut default away from
' serial, so ConsoleOut alone is not enough for automated QEMU capture).
FUNCTION SerialMarker(b0 AS U64, b1 AS U64, b2 AS U64, b3 AS U64) AS U64
    SerialByte(65)   ' A
    SerialByte(82)   ' R
    SerialByte(67)   ' C
    SerialByte(79)   ' O
    SerialByte(76)   ' L
    SerialByte(79)   ' O
    SerialByte(71)   ' G
    SerialByte(89)   ' Y
    SerialByte(32)   ' space
    SerialByte(71)   ' G
    SerialByte(80)   ' P
    SerialByte(84)   ' T
    SerialByte(32)   ' space
    SerialByte(83)   ' S
    SerialByte(89)   ' Y
    SerialByte(83)   ' S
    SerialByte(86)   ' V
    SerialByte(79)   ' O
    SerialByte(76)   ' L
    SerialByte(32)   ' space
    IF b0 <> 0 THEN
        SerialByte(b0)
    END IF
    IF b1 <> 0 THEN
        SerialByte(b1)
    END IF
    IF b2 <> 0 THEN
        SerialByte(b2)
    END IF
    IF b3 <> 0 THEN
        SerialByte(b3)
    END IF
    SerialByte(13)
    SerialByte(10)
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION GptSysvolBufferAddress() AS MMIOPTR
    RETURN ADDRESS.MMIO(ADDRESS.Virtual(39845888))   ' 0x2600000, this fixture's own scratch buffer
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION Main(imageHandle AS UEFI.Handle, systemTable AS UEFI.SystemTable) AS U64
    systemTable.BootServices.SetWatchdogTimer(0, 0, 0, 0)
    systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL START")
    SerialInit()
    SerialMarker(83, 84, 65, 82)   ' STAR

    ' RFC-0044 Phase 3: real multi-handle enumeration against the REAL single GPT-partitioned disk
    ' this fixture itself booted from (Phase 2's own image) -- not two separate -drive entries.
    ' Empirically confirmed during Phase 3 development (see aps-rfc-0044-phase-3.md): a single GPT
    ' disk under this QEMU/OVMF combination yields FOUR real BlockIo handles, not two -- the whole
    ' disk itself, one additional handle this fixture does not need to explain, then the ESP and
    ' the ArcFS partition, in that real discovery order. RFC-0044 Section 6's own literal
    ' "SelectDiscovered(1)" (a hardcoded index) does NOT correspond to the ArcFS partition in this
    ' real topology -- a real, honest, RFC-0044 Section 7.2-mandated finding, not silently patched
    ' over. This fixture instead identifies the ArcFS partition by its own known, real geometry
    ' (SectorCount = ARCFS_SECTORS from build-arcology-gpt-image.py), the SAME robust-to-discovery-
    ' order technique Phase 1's own multi-blockio-discovery.abas fixture already proved.
    LET count AS U64 = UefiBlockDevice.DiscoverAll(systemTable)
    IF count < 2 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL TOO FEW HANDLES")
        SerialMarker(84, 70, 72, 78)   ' TFHN
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    LET arcfsIndex AS U64 = 999
    LET i AS U64 = 0
    WHILE i < count
        LET sectors AS U64 = UefiBlockDevice.DiscoveredSectorCount(i)
        IF sectors = 16384 THEN
            IF arcfsIndex = 999 THEN
                arcfsIndex = i
            END IF
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    IF arcfsIndex = 999 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL ARCFS PARTITION NOT FOUND")
        SerialMarker(78, 79, 80, 84)   ' NOPT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    LET selected AS BOOL = UefiBlockDevice.SelectDiscovered(arcfsIndex)
    IF selected = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL SELECT FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 83, 69, 76)   ' NSEL
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    ArcFS.SelectBlockDevice(ArcFS.BlockDeviceProviderUefi())
    IF ArcFS.ActiveBlockDevice() <> 1 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL PROVIDER SELECT FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 80, 82, 86)   ' NPRV
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    ' The ArcFS partition starts out reserved and unformatted (Phase 2's own design) -- format a
    ' fresh volume, write one real file, and commit, exactly matching RFC-0043 Phase V's own
    ' writer-half proof, now on the SAME disk the system booted from instead of a separate device.
    IF ArcFS.FormatVolume() = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL FORMAT FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 70, 77, 84)   ' NFMT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    IF ArcFS.MountImage() = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL MOUNT FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 77, 78, 84)   ' NMNT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    LET nameBuf AS MMIOPTR = GptSysvolBufferAddress()
    MEMORY.Write8(nameBuf, 100)          ' d
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 1), 111)   ' o
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 2), 99)    ' c
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 3), 46)    ' .
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 4), 116)   ' t
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 5), 120)   ' x
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(nameBuf, 6), 116)   ' t
    LET oid AS U64 = ArcFS.CreateFile(1, nameBuf, 7)
    IF oid = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL CREATE FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 67, 82, 84)   ' NCRT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    LET content AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(GptSysvolBufferAddress(), 64)
    MEMORY.Write8(content, 83)             ' S
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(content, 1), 121)   ' y
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(content, 2), 115)   ' s
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(content, 3), 118)   ' v
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(content, 4), 111)   ' o
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(content, 5), 108)   ' l
    MEMORY.Write8(ADDRESS.Offset(content, 6), 33)    ' !
    LET h AS U64 = ArcFS.OpenHandle(oid)
    IF h = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL OPEN FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 79, 80, 78)   ' NOPN
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    LET written AS U64 = ArcFS.HandleWrite(h, content, 7, 0)
    IF written <> 7 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL WRITE FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 87, 82, 84)   ' NWRT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    ArcFS.CloseHandle(h)

    IF ArcFS.CommitImage() = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL COMMIT FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 67, 77, 84)   ' NCMT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL COMMIT OK")
    SerialMarker(67, 77, 79, 75)   ' CMOK

    ' The real boot-policy proof (RFC-0039 Phase G): re-activate the volume just committed via the
    ' SAME real path an actual boot sequence would use, not a plain MountImage -- proving
    ' ArcFS.ActivateSystemVolume works on the ArcFS partition of a real single, GPT-partitioned
    ' disk (RFC-0043 Phase V only ever proved it on a SEPARATE physical disk from the boot medium).
    LET activation AS U64 = ArcFS.ActivateSystemVolume()
    IF activation <> 1 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL ACTIVATE FAILED")
        SerialMarker(78, 65, 67, 84)   ' NACT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL ACTIVATED")
    SerialMarker(65, 67, 84, 79)   ' ACTO

    ' Real GOP render + pixel-readback self-verification, the SAME logic render-and-halt.abas
    ' already proved on real hardware (RFC-0006/WP-030) -- a visible confirmation the whole real
    ' single-disk chain (firmware ESP boot -> real partition-2 discovery/selection -> real ArcFS
    ' format/write/commit/activate) completed, not just that ArcFS's own internal state looks right.
    LET gop AS UEFI.GraphicsOutputProtocol = UEFI.GOP.Discover(systemTable)
    IF gop = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL GOP UNAVAILABLE")
        SerialMarker(78, 79, 71, 79)   ' NOGO
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    LET mode AS UEFI.GraphicsOutputMode = UEFI.GOP.Mode(gop)
    IF mode = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL MODE UNAVAILABLE")
        SerialMarker(78, 79, 77, 68)   ' NOMD
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    LET framebuffer AS PHYSICALPTR = UEFI.GOP.FrameBufferBase(gop)
    LET framebufferSize AS U64 = UEFI.GOP.FrameBufferSize(gop)
    LET width AS U32 = UEFI.GOP.Width(gop)
    LET height AS U32 = UEFI.GOP.Height(gop)
    LET stride AS U32 = UEFI.GOP.PixelsPerScanLine(gop)
    IF width = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL ZERO WIDTH")
        SerialMarker(90, 87, 68, 84)   ' ZWDT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    IF height = 0 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL ZERO HEIGHT")
        SerialMarker(90, 72, 71, 84)   ' ZHGT
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    LET mapped AS MMIOPTR = MEMORY.MapDevice(framebuffer, framebufferSize)

    LET accentLeft AS U32 = width \ 4
    LET accentRight AS U32 = accentLeft + (width \ 2)
    LET accentTop AS U32 = height \ 4
    LET accentBottom AS U32 = accentTop + (height \ 2)

    LET y AS U32 = 0
    WHILE y < height
        LET inAccentRow AS BOOL = 0
        IF y >= accentTop THEN
            IF y < accentBottom THEN
                inAccentRow = 1
            END IF
        END IF
        LET x AS U32 = 0
        WHILE x < width
            LET pixelOffset AS U32 = (y * stride + x) * 4
            LET pixel AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(mapped, pixelOffset)
            LET inAccentCol AS BOOL = 0
            IF x >= accentLeft THEN
                IF x < accentRight THEN
                    inAccentCol = 1
                END IF
            END IF
            IF inAccentRow = 1 THEN
                IF inAccentCol = 1 THEN
                    MEMORY.Write32(pixel, 65535)
                ELSE
                    MEMORY.Write32(pixel, 2105376)
                END IF
            ELSE
                MEMORY.Write32(pixel, 2105376)
            END IF
            x = x + 1
        WEND
        y = y + 1
    WEND

    CPU.MemoryBarrier

    LET bgProbeOffset AS U32 = (10 * stride + 10) * 4
    LET bgProbe AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(mapped, bgProbeOffset)
    LET bgValue AS U32 = MEMORY.Read32(bgProbe)
    LET accentCenterX AS U32 = accentLeft + (width \ 4)
    LET accentCenterY AS U32 = accentTop + (height \ 4)
    LET accentProbeOffset AS U32 = (accentCenterY * stride + accentCenterX) * 4
    LET accentProbe AS MMIOPTR = ADDRESS.Offset(mapped, accentProbeOffset)
    LET accentValue AS U32 = MEMORY.Read32(accentProbe)

    IF bgValue <> 2105376 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL VERIFY FAILED")
        SerialMarker(86, 70, 66, 71)   ' VFBG
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF
    IF accentValue <> 65535 THEN
        systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL VERIFY FAILED")
        SerialMarker(86, 70, 65, 67)   ' VFAC
        CPU.HaltForever
    END IF

    systemTable.ConsoleOut.Write("ARCOLOGY GPT SYSVOL DONE")
    SerialMarker(68, 79, 78, 69)   ' DONE
    CPU.HaltForever
END FUNCTION
